      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    cuboids = []\n    idx = 1\n    for i in range(N):\n        x1 = int(input_data[idx])\n        y1 = int(input_data[idx+1])\n        z1 = int(input_data[idx+2])\n        x2 = int(input_data[idx+3])\n        y2 = int(input_data[idx+4])\n        z2 = int(input_data[idx+5])\n        # Ensure coordinates are ordered\n        cuboids.append((min(x1, x2), min(y1, y2), min(z1, z2), \n                        max(x1, x2), max(y1, y2), max(z1, z2)))\n        idx += 6\n\n    # Since the coordinates are very small (0-100), we can use a 3D grid.\n    # The grid will store which cuboid occupies the unit cube [x, x+1) * [y, y+1) * [z, z+1).\n    # Grid size is 100x100x100.\n    grid = [[[-1 for _ in range(100)] for _ in range(100)] for _ in range(100)]\n    \n    for i in range(N):\n        x1, y1, z1, x2, y2, z2 = cuboids[i]\n        for x in range(x1, x2):\n            for y in range(y1, y2):\n                for z in range(z1, z2):\n                    grid[x][y][z] = i\n                    \n    adj = [set() for _ in range(N)]\n    \n    # Check adjacency in X direction\n    for x in range(99):\n        for y in range(100):\n            for z in range(100):\n                id1 = grid[x][y][z]\n                id2 = grid[x+1][y][z]\n                if id1 != -1 and id2 != -1 and id1 != id2:\n                    adj[id1].add(id2)\n                    adj[id2].add(id1)\n                    \n    # Check adjacency in Y direction\n    for x in range(100):\n        for y in range(99):\n            for z in range(100):\n                id1 = grid[x][y][z]\n                id2 = grid[x][y+1][z]\n                if id1 != -1 and id2 != -1 and id1 != id2:\n                    adj[id1].add(id2)\n                    adj[id2].add(id1)\n                    \n    # Check adjacency in Z direction\n    for x in range(100):\n        for y in range(100):\n            for z in range(99):\n                id1 = grid[x][y][z]\n                id2 = grid[x][y][z+1]\n                if id1 != -1 and id2 != -1 and id1 != id2:\n                    adj[id1].add(id2)\n                    adj[id2].add(id1)\n                    \n    # Print the number of neighbors for each cuboid\n    results = [str(len(adj[i])) for i in range(N)]\n    sys.stdout.write('\\n'.join(results) + '\\n')\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n\n```",
