    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_436",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a grid of H rows and W columns. Some cells (possibly zero) contain magnets.\nThe state of the grid is represented by H strings S_1, S_2, \\ldots, S_H of length W. If the j-th character of S_i is #, it indicates that there is a magnet in the cell at the i-th row from the top and j-th column from the left; if it is ., it indicates that the cell is empty.\nTakahashi, wearing an iron armor, can move in the grid as follows:\n\n- If any of the cells vertically or horizontally adjacent to the current cell contains a magnet, he cannot move at all.\n- Otherwise, he can move to any one of the vertically or horizontally adjacent cells.\nHowever, he cannot exit the grid.\n\nFor each cell without a magnet, define its degree of freedom as the number of cells he can reach by repeatedly moving from that cell. Find the maximum degree of freedom among all cells without magnets in the grid.\nHere, in the definition of degree of freedom, \"cells he can reach by repeatedly moving\" mean cells that can be reached from the initial cell by some sequence of moves (possibly zero moves). It is not necessary that there is a sequence of moves that visits all such reachable cells starting from the initial cell. Specifically, each cell itself (without a magnet) is always included in the cells reachable from that cell.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nH W\nS_1\nS_2\n\\vdots\nS_H\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the maximum degree of freedom among all cells without magnets.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq H, W \\leq 1000\n- H and W are integers.\n- S_i is a string of length W consisting of . and #.\n- There is at least one cell without a magnet.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3 5\n.#...\n.....\n.#..#\n\nSample Output 1\n\n9\n\nLet (i,j) denote the cell at the i-th row from the top and j-th column from the left. If Takahashi starts at (2,3), possible movements include:\n\n- (2,3) \\to (2,4) \\to (1,4) \\to (1,5) \\to (2,5)\n- (2,3) \\to (2,4) \\to (3,4)\n- (2,3) \\to (2,2)\n- (2,3) \\to (1,3)\n- (2,3) \\to (3,3)\n\nThus, including the cells he passes through, he can reach at least nine cells from (2,3).\nActually, no other cells can be reached, so the degree of freedom for (2,3) is 9.\nThis is the maximum degree of freedom among all cells without magnets, so print 9.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3 3\n..#\n#..\n..#\n\nSample Output 2\n\n1\n\nFor any cell without a magnet, there is a magnet in at least one of the adjacent cells.\nThus, he cannot move from any of these cells, so their degrees of freedom are 1.\nTherefore, print 1.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\n# Increase recursion depth for deep DFS in large grids\nsys.setrecursionlimit(2000000)\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read H and W\n    try:\n        line1 = sys.stdin.readline().split()\n        if not line1:\n            return\n        H, W = map(int, line1)\n    except ValueError:\n        return\n\n    grid = [sys.stdin.readline().strip() for _ in range(H)]\n\n    # Precompute if a cell is \"stuck\" (adjacent to a magnet)\n    # is_stuck[i][j] is True if cell (i, j) has a magnet nearby\n    is_stuck = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    dr = [0, 0, 1, -1]\n    dc = [1, -1, 0, 0]\n\n    for r in range(H):\n        for c in range(W):\n            if grid[r][c] == '#':\n                continue\n            for i in range(4):\n                nr, nc = r + dr[i], c + dc[i]\n                if 0 <= nr < H and 0 <= nc < W and grid[nr][nc] == '#':\n                    is_stuck[r][c] = True\n                    break\n\n    visited = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    max_freedom = 0\n\n    for r in range(H):\n        for c in range(W):\n            if grid[r][c] == '.' and not visited[r][c]:\n                # Start a BFS/DFS to find all reachable cells\n                # Only cells that are NOT stuck can move to others\n                # Cells that ARE stuck can be reached but cannot move further\n                component = []\n                stack = [(r, c)]\n                visited[r][c] = True\n                \n                while stack:\n                    curr_r, curr_c = stack.pop()\n                    component.append((curr_r, curr_c))\n                    \n                    # If the current cell is stuck, it cannot move to neighbors\n                    if is_stuck[curr_r][curr_c]:\n                        continue\n                    \n                    # Otherwise, explore neighbors\n                    for i in range(4):\n                        nr, nc = curr_r + dr[i], curr_c + dc[i]\n                        if 0 <= nr < H and 0 <= nc < W and grid[nr][nc] == '.' and not visited[nr][nc]:\n                            visited[nr][nc] = True\n                            stack.append((nr, nc))\n                \n                # In this specific problem, the movement is:\n                # If you are NOT stuck, you can move to any adjacent '.'\n                # If you are stuck, you can't move.\n                # This means the reachability is a directed graph.\n                # However, since \"can move\" is symmetric (if A is not stuck, it can move to B),\n                # the only \"one-way\" streets are into stuck cells.\n                # The component found by the stack logic above finds all cells reachable \n                # from the starting seed if the seed is not stuck.\n                # But wait: if the seed itself is stuck, it can only reach itself.\n                # The current logic treats all non-stuck cells in a connected block as one.\n                # Let's refine: we need the max degree of freedom.\n                # For any cell in a \"non-stuck\" connected component, they can all reach \n                # each other and any \"stuck\" cells adjacent to that component.\n                \n                # The logic above actually finds the set of cells reachable from (r,c)\n                # IF (r,c) is the start. But we need to check every starting cell.\n                # Actually, the logic above works for the non-stuck component.\n                # Let's use the size of the component found.\n                max_freedom = max(max_freedom, len(component))\n\n    # Special case: the logic above handles components. \n    # If all cells are stuck, max_freedom should be 1.\n    # The loop `for r in range(H): for c in range(W): if grid[r][c] == '.'` \n    # will ensure that if there is at least one '.', max_freedom is at least 1.\n    print(max_freedom)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
