      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Increase recursion depth for deep trees\n    sys.setrecursionlimit(300000)\n    \n    # Read N\n    line = sys.stdin.readline()\n    if not line:\n        return\n    n = int(line.strip())\n    \n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    for _ in range(n - 1):\n        u, v = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        adj[v].append(u)\n    \n    # The problem describes a tree formed by connecting leaves (degree 1) \n    # of level-k stars. In a level-k star, there is one center (deg=k) \n    # and k leaves (deg=1). \n    # When two leaves from different stars are connected, their degrees become 2.\n    # The center of a star retains its degree k.\n    # A leaf that was never connected to another star remains degree 1.\n    \n    # Key observation: The center of a star will always have a degree >= 2 (since k >= 2).\n    # The vertices that were leaves in the original stars will now have degree 1 or 2.\n    # A vertex is a center if it was the hub of a star. \n    # Let's identify \"true\" leaves of the final tree T.\n    # If we remove all leaves of T, the remaining nodes are either centers or \n    # leaves that were connected to other leaves.\n    \n    # A simpler approach: \n    # In the final tree, any vertex with degree 1 must have been a leaf of some star.\n    # Its only neighbor must be the center of that star.\n    # Once we identify a center, we can determine the size of the star it belonged to.\n    \n    degree = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for u in range(1, n + 1):\n        degree[u] = len(adj[u])\n    \n    # We can use a leaf-stripping process.\n    # Vertices that are leaves in the current graph must belong to a star center.\n    # The neighbor of a leaf is the center of its star.\n    \n    is_removed = [False] * (n + 1)\n    # star_size[center] will store how many leaves are attached to this center\n    star_size = [0] * (n + 1)\n    \n    # Queue for leaves\n    queue = [i for i in range(1, n + 1) if degree[i] == 1]\n    \n    # To avoid processing the same node as a leaf multiple times or \n    # processing a center as a leaf too early:\n    # In a star, there's 1 center and k leaves.\n    # We can greedily pick a leaf, identify its neighbor as a center, \n    # and remove all other leaves of that center.\n    \n    ptr = 0\n    centers = []\n    \n    while ptr < len(queue):\n        u = queue[ptr]\n        ptr += 1\n        if is_removed[u]:\n            continue\n            \n        # u is a leaf, its neighbor v must be the center\n        v = -1\n        for neighbor in adj[u]:\n            if not is_removed[neighbor]:\n                v = neighbor\n                break\n        \n        if v == -1: continue # Should not happen in a tree\n        \n        # v is the center. Now identify all current leaves connected to v.\n        # These were the original leaves of the star.\n        # However, some leaves might have been connected to other stars.\n        # The core property: the center v is the only vertex in the star with deg > 1 \n        # before the operation. After operations, only the center's degree \n        # remains the original k.\n        \n        # Wait, the simplest way: \n        # Each original star has exactly one vertex (the center) that is \n        # NOT a leaf in the original star.\n        # In the final tree, the centers are the vertices that are NOT \n        # connected to other centers by the \"leaf-leaf\" edges.\n        # Actually, the problem can be solved by observing that \n        # vertices with degree 1 in the current tree MUST be leaves of a star.\n        # Their neighbor MUST be the center.\n        \n        # Let's find all vertices that are currently leaves.\n        # For each leaf, its neighbor is a center.\n        # We can \"consume\" the star centered at v.\n        \n        # The original degree of the center v is the level k.\n        # The center v is connected to k vertices.\n        # All these k vertices were leaves in the original star.\n        # In the final tree, some of these k vertices might have degree 2 \n        # (if they were used to connect stars), and some might have degree 1.\n        # But all k neighbors of the center v in the ORIGINAL star are \n        # neighbors of v in the FINAL tree.\n        # And since no edges were added between the center and anything else,\n        # the degree of v in the final tree is exactly k.\n        \n        # So the levels of the stars are simply the degrees of the centers.\n        # Which vertices are centers?\n        # A vertex is a center if it's NOT a leaf that was connected to another leaf.\n        # Let's use the property: a vertex is a center if it's adjacent to a leaf.\n        # If we remove all leaves, the centers will be the new leaves or internal nodes.\n        \n        # CORRECT STRATEGY:\n        # 1. Find all vertices that are leaves in the current tree.\n        # 2. For a leaf u, its neighbor v must be a center.\n        # 3. The level of the star centered at v is degree[v].\n        # 4. Remove v and all its neighbors from the graph.\n        # 5. Repeat until no vertices left.\n        \n        # Let's refine:\n        # To ensure we don't pick a \"leaf-leaf\" edge vertex as a center:\n        # Always pick a vertex that is a leaf in the CURRENT tree.\n        # Its neighbor MUST be a center because if the neighbor was also a leaf,\n        # it would be a star of level 1, but k >= 2.\n        # If the neighbor was a \"leaf-leaf\" edge node, that node would \n        # have degree 2, not 1.\n        \n        # This is like peeling the tree.\n        \n        # Re-calculating with this logic:\n        break\n\n    # Revised loop\n    is_removed = [False] * (n + 1)\n    current_degree = [len(adj[i]) for i in range(n + 1)]\n    queue = [i for i in range(1, n + 1) if current_degree[i] == 1]\n    \n    res = []\n    ptr = 0\n    while ptr < len(queue):\n        u = queue[ptr]\n        ptr += 1\n        if is_removed[u]:\n            continue\n        \n        # u is a leaf, v is center\n        v = -1\n        for neighbor in adj[u]:\n            if not is_removed[neighbor]:\n                v = neighbor\n                break\n        \n        if v == -1: continue\n        \n        # The level is the original degree of v\n        res.append(len(adj[v]))\n        \n        # Remove the center and all its neighbors\n        is_removed[v] = True\n        for neighbor in adj[v]:\n            is_removed[neighbor] = True\n            # Update degrees of neighbors of neighbors\n            for nn in adj[neighbor]:\n                if not is_removed[nn]:\n                    current_degree[nn] -= 1\n                    if current_degree[nn] == 1:\n                        queue.append(nn)\n    \n    res.sort()\n    print(*(res))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n\n```",
