    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_471",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a array points representing integer coordinates of some points on a 2D plane, where points[i] = [x_i, y_i].\nThe distance between two points is defined as their Manhattan distance.\nReturn the minimum possible value for maximum distance between any two points by removing exactly one point.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: points = [[3,10],[5,15],[10,2],[4,4]]\nOutput: 12\nExplanation:\nThe maximum distance after removing each point is the following:\n\nAfter removing the 0^th point the maximum distance is between points (5, 15) and (10, 2), which is |5 - 10| + |15 - 2| = 18.\nAfter removing the 1^st point the maximum distance is between points (3, 10) and (10, 2), which is |3 - 10| + |10 - 2| = 15.\nAfter removing the 2^nd point the maximum distance is between points (5, 15) and (4, 4), which is |5 - 4| + |15 - 4| = 12.\nAfter removing the 3^rd point the maximum distance is between points (5, 15) and (10, 2), which is |5 - 10| + |15 - 2| = 18.\n\n12 is the minimum possible maximum distance between any two points after removing exactly one point.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: points = [[1,1],[1,1],[1,1]]\nOutput: 0\nExplanation:\nRemoving any of the points results in the maximum distance between any two points of 0.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n3 <= points.length <= 10^5\npoints[i].length == 2\n1 <= points[i][0], points[i][1] <= 10^8",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef min_max_distance(points):\n    \"\"\"\n    The Manhattan distance between (x1, y1) and (x2, y2) is |x1 - x2| + |y1 - y2|.\n    This can be rewritten as max(|(x1+y1) - (x2+y2)|, |(x1-y1) - (x2-y2)|).\n    Let u = x + y and v = x - y.\n    Max distance = max(max(u) - min(u), max(v) - min(v)).\n    \"\"\"\n    u_vals = []\n    v_vals = []\n    for x, y in points:\n        u_vals.append(x + y)\n        v_vals.append(x - y)\n    \n    n = len(points)\n    \n    def get_max_dist_after_removal(arr):\n        # To find the max range (max - min) after removing one element,\n        # we only need to consider removing the current min or current max.\n        # However, since we need to remove the SAME point for both u and v,\n        # we can't handle u and v independently inside this helper.\n        # This helper is for a single dimension; the main logic handles both.\n        pass\n\n    # To optimize, we identify the points that contribute to the current \n    # global max and min of u and v.\n    # Only removing one of these 'extreme' points can potentially reduce the max distance.\n    \n    u_sorted = sorted([(val, i) for i, val in enumerate(u_vals)])\n    v_sorted = sorted([(val, i) for i, val in enumerate(v_vals)])\n    \n    # Candidates for removal: the points that are min/max of u or v\n    candidates = {u_sorted[0][1], u_sorted[-1][1], v_sorted[0][1], v_sorted[-1][1]}\n    \n    def calculate_max_dist_excluding(idx):\n        # Find max(u) - min(u) and max(v) - min(v) excluding points[idx]\n        # We use the sorted lists to find the next best min/max quickly.\n        \n        # For u\n        u_min = u_sorted[1][0] if u_sorted[0][1] == idx else u_sorted[0][0]\n        u_max = u_sorted[-2][0] if u_sorted[-1][1] == idx else u_sorted[-1][0]\n        \n        # For v\n        v_min = v_sorted[1][0] if v_sorted[0][1] == idx else v_sorted[0][0]\n        v_max = v_sorted[-2][0] if v_sorted[-1][1] == idx else v_sorted[-1][0]\n        \n        return max(u_max - u_min, v_max - v_min)\n\n    min_overall_max = float('inf')\n    for cand_idx in candidates:\n        min_overall_max = min(min_overall_max, calculate_max_dist_excluding(cand_idx))\n        \n    return min_overall_max\n```",
