      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minOperations(word: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the minimum number of operations to remove all adjacent almost-equal characters.\n    Two characters are almost-equal if they are the same or adjacent in the alphabet.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(word)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    operations = 0\n    i = 0\n    while i < n - 1:\n        # Check if current character and next character are almost-equal\n        # abs(ord(a) - ord(b)) <= 1 covers equality and adjacency in alphabet\n        if abs(ord(word[i]) - ord(word[i+1])) <= 1:\n            # If they are almost-equal, we change word[i+1] to a character \n            # that is not almost-equal to word[i] nor to word[i+2] (if exists).\n            # This greedily resolves the conflict for both the previous and next pairs.\n            operations += 1\n            i += 2  # Skip the next character as it has been \"changed\"\n        else:\n            i += 1\n            \n    return operations\n```",
