      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimumCoins(prices):\n    n = len(prices)\n    # dp[i] will store the minimum cost to acquire all fruits from index i to n-1\n    # We initialize with infinity, and dp[n] = 0 as base case.\n    dp = [float('inf')] * (n + 1)\n    dp[n] = 0\n    \n    # Iterate backwards from the last fruit to the first\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        # The index of the fruit is 1-indexed for the offer calculation\n        fruit_index = i + 1\n        \n        # If we purchase the i-th fruit, we get the next 'fruit_index' fruits for free.\n        # This means we can cover fruits from i to min(i + fruit_index, n - 1).\n        # The next fruit we would need to pay for (or decide how to acquire) \n        # starts at index i + fruit_index + 1.\n        \n        # However, since we can choose to stop the \"free\" period early by purchasing \n        # any fruit within the range [i+1, i + fruit_index], the optimal strategy \n        # is to look at the cost of acquiring the remaining fruits starting from \n        # any index j where i + 1 <= j <= i + fruit_index + 1.\n        \n        # The range of fruits covered by the purchase of fruit i is [i, i + fruit_index].\n        # So the next fruit we MUST account for is at index i + fruit_index + 1.\n        # But we can also choose to pay for any fruit j in (i, i + fruit_index] \n        # to reset the offer.\n        \n        # The cost to cover everything from i onwards if we buy fruit i:\n        # cost = prices[i] + min(dp[j]) for i + 1 <= j <= i + fruit_index + 1\n        \n        # To optimize the range minimum query, we can just iterate or use a segment tree/deque.\n        # Given n=1000, O(n^2) is acceptable.\n        \n        limit = min(i + fruit_index + 1, n)\n        min_next_cost = float('inf')\n        for j in range(i + 1, limit + 1):\n            if dp[j] < min_next_cost:\n                min_next_cost = dp[j]\n        \n        dp[i] = prices[i] + min_next_cost\n        \n    return dp[0]\n```",
