    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_46",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nThe imbalance number of a 0-indexed integer array arr of length n is defined as the number of indices in sarr = sorted(arr) such that:\n\n0 <= i < n - 1, and\nsarr[i+1] - sarr[i] > 1\n\nHere, sorted(arr) is the function that returns the sorted version of arr.\nGiven a 0-indexed integer array nums, return the sum of imbalance numbers of all its subarrays.\nA subarray is a contiguous non-empty sequence of elements within an array.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [2,3,1,4]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: There are 3 subarrays with non-zero imbalance numbers:\n- Subarray [3, 1] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [3, 1, 4] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [1, 4] with an imbalance number of 1.\nThe imbalance number of all other subarrays is 0. Hence, the sum of imbalance numbers of all the subarrays of nums is 3. \n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,3,3,3,5]\nOutput: 8\nExplanation: There are 7 subarrays with non-zero imbalance numbers:\n- Subarray [1, 3] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [1, 3, 3] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [1, 3, 3, 3] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [1, 3, 3, 3, 5] with an imbalance number of 2. \n- Subarray [3, 3, 3, 5] with an imbalance number of 1. \n- Subarray [3, 3, 5] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [3, 5] with an imbalance number of 1.\nThe imbalance number of all other subarrays is 0. Hence, the sum of imbalance numbers of all the subarrays of nums is 8. \n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 1000\n1 <= nums[i] <= nums.length",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef sumImbalanceNumbers(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    The imbalance number of a sorted array is the number of gaps > 1.\n    The total imbalance sum is:\n    (Total number of pairs of distinct values in all subarrays) \n    - (Total number of pairs of consecutive values (x, x+1) in all subarrays).\n    \n    Actually, a simpler way to think about it:\n    For any subarray, the imbalance number is (number of distinct elements - 1) \n    minus (number of pairs {x, x+1} that both exist in the subarray).\n    \n    Sum over all subarrays:\n    Sum (distinct elements in subarray - 1) - Sum (pairs {x, x+1} present in subarray).\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    total_imbalance = 0\n    \n    # Part 1: Sum of (distinct elements - 1) for all subarrays\n    # Total subarrays = n * (n + 1) // 2\n    # Sum of (distinct elements) = Sum_{i=0 to n-1} (contribution of element at i)\n    # An element nums[i] is the first occurrence of that value in a subarray \n    # starting at 'start' and ending at 'end' if no index k in [start, i-1] has nums[k] == nums[i].\n    \n    distinct_sum = 0\n    last_seen = {}\n    for i in range(n):\n        val = nums[i]\n        prev_idx = last_seen.get(val, -1)\n        # This value is unique for all subarrays starting from (prev_idx + 1) to i\n        # and ending from i to n-1.\n        distinct_sum += (i - prev_idx) * (n - i)\n        last_seen[val] = i\n        \n    total_distinct_minus_one = distinct_sum - (n * (n + 1) // 2)\n    \n    # Part 2: Sum of pairs {x, x+1} present in subarrays\n    # For each value v, we find how many subarrays contain both v and v+1.\n    # A subarray contains both if it contains at least one index of v and at least one index of v+1.\n    # It's easier to use complementary counting: \n    # Total subarrays - subarrays that DON'T contain v - subarrays that DON'T contain v+1 \n    # + subarrays that contain NEITHER v nor v+1.\n    \n    consecutive_pairs_sum = 0\n    # Max value of nums[i] is n\n    for v in range(1, n):\n        # We look for pairs {v, v+1}\n        # Let indices of v be pos_v and indices of v+1 be pos_v_plus\n        # However, a simpler approach: \n        # A subarray [L, R] contains both v and v+1 if:\n        # max(first occurrence of v in [L,R], first occurrence of v+1 in [L,R]) <= R\n        # This is tricky. Let's use: \n        # Total subarrays - (subarrays without v) - (subarrays without v+1) + (subarrays without both)\n        \n        # Find indices where nums[i] == v or nums[i] == v+1\n        indices_v = [i for i, x in enumerate(nums) if x == v]\n        indices_v_plus = [i for i, x in enumerate(nums) if x == v + 1]\n        \n        if not indices_v or not indices_v_plus:\n            continue\n            \n        # Subarrays that contain BOTH v and v+1\n        # Total subarrays\n        total_subs = n * (n + 1) // 2\n        \n        # Subarrays NOT containing v\n        # These are subarrays formed by segments between indices of v\n        not_v = 0\n        prev = -1\n        for idx in indices_v:\n            gap = idx - prev - 1\n            not_v += gap * (gap + 1) // 2\n            prev = idx\n        gap = n - prev - 1\n        not_v += gap * (gap + 1) // 2\n        \n        # Subarrays NOT containing v+1\n        not_v_plus = 0\n        prev = -1\n        for idx in indices_v_plus:\n            gap = idx - prev - 1\n            not_v_plus += gap * (gap + 1) // 2\n            prev = idx\n        gap = n - prev - 1\n        not_v_plus += gap * (gap + 1) // 2\n        \n        # Subarrays containing NEITHER v nor v+1\n        # These are subarrays formed by segments that contain neither v nor v+1\n        neither = 0\n        prev = -1\n        combined_indices = sorted(indices_v + indices_v_plus)\n        for idx in combined_indices:\n            gap = idx - prev - 1\n            neither += gap * (gap + 1) // 2\n            prev = idx\n        gap = n - prev - 1\n        neither += gap * (gap + 1) // 2\n        \n        consecutive_pairs_sum += (total_subs - not_v - not_v_plus + neither)\n        \n    return total_distinct_minus_one - consecutive_pairs_sum\n```",
