    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_269",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a race through checkpoints 1,2,\\dots,N in this order on a coordinate plane.\nThe coordinates of checkpoint i are (X_i,Y_i), and all checkpoints have different coordinates.\nCheckpoints other than checkpoints 1 and N can be skipped.\nHowever, let C be the number of checkpoints skipped, and the following penalty will be imposed:\n\n- \\displaystyle 2^{{C−1}} if C>0, and\n- 0 if C=0.\n\nLet s be the total distance traveled (Euclidean distance) from checkpoint 1 to checkpoint N plus the penalty.\nFind the minimum achievable value as s.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nX_1 Y_1\nX_2 Y_2\n\\vdots\nX_N Y_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer. Your output is considered correct if the absolute or relative error from the true value is at most 10^{{-5}}.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- All input values are integers.\n- 2 \\le N \\le 10^4\n- 0 \\le X_i,Y_i \\le 10^4\n- (X_i,Y_i) \\neq (X_j,Y_j) if i \\neq j.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6\n0 0\n1 1\n2 0\n0 1\n1 0\n2 1\n\nSample Output 1\n\n5.82842712474619009753\n\nConsider passing through checkpoints 1,2,5,6 and skip checkpoints 3,4.\n\n- Move from checkpoint 1 to 2. The distance between them is \\sqrt{{2}}.\n- Move from checkpoint 2 to 5. The distance between them is 1.\n- Move from checkpoint 5 to 6. The distance between them is \\sqrt{{2}}.\n- Two checkpoints are skipped, so the penalty of 2 is imposed.\n\nIn this way, you can achieve s = 3 + 2\\sqrt{{2}} \\approx 5.828427.\nYou cannot make s smaller than this value.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n10\n1 8\n3 7\n9 4\n4 9\n6 1\n7 5\n0 0\n1 3\n6 8\n6 4\n\nSample Output 2\n\n24.63441361516795872523\n\nSample Input 3\n\n10\n34 24\n47 60\n30 31\n12 97\n87 93\n64 46\n82 50\n14 7\n17 24\n3 78\n\nSample Output 3\n\n110.61238353245736230207",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nimport math\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    coords = []\n    idx = 1\n    for _ in range(N):\n        x = int(input_data[idx])\n        y = int(input_data[idx+1])\n        coords.append((x, y))\n        idx += 2\n\n    # The penalty is 2^(C-1) for C > 0, and 0 for C = 0.\n    # Since N <= 10^4, and the max distance is roughly 10^4 * 10^4 * sqrt(2) \n    # (which is huge), but the penalty grows exponentially, C cannot be very large.\n    # 2^(C-1) will exceed the maximum possible distance very quickly.\n    # Max possible distance is roughly N * 10000 * 1.414 ≈ 1.4 * 10^8.\n    # 2^27 is ~1.3 * 10^8. So C will likely not exceed 30.\n    \n    # Let dp[i][c] be the minimum distance to reach checkpoint i having skipped c checkpoints.\n    # i: 0 to N-1\n    # c: 0 to min(i, 30)\n    \n    MAX_C = 31 \n    # dp[i][c]\n    dp = [[float('inf')] * MAX_C for _ in range(N)]\n    \n    dp[0][0] = 0.0\n    \n    for i in range(N):\n        for c in range(MAX_C):\n            if dp[i][c] == float('inf'):\n                continue\n            \n            # Try jumping to checkpoint j (j > i)\n            # The number of checkpoints skipped between i and j is (j - i - 1)\n            for j in range(i + 1, N):\n                skipped = j - i - 1\n                new_c = c + skipped\n                if new_c >= MAX_C:\n                    # Once we exceed MAX_C, the penalty 2^(C-1) is likely too high \n                    # compared to just visiting all points. However, we should \n                    # be careful. Let's check if j can jump straight to N-1.\n                    # If we jump from i to N-1, we skip all between them.\n                    # But we can only track up to MAX_C.\n                    # Actually, since penalty is 2^(C-1), if C is large, it's suboptimal.\n                    # If we are at index i and we want to jump to j, and the total skipped\n                    # so far + current jump > MAX_C, it's definitely worse than a smaller C.\n                    break \n                \n                dist = math.sqrt((coords[i][0] - coords[j][0])**2 + \n                                 (coords[i][1] - coords[j][1])**2)\n                if dp[j][new_c] > dp[i][c] + dist:\n                    dp[j][new_c] = dp[i][c] + dist\n\n    min_s = float('inf')\n    for c in range(MAX_C):\n        if dp[N-1][c] == float('inf'):\n            continue\n        \n        penalty = 0\n        if c > 0:\n            penalty = 2**(c - 1)\n        \n        min_s = min(min_s, dp[N-1][c] + penalty)\n\n    print(f\"{min_s:.20f}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n\n```",
