rings = 52^q. We need to count strings that do NOT satisfy this.\n    # Let q be the total number of '?'.\n    \n    # The condition for FAIL: exists k such that (Upper X appears twice in 0..k-1) AND (Upper Y appears in k+1..n-1).\n    # The condition for PASS: For all k where S[k] is Lower, \n    # EITHER (all Upper in 0..k-1 are distinct) OR (no Upper in k+1..n-1).\n    \n    # This means if we have lowercase letters, let the first one be at index 'first_L' \n    # and the last one be at index 'last_L'.\n    # If there is any Upper char after last_L, then for ALL lowercase letters S[k], \n    # the prefix S[0..k-1] must contain only distinct Upper characters.\n    # This implies that for the last lowercase letter S[last_L], S[0..last_L-1] contains distinct Upper characters.\n    # Since S[0..first_L-1] is a subset, that's automatically true.\n    \n    # Case 1: No Lowercase letters.\n    # Each '?' can be any of the 26 Uppercase letters.\n    # But wait, the original problem says replace '?' with any of 52 letters.\n    # If we want NO lowercase letters, each '?' must be one of the 26 uppercase.\n    # If S already contains a lowercase letter, this case is 0.\n    \n    # This is getting complex. Let's use the Complement:\n    # Total = 52^q.\n    # A string is \"Bad\" if there exists k: S[k] is Lower, prefix S[0..k-1] has duplicate Upper, suffix S[k+1..n-1] has any Upper.\n    \n    # Actually, the condition is: \n    # S is \"Good\" if:\n    # (No Lowercase letters) \n    # OR (No Uppercase letters after the last Lowercase letter)\n    # OR (All Uppercase letters before the first Lowercase letter are distinct, AND \n    #      all Uppercase letters before the second Lowercase... etc. \n    #      Actually, just: for all k where S[k] is Lower, S[0..k-1] has distinct Uppers OR S[k+1..n-1] has no Uppers).\n    \n    # Let's reconsider:\n    # If there are no lowercase letters, it's Good.\n    # If there are lowercase letters, let the last one be at index 'last_L'.