    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read N from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    n = int(input_data[0])\n    \n    # The problem asks for a Hamiltonian path on the grid graph \n    # that excludes the center cell (N+1)//2, (N+1)//2.\n    # Since N is odd, the grid has N^2 cells (odd). \n    # The center cell is at position (n//2, n//2) 0-indexed.\n    # A grid graph is bipartite. In an N x N grid, there are (N^2 + 1)//2 \n    # cells of one color and (N^2 - 1)//2 of the other.\n    # The center cell (n//2, n//2) always has the same color as the corners.\n    # If we remove the center cell, we are left with N^2 - 1 cells.\n    # For a Hamiltonian path to exist in a bipartite graph, the counts of \n    # the two colors must differ by at most 1. \n    # Removing the center cell (majority color) makes the counts equal.\n    \n    # We can construct this by finding a Hamiltonian cycle on the N x N grid \n    # (which is impossible for odd N), but we can find a path that covers \n    # everything except the center.\n    # A simpler approach: Use the standard snake-like traversal for \n    # the outer \"rings\" or segments.\n    \n    # Let's use a strategy: \n    # Divide the grid into a (N-1)x(N-1) block and a remaining strip, \n    # or use the property that a Hamiltonian path exists if we omit \n    # a cell of the majority color.\n    \n    # Construction for odd N:\n    # We can traverse the grid in a spiral-like or snake-like manner,\n    # but since the center is blocked, we can traverse the cells \n    # by skipping the center and adjusting the path.\n    \n    # Better approach: \n    # The center cell is (c, c) where c = n // 2.\n    # We can traverse the first c rows normally (snake), \n    # then the remaining rows skipping (c, c).\n    \n    res = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    c = n // 2\n    \n    # Path construction:\n    # 1. Start at (0, 0).\n    # 2. Cover rows 0 to c-1 using snake pattern.\n    # 3. At row c, cover columns 0 to c-1, then columns c+1 to n-1.\n    # 4. Cover rows c+1 to n-1 using snake pattern.\n    # However, we must ensure continuity.\n    \n    # Correct Hamiltonian path skipping center:\n    # Row 0: 0 -> n-1\n    # Row 1: n-1 -> 0\n    # ... until row c-1.\n    # If c-1 is even, we end at (c-1, n-1). If c-1 is odd, we end at (c-1, 0).\n    \n    path = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        if i % 2 == 0:\n            for j in range(n):\n                if not (i == c and j == c):\n                    path.append((i, j))\n        else:\n            for j in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n                if not (i == c and j == c):\n                    path.append((i, j))\n    \n    # The simple snake above breaks at row c.\n    # Let's refine: \n    # Rows 0 to c-1: Snake.\n    # Row c: go from (c, 0) to (c, c-1), then jump to (c, c+1) is not allowed.\n    # We need a continuous path.\n    \n    # Alternative: \n    # Divide the grid into two parts: Top N-1 rows and Bottom 1 row? No.\n    # Use the standard algorithm for Hamiltonian path in grid with one hole:\n    # For odd N, removing center (c, c) allows a path starting at (0,0).\n    \n    # Path:\n    # (0,0) -> (0,1) -> ... -> (0, n-1)\n    # (0, n-1) -> (1, n-1) -> (1, 1) -> (2, 1) -> (2, n-1) ... \n    # This covers the right side and the middle except column 0.\n    # Then come back through column 0.\n    \n    path = []\n    # Top rows (0 to c-1) - Snake\n    for i in range(c):\n        if i % 2 == 0:\n            for j in range(n): path.append((i, j))\n        else:\n            for j in range(n-1, -1, -1): path.append((i, j))\n            \n    # Now we are at (c-1, 0) if c is even, or (c-1, n-1) if c is odd.\n    # Row c:\n    curr_r, curr_c = path[-1]\n    if curr_c == 0:\n        # We are at (c-1, 0). Move to (c, 0) -> (c, c-1) -> (c+1, c-1) ...\n        # This is getting complex. Let's use a simpler zig-zag that avoids center.\n        pass\n\n    # Simpler logic: \n    # Traverse the boundary of the grid in a spiral, then move inward.\n    # But the center is the very last cell of a spiral.\n    # So we can just follow a spiral and stop at N^2-1!\n    # The spiral will end at (c, c). If we follow the spiral backwards, \n    # we start at the cell adjacent to (c, c) and end at (0, 0).\n    \n    spiral = []\n    top, bottom = 0, n - 1\n    left, right = 0, n - 1\n    while top <= bottom and left <= right:\n        for j in range(left, right + 1): spiral.append((top, j))\n        top += 1\n        for i in range(top, bottom + 1): spiral.append((i, right))\n        right -= 1\n        if top <= bottom:\n            for j in range(right, left - 1, -1): spiral.append((bottom, j))\n            bottom -= 1\n        if left <= right:\n            for i in range(bottom, top - 1, -1): spiral.append((i, left))\n            left += 1\n            \n    # The last element of spiral is (c, c).\n    # The path 1, 2, ..., N^2-1 is the spiral in REVERSE order, excluding (c, c).\n    # Reverse spiral: spiral[N^2-2], spiral[N^2-3], ..., spiral[0]\n    \n    final_path = spiral[:-1][::-1]\n    \n    grid = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for idx, (r, col) in enumerate(final_path):\n        grid[r][col] = idx + 1\n    \n    grid[c][c] = 'T'\n    \n    for row in grid:\n        print(*(row))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
