    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_169",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an array of positive integers nums of length n.\nA polygon is a closed plane figure that has at least 3 sides. The longest side of a polygon is smaller than the sum of its other sides.\nConversely, if you have k (k >= 3) positive real numbers a_1, a_2, a_3, ..., a_k where a_1 <= a_2 <= a_3 <= ... <= a_k and a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + ... + a_k-1 > a_k, then there always exists a polygon with k sides whose lengths are a_1, a_2, a_3, ..., a_k.\nThe perimeter of a polygon is the sum of lengths of its sides.\nReturn the largest possible perimeter of a polygon whose sides can be formed from nums, or -1 if it is not possible to create a polygon.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [5,5,5]\nOutput: 15\nExplanation: The only possible polygon that can be made from nums has 3 sides: 5, 5, and 5. The perimeter is 5 + 5 + 5 = 15.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,12,1,2,5,50,3]\nOutput: 12\nExplanation: The polygon with the largest perimeter which can be made from nums has 5 sides: 1, 1, 2, 3, and 5. The perimeter is 1 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 5 = 12.\nWe cannot have a polygon with either 12 or 50 as the longest side because it is not possible to include 2 or more smaller sides that have a greater sum than either of them.\nIt can be shown that the largest possible perimeter is 12.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [5,5,50]\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: There is no possible way to form a polygon from nums, as a polygon has at least 3 sides and 50 > 5 + 5.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n3 <= n <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef largestPerimeter(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    To maximize the perimeter, we should sort the array in ascending order.\n    The condition for a polygon with k sides is that the sum of the k-1 smaller sides \n    must be greater than the longest side.\n    \n    By iterating from the end of the sorted array, we can keep track of the \n    prefix sum of elements. The first time we encounter a situation where the \n    sum of all elements before index i is greater than nums[i], we have found \n    the largest possible perimeter because any further valid polygons would \n    necessarily use a smaller 'longest side'.\n    \"\"\"\n    nums.sort()\n    prefix_sum = 0\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        prefix_sum += nums[i]\n    \n    # Iterate from the largest element backwards\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 1, 1, -1):\n        # current_sum is sum of all elements from index 0 to i-1\n        current_sum = prefix_sum - nums[i]\n        if current_sum > nums[i]:\n            return prefix_sum\n        # Update prefix_sum for the next iteration (removing the current largest)\n        prefix_sum -= nums[i]\n        \n    return -1\n```",
