    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string s, which is known to be a concatenation of anagrams of some string t.\nReturn the minimum possible length of the string t.\nAn anagram is formed by rearranging the letters of a string. For example, \"aab\", \"aba\", and, \"baa\" are anagrams of \"aab\".\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s = \"abba\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation:\nOne possible string t could be \"ba\".\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s = \"cdef\"\nOutput: 4\nExplanation:\nOne possible string t could be \"cdef\", notice that t can be equal to s.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s.length <= 10^5\ns consist only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef min_length_of_t(s: str) -> int:\n    n = len(s)\n    # The length of t must be a divisor of the length of s\n    # because s is a concatenation of anagrams of t.\n    # If s is made of k copies of t, then n = k * len(t).\n    \n    # Pre-calculate the total counts of characters in s\n    total_counts = Counter(s)\n    \n    # Iterate through all possible lengths of t (divisors of n)\n    # starting from the smallest possible length.\n    for length in range(1, n + 1):\n        if n % length == 0:\n            # If length is a divisor, check if s can be split into \n            # anagrams of length 'length'.\n            # Every block of 'length' must have the same character distribution.\n            \n            # First block's counts will be the target distribution\n            target_counts = Counter(s[:length])\n            \n            # Check if total counts are compatible with this target\n            # (Every char count in total_counts must be target_count * (n // length))\n            possible = True\n            k = n // length\n            for char, count in total_counts.items():\n                if count != target_counts[char] * k:\n                    possible = False\n                    break\n            \n            if not possible:\n                continue\n                \n            # Verify each segment of the string\n            is_valid = True\n            for i in range(length, n, length):\n                current_counts = Counter(s[i : i + length])\n                if current_counts != target_counts:\n                    is_valid = False\n                    break\n            \n            if is_valid:\n                return length\n    \n    return n\n```",
