      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nimport math\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read D from stdin\n    try:\n        line = sys.stdin.read().strip()\n        if not line:\n            return\n        D = int(line)\n    except EOFError:\n        return\n\n    # We want to minimize |x^2 + y^2 - D|\n    # x can range from 0 up to ceil(sqrt(D)) + some small margin to check for values > D\n    # However, since x^2 + y^2 can be slightly larger than D, \n    # the maximum reasonable value for x is roughly sqrt(D + current_min).\n    \n    # To optimize, we iterate x from 0 up to sqrt(D) + small offset.\n    # For a fixed x, we want y^2 to be as close to (D - x^2) as possible.\n    \n    ans = float('inf')\n    \n    # Limit x to be up to sqrt(D) + some constant to check cases where x^2 + y^2 > D\n    # If x^2 > D + ans, then x^2 + y^2 - D will definitely be > ans.\n    # So x can go up to roughly sqrt(D + ans).\n    # Since D is up to 2e12, sqrt(D) is ~1.414e6, which is feasible in Python.\n    \n    limit = int(math.isqrt(D)) + 2\n    for x in range(limit + 1):\n        x2 = x * x\n        \n        # Target for y^2 is D - x2\n        target = D - x2\n        \n        if target <= 0:\n            # x^2 is already >= D, so y=0 is the best we can do for this x\n            ans = min(ans, abs(x2 - D))\n            # If x^2 already exceeds D + current_ans, further increasing x won't help\n            if x2 - D > ans:\n                break\n        else:\n            # target is positive, find y such that y^2 is close to target\n            y_low = int(math.isqrt(target))\n            # Check y_low and y_low + 1\n            ans = min(ans, abs(x2 + y_low**2 - D))\n            ans = min(ans, abs(x2 + (y_low + 1)**2 - D))\n            \n        # Early exit if we found the absolute minimum\n        if ans == 0:\n            print(0)\n            return\n\n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n\n```",
