    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_477",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an integer array coins representing coins of different denominations and an integer k.\nYou have an infinite number of coins of each denomination. However, you are not allowed to combine coins of different denominations.\nReturn the k^th smallest amount that can be made using these coins.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: coins = [3,6,9], k = 3\nOutput:  9\nExplanation: The given coins can make the following amounts:\nCoin 3 produces multiples of 3: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, etc.\nCoin 6 produces multiples of 6: 6, 12, 18, 24, etc.\nCoin 9 produces multiples of 9: 9, 18, 27, 36, etc.\nAll of the coins combined produce: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, etc.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: coins = [5,2], k = 7\nOutput: 12 \nExplanation: The given coins can make the following amounts:\nCoin 5 produces multiples of 5: 5, 10, 15, 20, etc.\nCoin 2 produces multiples of 2: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, etc.\nAll of the coins combined produce: 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, etc.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= coins.length <= 15\n1 <= coins[i] <= 25\n1 <= k <= 2 * 10^9\ncoins contains pairwise distinct integers.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef findKthSmallestAmount(coins, k):\n    # The number of multiples of x up to m is m // x.\n    # To find the number of unique multiples of a set of numbers,\n    # we use the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion (PIE).\n    \n    # Precompute least common multiples for all subsets to avoid redundant calculations.\n    # Since the number of coins is small (up to 15), we can use bitmasking.\n    n = len(coins)\n    lcm_map = {}\n    \n    # We only need subsets that could reasonably contribute to the count.\n    # However, 2^15 is small enough to precompute or calculate on the fly.\n    \n    def get_lcm(a, b):\n        return (a * b) // math.gcd(a, b)\n\n    # Precompute LCMs for all subsets using a bitmask\n    # subset_lcm[mask] will store the LCM of coins represented by the mask\n    subset_lcm = [0] * (1 << n)\n    subset_lcm[0] = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        subset_lcm[1 << i] = coins[i]\n    \n    for mask in range(1, 1 << n):\n        # Get the last set bit\n        lowbit = mask & -mask\n        # LCM of current mask is LCM of (mask without lowbit) and (lowbit coin)\n        subset_lcm[mask] = get_lcm(subset_lcm[mask ^ lowbit], subset_lcm[lowbit])\n\n    def count_multiples(m):\n        count = 0\n        for mask in range(1, 1 << n):\n            # Inclusion-Exclusion: odd number of elements are added, even are subtracted\n            bits = bin(mask).count('1')\n            term = m // subset_lcm[mask]\n            if bits % 2 == 1:\n                count += term\n            else:\n                count -= term\n        return count\n\n    # Binary search for the k-th smallest amount\n    # Minimum possible answer is min(coins), maximum is min(coins) * k\n    low = 1\n    high = min(coins) * k\n    ans = high\n    \n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if count_multiples(mid) >= k:\n            ans = mid\n            high = mid - 1\n        else:\n            low = mid + 1\n            \n    return ans\n```",
