    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_60",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nGiven three strings a, b, and c, your task is to find a string that has the minimum length and contains all three strings as substrings.\nIf there are multiple such strings, return the lexicographically smallest one.\nReturn a string denoting the answer to the problem.\nNotes\n\nA string a is lexicographically smaller than a string b (of the same length) if in the first position where a and b differ, string a has a letter that appears earlier in the alphabet than the corresponding letter in b.\nA substring is a contiguous sequence of characters within a string.\n\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: a = \"abc\", b = \"bca\", c = \"aaa\"\nOutput: \"aaabca\"\nExplanation:  We show that \"aaabca\" contains all the given strings: a = ans[2...4], b = ans[3..5], c = ans[0..2]. It can be shown that the length of the resulting string would be at least 6 and \"aaabca\" is the lexicographically smallest one.\nExample 2:\n\nInput: a = \"ab\", b = \"ba\", c = \"aba\"\nOutput: \"aba\"\nExplanation: We show that the string \"aba\" contains all the given strings: a = ans[0..1], b = ans[1..2], c = ans[0..2]. Since the length of c is 3, the length of the resulting string would be at least 3. It can be shown that \"aba\" is the lexicographically smallest one.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= a.length, b.length, c.length <= 100\na, b, c consist only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport itertools\n\ndef solve(a, b, c):\n    def merge(s1, s2):\n        \"\"\"Merges s1 and s2 to find the shortest string containing both as substrings.\"\"\"\n        # If one is a substring of another, the longer one is the result\n        if s1 in s2: return s2\n        if s2 in s1: return s1\n        \n        # Find the maximum overlap where s1 is prefix and s2 is suffix\n        max_overlap = 0\n        for i in range(1, min(len(s1), len(s2)) + 1):\n            if s1.endswith(s2[:i]):\n                max_overlap = i\n        \n        return s1 + s2[max_overlap:]\n\n    def get_shortest_superstring(strings):\n        \"\"\"\n        Given a permutation of strings, merges them sequentially.\n        Note: sequential merging might not be optimal for general cases, \n        but for 3 strings and the constraints, checking all permutations \n        and handling internal containment is key.\n        \"\"\"\n        # Since we only have 3 strings, we can test all permutations.\n        # However, the \"merge\" logic above only handles overlap at the end.\n        # To be robust, we need to check if any string is contained in another first.\n        \n        best_s = None\n        \n        for p in itertools.permutations(strings):\n            # Merge first two, then merge result with the third\n            res = merge(p[0], p[1])\n            res = merge(res, p[2])\n            \n            if best_s is None or len(res) < len(best_s):\n                best_s = res\n            elif len(res) == len(best_s):\n                if res < best_s:\n                    best_s = res\n        return best_s\n\n    # To handle cases where a string might be entirely contained in another \n    # (which simplify the problem), we can't just use simple merge.\n    # The merge function above handles containment for the specific pair.\n    \n    # We must consider that the optimal string might not be a simple linear merge \n    # of a permutation if we don't account for the \"substring\" property correctly.\n    # But for 3 strings, all permutations of sequential merging cover all \n    # shortest superstring possibilities.\n    \n    # Actually, the 'merge' logic provided is for the Shortest Common Superstring problem.\n    # With only 3 strings, checking all permutations and using the overlap logic is sufficient.\n    \n    # Correcting the merge to handle lexicographical smallest:\n    # When merging s1 and s2, there's only one 'shortest' merge for a fixed order.\n    # But the order of strings (a, b, c) changes.\n    \n    # Let's redefine merge to be more precise.\n    def overlap(s1, s2):\n        for i in range(min(len(s1), len(s2)), 0, -1):\n            if s1.endswith(s2[:i]):\n                return i\n        return 0\n\n    # We need to try all 6 permutations.\n    # For each permutation (p1, p2, p3):\n    # 1. Combine p1 and p2 to get s12 (shortest containing p1 then p2)\n    # 2. Combine s12 and p3 to get s123\n    # But wait, if p2 is already inside p1, s12 is just p1.\n    \n    ans = None\n    for p in itertools.permutations([a, b, c]):\n        # Sequential merge\n        current = p[0]\n        for i in range(1, 3):\n            next_s = p[i]\n            if next_s in current:\n                continue\n            # Find overlap\n            ov = 0\n            for length in range(min(len(current), len(next_s)), 0, -1):\n                if current.endswith(next_s[:length]):\n                    ov = length\n                    break\n            current += next_s[ov:]\n        \n        if ans is None or len(current) < len(ans):\n            ans = current\n        elif len(current) == len(ans):\n            if current < ans:\n                ans = current\n                \n    return ans\n```",
