      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef countStableArrays(zero, one, limit):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # dp[i][j][k] where:\n    # i: number of 0s used\n    # j: number of 1s used\n    # k: 0 if the last element added was '0', 1 if it was '1'\n    dp = [[[0] * 2 for _ in range(one + 1)] for _ in range(zero + 1)]\n    \n    # Base cases: if we only use 0s or only use 1s\n    # We can start with a sequence of 0s up to 'limit' (and not exceeding total 'zero')\n    for i in range(1, min(zero, limit) + 1):\n        dp[i][0][0] = 1\n    # We can start with a sequence of 1s up to 'limit' (and not exceeding total 'one')\n    for j in range(1, min(one, limit) + 1):\n        dp[0][j][1] = 1\n        \n    for i in range(zero + 1):\n        for j in range(one + 1):\n            # Transition for ending with '0'\n            # To place a '0' at the end, the previous state must have had (i-1) 0s and j 1s.\n            if i > 0:\n                # If the previous element was '1', we can always add a '0'.\n                dp[i][j][0] = (dp[i][j][0] + dp[i-1][j][1]) % MOD\n                \n                # If the previous element was '0', we can add another '0' as long as the \n                # contiguous block of 0s does not exceed 'limit'.\n                # This means we subtract cases where we already had 'limit' zeros in a row.\n                # Those cases happened when we transitioned from dp[i-limit][j][1] to \n                # a sequence of 'limit' zeros.\n                if i > limit:\n                    # The sequence ending in 'limit' zeros was formed by dp[i-limit-1][j][1] \n                    # and then adding 'limit' zeros. However, the logic is simpler:\n                    # dp[i][j][0] = dp[i-1][j][0] + dp[i-1][j][1]\n                    # But if i > limit, we must subtract the case where the last 'limit' elements were 0\n                    # and the element before that was a 1.\n                    dp[i][j][0] = (dp[i][j][0] + dp[i-1][j][0]) % MOD\n                    dp[i][j][0] = (dp[i][j][0] - dp[i-limit-1][j][1] + MOD) % MOD\n                else:\n                    # If i <= limit, we just add the previous state ending in 0\n                    # unless i is the very first block (handled by base case).\n                    # But we must avoid double counting base cases.\n                    # Since dp[i][0][0] is set to 1 for i <= limit, \n                    # we only add dp[i-1][j][0] if j > 0.\n                    if j > 0:\n                        dp[i][j][0] = (dp[i][j][0] + dp[i-1][j][0]) % MOD\n            \n            # Transition for ending with '1'\n            if j > 0:\n                dp[i][j][1] = (dp[i][j][1] + dp[i][j-1][0]) % MOD\n                if j > limit:\n                    dp[i][j][1] = (dp[i][j][1] + dp[i][j-1][1]) % MOD\n                    dp[i][j][1] = (dp[i][j][1] - dp[i][j-limit-1][0] + MOD) % MOD\n                else:\n                    if i > 0:\n                        dp[i][j][1] = (dp[i][j][1] + dp[i][j-1][1]) % MOD\n                        \n    return (dp[zero][one][0] + dp[zero][one][1]) % MOD\n```",
