    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_12",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed integer array nums representing the score of students in an exam. The teacher would like to form one non-empty group of students with maximal strength, where the strength of a group of students of indices i_0, i_1, i_2, ... , i_k is defined as nums[i_0] * nums[i_1] * nums[i_2] * ... * nums[i_k​].\nReturn the maximum strength of a group the teacher can create.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [3,-1,-5,2,5,-9]\nOutput: 1350\nExplanation: One way to form a group of maximal strength is to group the students at indices [0,2,3,4,5]. Their strength is 3 * (-5) * 2 * 5 * (-9) = 1350, which we can show is optimal.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [-4,-5,-4]\nOutput: 20\nExplanation: Group the students at indices [0, 1] . Then, we’ll have a resulting strength of 20. We cannot achieve greater strength.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 13\n-9 <= nums[i] <= 9",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maxStrength(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum strength of a group of students.\n    The strength is the product of the selected numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(nums) == 1:\n        return nums[0]\n\n    positives = [x for x in nums if x > 0]\n    negatives = sorted([x for x in nums if x < 0])\n    \n    # To maximize the product:\n    # 1. Multiply all positive numbers.\n    # 2. Multiply negative numbers in pairs (to make them positive).\n    # 3. If there's an odd number of negatives, leave out the one closest to 0.\n    \n    product = 1\n    has_contributed = False\n    \n    for p in positives:\n        product *= p\n        has_contributed = True\n        \n    # Use negative numbers in pairs\n    # Since negatives are sorted (e.g., [-9, -5, -2]), \n    # we take them two at a time from the smallest (most negative).\n    num_neg = len(negatives)\n    limit = num_neg if num_neg % 2 == 0 else num_neg - 1\n    \n    for i in range(limit):\n        product *= negatives[i]\n        has_contributed = True\n        \n    if not has_contributed:\n        # If no positives and not enough negatives to make a pair,\n        # the result is the maximum single element (likely 0 or the largest negative).\n        return max(nums)\n        \n    return product\n```",
