    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_293",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nAtCoder Inc. is planning to develop a product. The product has K parameters, whose values are currently all zero. The company aims to raise all parameter values to at least P.\nThere are N development plans. Executing the i-th development plan (1 \\le i \\le N) increases the value of the j-th parameter by A_{{i,j}} for every integer j such that 1 \\le j \\le K, at the cost of C_i.\nA development plan cannot be executed more than once. Determine whether the company can achieve its goal, and if it can, find the minimum total cost required to achieve the goal.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN K P\r\nC_1 A_{{1,1}} A_{{1,2}} \\dots A_{{1,K}}\r\nC_2 A_{{2,1}} A_{{2,2}} \\dots A_{{2,K}}\r\n\\dots\r\nC_N A_{{N,1}} A_{{N,2}} \\dots A_{{N,K}}\n\nOutput\n\nIf AtCoder Inc. can achieve its goal, print the minimum total cost required to achieve the goal; otherwise, print -1.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\le N \\le 100\n- 1 \\le K,P \\le 5\n- 0 \\le A_{{i,j}} \\le P(1 \\le i \\le N,1 \\le j \\le K)\n- 1 \\le C_i \\le 10^9(1 \\le i \\le N)\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4 3 5\r\n5 3 0 2\r\n3 1 2 3\r\n3 2 4 0\r\n1 0 1 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\n9\r\n\nIf you execute the first, third, and fourth development plans, each parameter will be 3+2+0=5,0+4+1=5,2+0+4=6, all of which are at least 5, so the goal is achieved. The total cost in this case is 5 + 3 + 1 = 9.\nIt is impossible to achieve the goal at a total cost of 8 or less. Thus, the answer is 9.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n7 3 5\r\n85 1 0 1\r\n37 1 1 0\r\n38 2 0 0\r\n45 0 2 2\r\n67 1 1 0\r\n12 2 2 0\r\n94 2 2 1\n\nSample Output 2\n\n-1\r\n\nYou cannot achieve the goal no matter what you do. Thus, print -1.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read N, K, P from standard input\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    K = int(input_data[1])\n    P = int(input_data[2])\n    \n    plans = []\n    idx = 3\n    for _ in range(N):\n        cost = int(input_data[idx])\n        attrs = [int(x) for x in input_data[idx+1 : idx+1+K]]\n        plans.append((cost, attrs))\n        idx += 1 + K\n        \n    # The total number of states is (P+1)^K\n    # Since K, P <= 5, max states = 6^5 = 7776\n    num_states = (P + 1) ** K\n    inf = float('inf')\n    dp = [inf] * num_states\n    dp[0] = 0\n    \n    # Helper to convert tuple state to index\n    def get_index(state):\n        res = 0\n        for val in state:\n            res = res * (P + 1) + val\n        return res\n\n    # Helper to convert index back to state tuple\n    def get_state(index):\n        state = []\n        for _ in range(K):\n            state.append(index % (P + 1))\n            index //= (P + 1)\n        return tuple(reversed(state))\n\n    # Precompute state mappings for efficiency\n    # We can simply iterate through all states in order for each plan\n    for cost, attrs in plans:\n        # Iterate backwards through DP to ensure each plan is used at most once\n        # However, since we are updating a new list or iterating carefully, \n        # it's safer to use a temp array or iterate in reverse order of index\n        # because we are adding values to state indices.\n        \n        # To avoid using the same plan twice in one transition, we iterate \n        # from the highest state index down to 0.\n        for s_idx in range(num_states - 1, -1, -1):\n            if dp[s_idx] == inf:\n                continue\n            \n            # Current state values\n            current_state = get_state(s_idx)\n            next_state = []\n            for i in range(K):\n                # Cap the parameter value at P\n                next_state.append(min(P, current_state[i] + attrs[i]))\n            \n            next_idx = get_index(next_state)\n            if dp[next_idx] > dp[s_idx] + cost:\n                dp[next_idx] = dp[s_idx] + cost\n                \n    # The target state is when all K parameters are at least P\n    target_idx = get_index([P] * K)\n    result = dp[target_idx]\n    \n    if result == inf:\n        print(\"-1\")\n    else:\n        print(result)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n\n```",
