    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_27",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given two integers, n and k.\nAn array of distinct positive integers is called a k-avoiding array if there does not exist any pair of distinct elements that sum to k.\nReturn the minimum possible sum of a k-avoiding array of length n.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: n = 5, k = 4\nOutput: 18\nExplanation: Consider the k-avoiding array [1,2,4,5,6], which has a sum of 18.\nIt can be proven that there is no k-avoiding array with a sum less than 18.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: n = 2, k = 6\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: We can construct the array [1,2], which has a sum of 3.\nIt can be proven that there is no k-avoiding array with a sum less than 3.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= n, k <= 50",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimumSum(n: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the minimum possible sum of a k-avoiding array of length n.\n    A k-avoiding array contains distinct positive integers such that no two \n    distinct elements sum to k.\n    \"\"\"\n    elements = []\n    current = 1\n    \n    while len(elements) < n:\n        # An element 'current' can be added if:\n        # 1. It is >= k (it cannot form a pair summing to k with a positive integer)\n        # 2. Its complement (k - current) is not already in the array.\n        # Since we pick numbers greedily starting from 1, if current < k, \n        # we only need to ensure k - current is not already picked.\n        # However, to keep the sum minimum, we pick elements greedily.\n        # If current < k and k - current > current, we can pick 'current'.\n        # If current < k and k - current <= current, we must check if k - current was picked.\n        \n        if current >= k or (k - current) <= 0 or (k - current) > current or (k - current) not in elements:\n            # Special logic for k-avoiding: \n            # For any x < k, we can pick either x or k-x, but not both.\n            # To minimize sum, we pick x as long as x < k/2.\n            # Once x reaches k/2, we skip to k.\n            if current < k and (k - current) < current:\n                # This current value would form a sum of k with a smaller value \n                # that we already decided to include.\n                pass \n            else:\n                # This is a simplified approach: \n                # Pick all numbers from 1 up to (k-1)//2.\n                # Then pick all numbers from k onwards.\n                # If we still need more, we might pick numbers between (k-1)//2 + 1 and k-1 \n                # but only those that don't pair with our picked small numbers.\n                # Actually, the simplest greedy strategy:\n                # 1. Pick integers from 1 up to (k-1)//2.\n                # 2. Pick integers from k onwards.\n                # 3. If we still need more, pick from the range [(k+1)//2, k-1] \n                # but we can only pick those that don't conflict.\n                # Wait, the rule is: for any pair (i, k-i), we can only pick one.\n                # To minimize sum, we pick i if i < k-i.\n                # So we pick 1, 2, ..., floor((k-1)/2).\n                # Then we can't pick k - floor((k-1)/2) ... k-1.\n                # Then we pick k, k+1, ...\n                break\n        current += 1\n\n    # Correct Greedy implementation:\n    res = []\n    # 1. Pick small values that don't sum to k with each other\n    # Values from 1 up to (k-1)//2 can be picked without ever summing to k.\n    for i in range(1, (k + 1) // 2):\n        if len(res) < n:\n            res.append(i)\n            \n    # 2. Pick values from k onwards (they can't sum to k with positive integers)\n    # and values from (k+1)//2 up to k-1 that don't pair with the ones picked.\n    # Actually, the optimal set is {1, 2, ..., floor((k-1)/2), k, k+1, ...}\n    # and if we still need more, we pick from {ceil(k/2), ..., k-1}? \n    # No, because any x in [ceil(k/2), k-1] pairs with something in [1, floor((k-1)/2)].\n    # So we pick 1...floor((k-1)/2), then we MUST skip to k, k+1...\n    \n    # Let's refine:\n    final_elements = []\n    # Pick 1, 2, ..., (k-1)//2\n    for i in range(1, (k // 2) + 1):\n        if i < k - i:\n            final_elements.append(i)\n            if len(final_elements) == n: return sum(final_elements)\n            \n    # Now we cannot pick any number x such that k-x is already in final_elements.\n    # The numbers we can pick now are k, k+1, k+2...\n    # Also, we can check numbers from (k+1)//2 to k-1, but they all pair with 1...\n    # Wait, if k=4, (k-1)//2 = 1. We pick [1]. Then we can't pick 3. We can pick 2 (2+2=4, but elements must be distinct).\n    # If k=4, n=5: 1 is picked. 2 is picked (2+2=4 is okay because elements are distinct). 3 is forbidden. 4 is picked, 5 is picked, 6 is picked.\n    # Array: [1, 2, 4, 5, 6]. Sum = 18. Correct.\n    \n    # Revised Greedy:\n    elements = []\n    i = 1\n    while len(elements) < n:\n        # If i < k, we can pick i if (k-i) is not already picked AND i != k-i\n        # Actually, if i < k and i == k-i, it's okay to pick i because we need distinct elements to sum to k.\n        # If i < k and i != k-i, we pick i only if k-i is not already picked.\n        # To minimize sum, if i < k-i, we definitely pick i.\n        # If i > k-i, we only pick i if we have no other choice, but we already checked k-i.\n        \n        if i >= k:\n            elements.append(i)\n        elif i * 2 == k:\n            elements.append(i)\n        elif i < k - i:\n            elements.append(i)\n        elif i > k - i:\n            # Check if the complement k-i was picked.\n            # Since we iterate i upwards, if i > k-i, then k-i was already considered.\n            # If k-i was picked, we cannot pick i.\n            if (k - i) not in elements:\n                elements.append(i)\n        i += 1\n        \n    return sum(elements)\n```",
