    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_19",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed permutation of n integers nums.\nA permutation is called semi-ordered if the first number equals 1 and the last number equals n. You can perform the below operation as many times as you want until you make nums a semi-ordered permutation:\n\nPick two adjacent elements in nums, then swap them.\n\nReturn the minimum number of operations to make nums a semi-ordered permutation.\nA permutation is a sequence of integers from 1 to n of length n containing each number exactly once.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [2,1,4,3]\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: We can make the permutation semi-ordered using these sequence of operations: \n1 - swap i = 0 and j = 1. The permutation becomes [1,2,4,3].\n2 - swap i = 2 and j = 3. The permutation becomes [1,2,3,4].\nIt can be proved that there is no sequence of less than two operations that make nums a semi-ordered permutation. \n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [2,4,1,3]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: We can make the permutation semi-ordered using these sequence of operations:\n1 - swap i = 1 and j = 2. The permutation becomes [2,1,4,3].\n2 - swap i = 0 and j = 1. The permutation becomes [1,2,4,3].\n3 - swap i = 2 and j = 3. The permutation becomes [1,2,3,4].\nIt can be proved that there is no sequence of less than three operations that make nums a semi-ordered permutation.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [1,3,4,2,5]\nOutput: 0\nExplanation: The permutation is already a semi-ordered permutation.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n2 <= nums.length == n <= 50\n1 <= nums[i] <= 50\nnums is a permutation.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimumOperations(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the minimum number of adjacent swaps to move 1 to the first position\n    and n to the last position of the permutation.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    # Find the indices of 1 and n\n    idx_1 = nums.index(1)\n    idx_n = nums.index(n)\n    \n    # Cost to move 1 to the front: idx_1\n    # Cost to move n to the end: (n - 1) - idx_n\n    ans = idx_1 + (n - 1) - idx_n\n    \n    # If 1 is to the right of n, moving 1 to the front will \n    # shift n one position to the right, reducing the cost \n    # to move n to the end by 1.\n    if idx_1 > idx_n:\n        ans -= 1\n        \n    return ans\n```",
