    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_420",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given a tree with N vertices. The vertices are numbered 1 to N, and the i-th edge connects vertices A_i and B_i.\nYou are also given a sequence of positive integers C = (C_1, C_2, \\ldots ,C_N) of length N. Let d(a, b) be the number of edges between vertices a and b, and for x = 1, 2, \\ldots, N, let \\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{{i=1}}^{{N}} (C_i \\times d(x, i)). Find \\displaystyle \\min_{{1 \\leq v \\leq N}} f(v).\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nA_1 B_1\nA_2 B_2\n\\vdots\nA_{{N - 1}} B_{{N - 1}}\nC_1 C_2 \\cdots C_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer in one line.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq N \\leq 10^5\n- 1 \\leq A_i, B_i \\leq N\n- The given graph is a tree.\n- 1 \\leq C_i \\leq 10^9\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4\n1 2\n1 3\n2 4\n1 1 1 2\n\nSample Output 1\n\n5\n\nFor example, consider calculating f(1). We have d(1, 1) = 0, d(1, 2) = 1, d(1, 3) = 1, d(1, 4) = 2.\nThus, f(1) = 0 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 1 + 2 \\times 2 = 6.\nSimilarly, f(2) = 5, f(3) = 9, f(4) = 6. Since f(2) is the minimum, print 5.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n2\n2 1\n1 1000000000\n\nSample Output 2\n\n1\n\nf(2) = 1, which is the minimum.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n7\n7 3\n2 5\n2 4\n3 1\n3 6\n2 1\n2 7 6 9 3 4 6\n\nSample Output 3\n\n56",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\n# Increase recursion depth for deep trees\nsys.setrecursionlimit(200000)\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input at once for speed\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    ptr = 0\n    N = int(input_data[ptr])\n    ptr += 1\n    \n    adj = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    for _ in range(N - 1):\n        u = int(input_data[ptr])\n        v = int(input_data[ptr + 1])\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        adj[v].append(u)\n        ptr += 2\n        \n    C = [0] * (N + 1)\n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        C[i] = int(input_data[ptr])\n        ptr += 1\n\n    # subtree_sum[u] will store the sum of C_i for all i in the subtree rooted at u\n    subtree_sum = [0] * (N + 1)\n    # f[u] will store the total weighted distance from u to all other nodes\n    f = [0] * (N + 1)\n    \n    # Total weight of all C_i\n    total_C = sum(C)\n    \n    # First DFS to calculate f(1) and subtree weights\n    # Using iterative DFS to avoid recursion depth issues on some platforms, \n    # though setrecursionlimit is usually enough.\n    stack = [(1, 0)]\n    order = []\n    parent = [0] * (N + 1)\n    \n    while stack:\n        u, p = stack.pop()\n        order.append(u)\n        parent[u] = p\n        for v in adj[u]:\n            if v != p:\n                stack.append((v, u))\n    \n    # Process nodes in reverse topological order to compute subtree sums and f(1)\n    for u in reversed(order):\n        subtree_sum[u] = C[u]\n        for v in adj[u]:\n            if v != parent[u]:\n                subtree_sum[u] += subtree_sum[v]\n                f[1] += subtree_sum[v]\n    \n    # Note: The initial f[1] calculation above is effectively:\n    # f(1) = sum_{v != 1} (subtree_sum[v])\n    # which is equivalent to sum_{i} (C_i * depth(i))\n    \n    # Second DFS to calculate f(v) for all v using the rerooting technique\n    # f(v) = f(u) - subtree_sum[v] + (total_C - subtree_sum[v])\n    # f(v) = f(u) + total_C - 2 * subtree_sum[v]\n    \n    # Process nodes in topological order\n    for u in order:\n        if u == 1: continue\n        p = parent[u]\n        f[u] = f[p] + total_C - 2 * subtree_sum[u]\n        \n    print(min(f[1:]))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n\n```",
