    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve(n):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    if n < 4:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Precompute factorials and their inverses for combinations\n    fact = [1] * (n + 1)\n    inv = [1] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        fact[i] = (fact[i - 1] * i) % MOD\n        \n    inv[n] = pow(fact[n], MOD - 2, MOD)\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        inv[i] = (inv[i + 1] * (i + 1)) % MOD\n        \n    def nCr(n, r):\n        if r < 0 or r > n:\n            return 0\n        num = fact[n]\n        den = (inv[r] * inv[n - r]) % MOD\n        return (num * den) % MOD\n\n    # Total strings of length n is 26^n\n    # A string is \"good\" if it has at least one 'l', at least one 't', and at least two 'e's.\n    # We use Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion (PIE) to find the number of strings that \n    # FAIL the criteria and subtract from 26^n.\n    \n    # Conditions for NOT being good:\n    # A: count('l') == 0\n    # B: count('t') == 0\n    # C: count('e') < 2 (i.e., count('e') == 0 or count('e') == 1)\n    \n    # We want to find |A U B U C|\n    # |A U B U C| = |A| + |B| + |C| - (|A∩B| + |A∩C| + |B∩C|) + |A∩B∩C|\n    \n    def count_with_constraints(forbidden_chars, e_limit):\n        # forbidden_chars is the number of characters we cannot use (excluding 'e')\n        # e_limit is the max number of 'e's allowed (0 or 1)\n        # Total available characters besides 'e' = 25 - forbidden_chars\n        available_others = 25 - forbidden_chars\n        res = 0\n        # iterate through allowed number of 'e's\n        for e_count in range(e_limit + 1):\n            if e_count > n: continue\n            # Choose positions for 'e', fill rest with available_others\n            ways = (nCr(n, e_count) * pow(available_others, n - e_count, MOD)) % MOD\n            res = (res + ways) % MOD\n        return res\n\n    # |A|: count('l') = 0. Available: 25 chars. 'e' can be anything.\n    # This is actually simpler: just 25^n. But to keep logic consistent:\n    # |A| is just 25^n\n    total_a = pow(25, n, MOD)\n    total_b = pow(25, n, MOD)\n    \n    # |C|: count('e') < 2. \n    # Ways = (nC0 * 25^n) + (nC1 * 25^(n-1))\n    total_c = (pow(25, n, MOD) + n * pow(25, n - 1, MOD)) % MOD\n    \n    # |A ∩ B|: count('l')=0 and count('t')=0. Available: 24 chars.\n    total_ab = pow(24, n, MOD)\n    \n    # |A ∩ C|: count('l')=0 and count('e') < 2. Available others: 24 chars.\n    total_ac = (pow(24, n, MOD) + n * pow(24, n - 1, MOD)) % MOD\n    \n    # |B ∩ C|: count('t')=0 and count('e') < 2. Available others: 24 chars.\n    total_bc = (pow(24, n, MOD) + n * pow(24, n - 1, MOD)) % MOD\n    \n    # |A ∩ B ∩ C|: count('l')=0, count('t')=0, count('e') < 2. Available others: 23 chars.\n    total_abc = (pow(23, n, MOD) + n * pow(23, n - 1, MOD)) % MOD\n    \n    union = (total_a + total_b + total_c) % MOD\n    union = (union - (total_ab + total_ac + total_bc)) % MOD\n    union = (union + total_abc) % MOD\n    \n    ans = (pow(26, n, MOD) - union) % MOD\n    return (ans + MOD) % MOD\n```",
