    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given two strings: S, which consists of uppercase English letters and has length N, and T, which also consists of uppercase English letters and has length M\\ (\\leq N).\nThere is a string X of length N consisting only of the character #. Determine whether it is possible to make X match S by performing the following operation any number of times:\n\n- Choose M consecutive characters in X and replace them with T.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\nS\nT\n\nOutput\n\nPrint Yes if it is possible to make X match S; print No otherwise.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq N \\leq 2\\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq M \\leq \\min(N, 5)\n- S is a string consisting of uppercase English letters with length N.\n- T is a string consisting of uppercase English letters with length M.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n7 3\nABCBABC\nABC\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\n\nBelow, let X[l:r] denote the part from the l-th through the r-th character of X.\nYou can make X match S by operating as follows.\n\n- Replace X[3:5] with T. X becomes ##ABC##.\n- Replace X[1:3] with T. X becomes ABCBC##.\n- Replace X[5:7] with T. X becomes ABCBABC.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n7 3\nABBCABC\nABC\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\n\nNo matter how you operate, it is impossible to make X match S.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n12 2\nXYXXYXXYYYXY\nXY\n\nSample Output 3\n\nYes",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    M = int(input_data[1])\n    S = input_data[2]\n    T = input_data[3]\n    \n    # The problem asks if S can be constructed by overlapping placements of T.\n    # This is essentially a reachability problem. We can think of this in reverse:\n    # Can we \"erase\" S back to a string of '#' by removing occurrences of T?\n    # Since M is very small (M <= 5), we can use a stack-based approach or \n    # dynamic programming. However, the most robust way is to treat it as \n    # a reachability problem where we mark indices that can be \"covered\" by T.\n    \n    # We can use a boolean array to keep track of which characters in S \n    # have been \"fixed\" as part of a T placement.\n    # But since we can overwrite previously placed T's, the \"last\" operation \n    # determines the characters. \n    # A character S[i] is valid if it is part of at least one placement of T \n    # that is not overwritten by a later placement of T at that specific position.\n    \n    # Actually, the simpler logic: S can be formed if and only if every character S[i] \n    # is part of some occurrence of T in S, and those occurrences can be ordered \n    # such that no \"necessary\" character is overwritten.\n    \n    # Correct Logic: This is a reachability problem on the string.\n    # Let dp[i] be true if the prefix S[0...i-1] can be formed.\n    # But T can overlap. This means we can place T at index i if S[i...i+M-1] == T.\n    # If we place T at i, then all positions from i to i+M-1 are now \"filled\".\n    # We can fill S if we can cover all indices 0...N-1 using intervals [i, i+M-1]\n    # where S[i...i+M-1] == T, and the intervals are applied in some order.\n    # Crucially, if we place an interval, it might overwrite a previous one.\n    # This is equivalent to saying: we can reach the end if we can cover \n    # the string using these intervals such that the \"top-most\" layer matches S.\n    \n    # Since we can overwrite, we only care if S[i...i+M-1] matches T.\n    # Let's define \"available\" intervals as those where S[i...i+M-1] == T.\n    # If we can cover the whole string using these intervals such that \n    # they \"connect\", we can potentially form S.\n    # Specifically, if we can cover [0, N-1] using intervals [i, i+M-1] such that \n    # each interval [i, i+M-1] overlaps or touches the previous coverage, \n    # we can build it from left to right or right to left.\n    \n    # However, the simplest observation for M <= 5:\n    # S can be formed if and only if every S[i] is part of at least one \n    # occurrence of T in S, AND there are no \"gaps\" that cannot be bridged.\n    # Actually, if every S[i] is covered by some occurrence of T in S, \n    # and these occurrences can be ordered, it's possible.\n    # For small M, we can use BFS/DP:\n    # dp[i] = can we cover the prefix up to index i?\n    \n    can_start = [False] * (N - M + 1)\n    for i in range(N - M + 1):\n        if S[i:i+M] == T:\n            can_start[i] = True\n            \n    # dp[i] is true if the first i characters can be covered by T's\n    dp = [False] * (N + 1)\n    dp[0] = True\n    \n    for i in range(N):\n        if dp[i]:\n            # Try to place T at any position j such that it covers index i\n            # and doesn't exceed the boundary.\n            # The placement must be at some j <= i and j + M > i.\n            # But since we are moving forward, we look for placements starting at j\n            # where j <= i and j + M > i, and the placement matches S.\n            for j in range(max(0, i - M + 1), min(i + 1, N - M + 1)):\n                if can_start[j]:\n                    dp[j + M] = True\n                    \n    if dp[N]:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
