    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_25",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given two 0-indexed integer arrays, cost and time, of size n representing the costs and the time taken to paint n different walls respectively. There are two painters available:\n\nA paid painter that paints the i^th wall in time[i] units of time and takes cost[i] units of money.\nA free painter that paints any wall in 1 unit of time at a cost of 0. But the free painter can only be used if the paid painter is already occupied.\n\nReturn the minimum amount of money required to paint the n walls.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: cost = [1,2,3,2], time = [1,2,3,2]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: The walls at index 0 and 1 will be painted by the paid painter, and it will take 3 units of time; meanwhile, the free painter will paint the walls at index 2 and 3, free of cost in 2 units of time. Thus, the total cost is 1 + 2 = 3.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: cost = [2,3,4,2], time = [1,1,1,1]\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: The walls at index 0 and 3 will be painted by the paid painter, and it will take 2 units of time; meanwhile, the free painter will paint the walls at index 1 and 2, free of cost in 2 units of time. Thus, the total cost is 2 + 2 = 4.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= cost.length <= 500\ncost.length == time.length\n1 <= cost[i] <= 10^6\n1 <= time[i] <= 500",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef paintWalls(cost, time):\n    n = len(cost)\n    # dp[i] represents the minimum cost to paint i walls.\n    # The maximum number of walls we need to account for is n.\n    # We initialize dp with infinity, except dp[0] = 0.\n    dp = [float('inf')] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0] = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        c = cost[i]\n        t = time[i]\n        # The paid painter takes time[i], and during that time, \n        # the free painter can paint (time[i] - 1) additional walls.\n        # Total walls painted by choosing this wall = t + 1 (if we consider the paid painter's 1 unit).\n        # However, it's simpler to say: this wall provides 't' units of coverage \n        # (1 for itself, and t-1 for others). \n        # Wait, the standard interpretation: the paid painter takes t units, \n        # so in that time, the free painter can paint t-1 other walls.\n        # Total walls covered = t + (t - 1)? No.\n        # Correct logic: The paid painter paints 1 wall and spends t time. \n        # In those t units of time, the free painter can paint t - 1 walls.\n        # Total walls painted = 1 + (t - 1) = t. \n        # Wait, if time[i] = 1, paid paints 1, free paints 0. Total = 1.\n        # If time[i] = 2, paid paints 1, free paints 1. Total = 2.\n        # Actually, the paid painter is occupied for 't' units. During those 't' units, \n        # the free painter can paint 't' walls? \n        # Re-reading: \"the free painter can only be used if the paid painter is already occupied.\"\n        # If paid painter starts wall i, they are occupied for time[i] units.\n        # In those time[i] units, the free painter can paint time[i] walls.\n        # Total walls painted = 1 (by paid) + time[i] (by free) = time[i] + 1.\n        \n        # Let's re-verify Example 1: cost=[1,2,3,2], time=[1,2,3,2]. n=4.\n        # If we pick wall 0 (t=1) and wall 1 (t=2):\n        # Wall 0: Paid paints it (1 unit), Free paints 1 wall.\n        # Wall 1: Paid paints it (2 units), Free paints 2 walls.\n        # Total coverage = (1+1) + (1+2) = 5. We only need 4.\n        # Let's check: paid paints wall 0 and 1. Total time = 1+2 = 3.\n        # While paid is working for 3 units, free can paint 3 walls.\n        # Total walls painted = 2 (paid) + 3 (free) = 5.\n        # So the formula is: Walls covered = (number of paid walls) + (sum of time[i] for paid walls).\n        # This is equivalent to: each paid wall i covers (time[i] + 1) walls.\n        \n        walls_covered = t + 1\n        for j in range(n, 0, -1):\n            # We take the minimum of not picking this wall, or picking it and\n            # covering up to 'walls_covered' walls.\n            dp[j] = min(dp[j], dp[max(0, j - walls_covered)] + c)\n            \n    return dp[n]\n```",
