    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_111",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array nums and an integer target.\nA 0-indexed array infinite_nums is generated by infinitely appending the elements of nums to itself.\nReturn the length of the shortest subarray of the array infinite_nums with a sum equal to target. If there is no such subarray return -1.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3], target = 5\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: In this example infinite_nums = [1,2,3,1,2,3,1,2,...].\nThe subarray in the range [1,2], has the sum equal to target = 5 and length = 2.\nIt can be proven that 2 is the shortest length of a subarray with sum equal to target = 5.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,1,1,2,3], target = 4\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: In this example infinite_nums = [1,1,1,2,3,1,1,1,2,3,1,1,...].\nThe subarray in the range [4,5], has the sum equal to target = 4 and length = 2.\nIt can be proven that 2 is the shortest length of a subarray with sum equal to target = 4.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [2,4,6,8], target = 3\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: In this example infinite_nums = [2,4,6,8,2,4,6,8,...].\nIt can be proven that there is no subarray with sum equal to target = 3.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^5\n1 <= target <= 10^9",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef shortestSubarray(nums, target):\n    n = len(nums)\n    total_sum = sum(nums)\n    \n    # The subarray can span multiple full repetitions of nums.\n    # Let target = k * total_sum + remainder.\n    k = target // total_sum\n    remainder = target % total_sum\n    \n    # If remainder is 0, the shortest subarray could be exactly k full copies,\n    # or it could be shorter by taking parts of the array from a sequence \n    # that wraps around (e.g., end of one copy and start of another).\n    # However, if k > 0, we can potentially reduce the length by looking for \n    # a subarray of length < n with sum == remainder.\n    # If k == 0, we only look for a subarray with sum == target.\n    \n    # To handle the \"wrap around\" cases, we concatenate nums with itself.\n    # Any subarray in infinite_nums with sum <= total_sum will be found in nums + nums.\n    extended_nums = nums + nums\n    \n    # Sliding window to find the minimum length subarray with sum == remainder\n    min_len = float('inf')\n    current_sum = 0\n    left = 0\n    \n    for right in range(len(extended_nums)):\n        current_sum += extended_nums[right]\n        \n        while current_sum > remainder and left <= right:\n            current_sum -= extended_nums[left]\n            left += 1\n            \n        if current_sum == remainder:\n            min_len = min(min_len, right - left + 1)\n            \n    if min_len == float('inf'):\n        # If we couldn't find the remainder, and remainder is 0, then k * n is a candidate.\n        # But if remainder != 0 and not found, it's impossible.\n        if remainder == 0:\n            return k * n if k > 0 else -1\n        return -1\n    \n    # The total length is the number of full copies plus the shortest subarray for the remainder.\n    # If remainder was 0, k * n is the base. But if we found a shorter subarray \n    # (which happens if we take k-1 full copies and a wrap-around subarray summing to total_sum),\n    # the sliding window logic over nums+nums handles that if we consider remainder = total_sum.\n    \n    # Correction for remainder == 0 case:\n    if remainder == 0:\n        # We search for the shortest subarray with sum == total_sum in nums + nums\n        # This is already covered by the logic above if we had set remainder = total_sum.\n        # Let's re-run for total_sum to see if we can get something shorter than n.\n        # But by definition, a subarray with sum == total_sum can be at most length n.\n        # If k > 0, the answer is (k-1)*n + min_len_for_total_sum.\n        # To simplify: if target % total_sum == 0, we search for remainder = total_sum and use (k-1)\n        \n        # Reset for remainder = total_sum\n        min_len = float('inf')\n        current_sum = 0\n        left = 0\n        rem_sum = total_sum\n        for right in range(len(extended_nums)):\n            current_sum += extended_nums[right]\n            while current_sum > rem_sum and left <= right:\n                current_sum -= extended_nums[left]\n                left += 1\n            if current_sum == rem_sum:\n                min_len = min(min_len, right - left + 1)\n        \n        return (k - 1) * n + min_len if k > 0 else -1\n\n    return k * n + min_len\n```",
