      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    # N: number of players, T: number of teams, M: incompatible pairs\n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    T = int(input_data[1])\n    M = int(input_data[2])\n    \n    adj = []\n    idx = 3\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(input_data[idx])\n        v = int(input_data[idx+1])\n        adj.append((u, v))\n        idx += 2\n        \n    # We need to partition N players into exactly T non-empty sets\n    # such that no two players in the same set are an incompatible pair.\n    \n    # Since N is small (<= 10), we can use recursion to assign each player \n    # to a team. To avoid overcounting partitions (since teams are indistinguishable),\n    # we use a canonical representation: a player is assigned to a team \n    # already in use, or to the first available new team.\n    \n    count = 0\n    # team_assignment stores which team (0 to T-1) each player i (1 to N) belongs to\n    assignment = [0] * (N + 1)\n    \n    def backtrack(player, teams_used):\n        nonlocal count\n        if player > N:\n            # We must have used exactly T teams\n            if teams_used == T:\n                count += 1\n            return\n        \n        # Try assigning 'player' to one of the teams already used\n        for t in range(1, teams_used + 1):\n            # Check if assigning player to team 't' violates any incompatibility\n            possible = True\n            for u, v in adj:\n                if u == player and v < player:\n                    if assignment[v] == t:\n                        possible = False\n                        break\n                elif v == player and u < player:\n                    if assignment[u] == t:\n                        possible = False\n                        break\n            \n            if possible:\n                assignment[player] = t\n                backtrack(player + 1, teams_used)\n                assignment[player] = 0\n        \n        # Try assigning 'player' to a new team (the next available ID)\n        if teams_used < T:\n            t = teams_used + 1\n            # Check compatibility for the new team (always true since it's new, \n            # but we check logic for consistency)\n            possible = True\n            for u, v in adj:\n                if u == player and v < player:\n                    if assignment[v] == t:\n                        possible = False\n                        break\n                elif v == player and u < player:\n                    if assignment[u] == t:\n                        possible = False\n                        break\n            \n            if possible:\n                assignment[player] = t\n                backtrack(player + 1, teams_used + 1)\n                assignment[player] = 0\n\n    # Start assigning from player 1, with 0 teams used\n    backtrack(1, 0)\n    print(count)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
