    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_441",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given a permutation P = (P_1, P_2, \\dots, P_N) of (1, 2, \\dots, N).\nA length-K sequence of indices (i_1, i_2, \\dots, i_K) is called a good index sequence if it satisfies both of the following conditions:\n\n- 1 \\leq i_1 < i_2 < \\dots < i_K \\leq N.\n- The subsequence (P_{{i_1}}, P_{{i_2}}, \\dots, P_{{i_K}}) can be obtained by rearranging some consecutive K integers.\nFormally, there exists an integer a such that \\lbrace P_{{i_1}},P_{{i_2}},\\dots,P_{{i_K}} \\rbrace = \\lbrace a,a+1,\\dots,a+K-1 \\rbrace.\n\nFind the minimum value of i_K - i_1 among all good index sequences. It can be shown that at least one good index sequence exists under the constraints of this problem.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN K\nP_1 P_2 \\dots P_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the minimum value of i_K - i_1 among all good index sequences.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq K \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq P_i \\leq N\n- P_i \\neq P_j if i \\neq j.\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4 2\n2 3 1 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\n1\n\nThe good index sequences are (1,2),(1,3),(2,4). For example, (i_1, i_2) = (1,3) is a good index sequence because 1 \\leq i_1 < i_2 \\leq N and (P_{{i_1}}, P_{{i_2}}) = (2,1) is a rearrangement of two consecutive integers 1, 2.\nAmong these good index sequences, the smallest value of i_K - i_1 is for (1,2), which is 2-1=1.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n4 1\n2 3 1 4\n\nSample Output 2\n\n0\n\ni_K - i_1 = i_1 - i_1 = 0 in all good index sequences.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n10 5\n10 1 6 8 7 2 5 9 3 4\n\nSample Output 3\n\n5",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    n = int(input_data[0])\n    k = int(input_data[1])\n    p = list(map(int, input_data[2:]))\n    \n    # Special case for k=1, the distance i_K - i_1 is always 0\n    if k == 1:\n        print(0)\n        return\n\n    # pos[v] stores the 1-based index of value v in the permutation P\n    pos = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n):\n        pos[p[i]] = i + 1\n    \n    # We are looking for a set of values {a, a+1, ..., a+k-1}\n    # The indices of these values are pos[a], pos[a+1], ..., pos[a+k-1]\n    # For a fixed 'a', the indices must be sorted to find i_1 and i_K.\n    # i_1 = min(pos[a...a+k-1]) and i_K = max(pos[a...a+k-1])\n    # We want to minimize (max - min) over all possible starting values a from 1 to N-K+1.\n    \n    # To efficiently find the min and max in a sliding window of size K,\n    # we can use a Monotonic Queue or a Segment Tree. \n    # Given N=2e5, an O(N) sliding window approach using two deques is optimal.\n    \n    from collections import deque\n    \n    min_deque = deque() # Stores indices of values in pos that are candidates for minimum\n    max_deque = deque() # Stores indices of values in pos that are candidates for maximum\n    \n    ans = n\n    \n    # Iterate through the values 1 to N\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # Maintain min_deque\n        while min_deque and pos[min_deque[-1]] >= pos[i]:\n            min_deque.pop()\n        min_deque.append(i)\n        \n        # Maintain max_deque\n        while max_deque and pos[max_deque[-1]] <= pos[i]:\n            max_deque.pop()\n        max_deque.append(i)\n        \n        # Remove elements that are out of the window [i-k+1, i]\n        if min_deque[0] <= i - k:\n            min_deque.popleft()\n        if max_deque[0] <= i - k:\n            max_deque.popleft()\n            \n        # When we have a full window of size K\n        if i >= k:\n            current_min = pos[min_deque[0]]\n            current_max = pos[max_deque[0]]\n            ans = min(ans, current_max - current_min)\n            \n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
