    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef countSubarrays(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the number of subarrays where the first and last elements are equal \n    and are also the maximum element within that subarray.\n    \"\"\"\n    import collections\n    \n    # To solve this efficiently, we use a monotonic stack to find the boundaries\n    # where each element is the maximum.\n    # For each index i, we want to find the range [L, R] such that for all k in [L, R], nums[k] <= nums[i].\n    # However, a simpler way to count subarrays where first == last == max is to group\n    # indices of the same value and check if any element between two occurrences is larger.\n    \n    n = len(nums)\n    # Store indices for each value\n    pos = collections.defaultdict(list)\n    for i, val in enumerate(nums):\n        pos[val].append(i)\n    \n    # We need to find the nearest element to the left and right that is strictly greater than the current element.\n    # left[i] = index of the first element to the left of i that is > nums[i]\n    # right[i] = index of the first element to the right of i that is > nums[i]\n    left = [-1] * n\n    right = [n] * n\n    \n    stack = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] <= nums[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n        if stack:\n            left[i] = stack[-1]\n        stack.append(i)\n        \n    stack = []\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] <= nums[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n        if stack:\n            right[i] = stack[-1]\n        stack.append(i)\n        \n    # For a value V, if indices are p1, p2, ..., pk\n    # a pair (pi, pj) forms a valid subarray if for all m between i and j, nums[m] <= V.\n    # This means no element in (pi, pj) can be > V.\n    # This implies that all indices from pi to pj must have their 'left' boundary < pi \n    # and 'right' boundary > pj relative to the maximum value.\n    # Actually, it's simpler: pi and pj can form a subarray if there is no element > V between them.\n    # This means they must belong to the same \"contiguous block\" of elements <= V.\n    \n    ans = 0\n    for val in pos:\n        indices = pos[val]\n        count = 0\n        for i in range(len(indices)):\n            # Start a new contiguous block of elements <= val\n            count = 1\n            # Check how many subsequent indices of the same value are not separated by an element > val\n            # We can use the 'right' array we computed: if right[indices[i]] <= indices[i+1], \n            # it means there is an element > val between indices[i] and indices[i+1].\n            \n            # Since we are iterating through the sorted indices list:\n            # We can just check if indices[j] < right[indices[j-1]]\n            # But we need to be careful about the loop. Let's refine:\n        \n        # Correct counting logic for indices of the same value:\n        # Divide indices into groups where each group is separated by at least one element > val.\n        if not indices: continue\n        \n        group_size = 1\n        for i in range(1, len(indices)):\n            # If there is any element > val between indices[i-1] and indices[i],\n            # then right[indices[i-1]] will be <= indices[i].\n            # Wait, right[i] is the first element STRICTLY greater.\n            # So if right[indices[i-1]] > indices[i], then no element between them is > val.\n            if right[indices[i-1]] > indices[i]:\n                group_size += 1\n            else:\n                ans += (group_size * (group_size + 1)) // 2\n                group_size = 1\n        ans += (group_size * (group_size + 1)) // 2\n        \n    return ans\n```",
