    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_508",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nA powerful array for an integer x is the shortest sorted array of powers of two that sum up to x. For example, the powerful array for 11 is [1, 2, 8].\nThe array big_nums is created by concatenating the powerful arrays for every positive integer i in ascending order: 1, 2, 3, and so forth. Thus, big_nums starts as [1, 2, 1, 2, 4, 1, 4, 2, 4, 1, 2, 4, 8, ...].\nYou are given a 2D integer matrix queries, where for queries[i] = [from_i, to_i, mod_i] you should calculate (big_nums[from_i] * big_nums[from_i + 1] * ... * big_nums[to_i]) % mod_i.\nReturn an integer array answer such that answer[i] is the answer to the i^th query.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: queries = [[1,3,7]]\nOutput: [4]\nExplanation:\nThere is one query.\nbig_nums[1..3] = [2,1,2]. The product of them is 4. The remainder of 4 under 7 is 4.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: queries = [[2,5,3],[7,7,4]]\nOutput: [2,2]\nExplanation:\nThere are two queries.\nFirst query: big_nums[2..5] = [1,2,4,1]. The product of them is 8. The remainder of 8 under 3 is 2.\nSecond query: big_nums[7] = 2. The remainder of 2 under 4 is 2.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= queries.length <= 500\nqueries[i].length == 3\n0 <= queries[i][0] <= queries[i][1] <= 10^15\n1 <= queries[i][2] <= 10^5",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve(queries):\n    def count_bits_up_to(n):\n        \"\"\"Returns the total number of 1-bits in binary representations of 1 to n.\"\"\"\n        if n < 0: return 0\n        count = 0\n        i = 0\n        while (1 << i) <= n:\n            full_blocks = (n + 1) // (1 << (i + 1))\n            count += full_blocks * (1 << i)\n            remaining = (n + 1) % (1 << (i + 1))\n            count += max(0, remaining - (1 << i))\n            i += 1\n        return count\n\n    def find_x(index):\n        \"\"\"Finds the smallest integer x such that the total bits up to x > index.\"\"\"\n        low = 0\n        high = 10**15 # Sufficiently large bound for 10^15 index\n        ans = high\n        while low <= high:\n            mid = (low + high) // 2\n            if count_bits_up_to(mid) > index:\n                ans = mid\n                high = mid - 1\n            else:\n                low = mid + 1\n        return ans\n\n    def get_power_at_index(index):\n        \"\"\"Returns the specific power of 2 at big_nums[index].\"\"\"\n        x = find_x(index)\n        # Position within the powerful array of x\n        pos = index - count_bits_up_to(x - 1)\n        # The powerful array for x consists of its set bits in ascending order\n        bits = []\n        temp_x = x\n        bit_val = 1\n        while temp_x > 0:\n            if temp_x & 1:\n                bits.append(bit_val)\n            temp_x >>= 1\n            bit_val <<= 1\n        return bits[pos]\n\n    def count_occurrences_of_bit(n, k):\n        \"\"\"Counts how many times 2^k appears in powerful arrays from 1 to n.\"\"\"\n        # 2^k appears in x if the k-th bit of x is 1.\n        # This is the number of integers from 1 to n that have the k-th bit set.\n        if n <= 0: return 0\n        full_blocks = (n + 1) // (1 << (k + 1))\n        count = full_blocks * (1 << k)\n        remaining = (n + 1) % (1 << (k + 1))\n        count += max(0, remaining - (1 << k))\n        return count\n\n    results = []\n    for from_i, to_i, mod_i in queries:\n        # Find x_start and x_end associated with the range\n        x_start = find_x(from_i)\n        x_end = find_x(to_i)\n        \n        total_exponent = 0\n        \n        if x_start == x_end:\n            # Both indices fall within the same integer x's powerful array\n            # Just find which powers of 2 are used\n            bits = []\n            temp_x = x_start\n            bit_val = 0\n            while temp_x > 0:\n                if temp_x & 1:\n                    bits.append(bit_val)\n                temp_x >>= 1\n                bit_val += 1\n            \n            start_pos = from_i - count_bits_up_to(x_start - 1)\n            end_pos = to_i - count_bits_up_to(x_start - 1)\n            for i in range(start_pos, end_pos + 1):\n                total_exponent += bits[i]\n        else:\n            # Part 1: bits of x_start from from_i onwards\n            bits_start = []\n            temp_x = x_start\n            bit_val = 0\n            while temp_x > 0:\n                if temp_x & 1: bits_start.append(bit_val)\n                temp_x >>= 1\n                bit_val += 1\n            start_pos = from_i - count_bits_up_to(x_start - 1)\n            for i in range(start_pos, len(bits_start)):\n                total_exponent += bits_start[i]\n                \n            # Part 2: All bits of integers from x_start + 1 to x_end - 1\n            if x_end > x_start + 1:\n                # Sum of k * (count of 2^k in range [x_start+1, x_end-1])\n                # Use property: sum_{k=0 to 60} k * count_occurrences(k, n)\n                for k in range(62):\n                    count = count_occurrences_of_bit(x_end - 1, k) - count_occurrences_of_bit(x_start, k)\n                    total_exponent += k * count\n            \n            # Part 3: bits of x_end up to to_i\n            bits_end = []\n            temp_x = x_end\n            bit_val = 0\n            while temp_x > 0:\n                if temp_x & 1: bits_end.append(bit_val)\n                temp_x >>= 1\n                bit_val += 1\n            end_pos = to_i - count_bits_up_to(x_end - 1)\n            for i in range(0, end_pos + 1):\n                total_exponent += bits_end[i]\n        \n        results.append(pow(2, total_exponent, mod_i))\n        \n    return results\n```",
