      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimizeStringLength(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    The operation allows us to pick a character and remove one occurrence to its left\n    and one to its right. This effectively means that as long as there are 3 or more \n    occurrences of a character, we can reduce the count by 2.\n    If there are 2 occurrences, we can reduce the count to 1.\n    If there is 1 occurrence, it remains 1.\n    \n    Crucially, the problem asks for the minimum length. Since removing characters \n    of one type does not affect the existence or relative positions of other \n    characters (only their indices shift), we can evaluate each character \n    independently. \n    \n    If a character appears N times:\n    - If N is odd, we can repeatedly remove pairs until 1 remains.\n    - If N is even, we can repeatedly remove pairs until 2 remain? No, if N=2, \n      picking one removes the other, leaving 1. If N=4, picking one removes two, \n      leaving 2, then picking one of those removes the other, leaving 1.\n      Wait, let's re-read: \"Delete the closest occurrence to the left AND the \n      closest occurrence to the right\".\n      \n      Example 1: \"aaabc\" -> 'a' at index 1 removes index 0 and 2. 'a' remains.\n      Example 2: \"cbbd\" -> 'b' at index 1 removes index 2. 'b' remains.\n      Example 3: \"dddaaa\" -> 'd' at 1 removes 0, 2. 'a' at 2 removes 1, 3. \n      Result: \"da\".\n      \n      Observation: For any character that appears at least once, we can \n      always reduce its count to 1 regardless of how many there were (N >= 1), \n      because as long as there are >= 2 characters, picking one will \n      always remove at least one other.\n    \"\"\"\n    # The minimum length is simply the number of unique characters present in the string.\n    return len(set(s))\n```",
