      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maxGroups(usageLimits):\n    \"\"\"\n    To maximize the number of groups, we want each group to be as small as possible.\n    The smallest possible sizes are 1, 2, 3, ... k.\n    \n    Since each group must contain distinct numbers, the total number of elements \n    needed for k groups is 1 + 2 + ... + k = k(k + 1) / 2.\n    \n    Crucially, the most restrictive part is the availability of numbers. \n    If we sort the usageLimits, we can greedily use the elements with the \n    highest limits to fill the larger groups. \n    \n    A simpler way to think about it: \n    Sort usageLimits in ascending order. Maintain a running total of available \n    elements. If the total elements available so far are at least the size \n    required for the next group (current_group_size + 1), we can increment \n    the number of groups.\n    \"\"\"\n    usageLimits.sort()\n    total_available = 0\n    groups = 0\n    \n    for limit in usageLimits:\n        total_available += limit\n        # Check if we have enough total elements to support a group of size (groups + 1)\n        if total_available >= groups + 1:\n            groups += 1\n            # Subtract the elements used for the group we just created\n            # Note: The condition 'distinct numbers' is implicitly handled because \n            # sorting and greedy accumulation ensures we don't use one number \n            # more than its limit across the total sum.\n            total_available -= groups\n            \n    return groups\n```",
