      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minCost(s1: str, s2: str, x: int) -> int:\n    n = len(s1)\n    diff_indices = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        if s1[i] != s2[i]:\n            diff_indices.append(i)\n    \n    # Each operation flips two bits. \n    # Therefore, the number of differing indices must be even to be solvable.\n    if len(diff_indices) % 2 != 0:\n        return -1\n    \n    if not diff_indices:\n        return 0\n    \n    m = len(diff_indices)\n    # dp[i] will store the minimum cost to fix the first i differences.\n    # Since we must fix differences in pairs, we only care about even i.\n    dp = [float('inf')] * (m + 1)\n    dp[0] = 0\n    \n    for i in range(2, m + 1, 2):\n        # Option 1: Pair the (i-1)-th and (i-2)-th differences using the cost-1 operation.\n        # The cost to flip bits at indices diff_indices[i-2] and diff_indices[i-1]\n        # using adjacent flips is simply the distance between them.\n        cost_adjacent = diff_indices[i-1] - diff_indices[i-2]\n        dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[i-2] + cost_adjacent)\n        \n        # Option 2: Pair the (i-1)-th and (i-2)-th differences using the cost-x operation.\n        # This is a direct flip of any two indices.\n        dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[i-2] + x)\n        \n        # Option 3: Consider the possibility that we paired indices in a non-sequential way.\n        # However, because we can pair any two indices for cost x, we only need to check\n        # the most recent pair and the global \"any two\" cost.\n        # The logic dp[i-2] + min(dist, x) covers the base cases.\n        # To handle the case where we use 'x' for pairs that are not adjacent in diff_indices,\n        # we realize that any set of m differences can be paired into m/2 pairs.\n        # The cost would be sum(min(dist_i, x)).\n    \n    # The DP above actually simplifies to:\n    # total_cost = 0\n    # for i in range(0, m, 2):\n    #     total_cost += min(diff_indices[i+1] - diff_indices[i], x)\n    # return total_cost\n    \n    # Let's re-verify: if x is very small, we might want to pair indices 0 & 3 and 1 & 2.\n    # But cost(0,3) + cost(1,2) = x + x = 2x.\n    # cost(0,1) + cost(2,3) = min(dist(0,1), x) + min(dist(2,3), x) <= 2x.\n    # So pairing greedily in sequence is optimal.\n    \n    res = 0\n    for i in range(0, m, 2):\n        res += min(diff_indices[i+1] - diff_indices[i], x)\n        \n    return res\n```",
