already in the \n        # set of distinct characters of the current partition.\n        pass\n\n    # Given the constraints and the nature of the \"one change\", \n    # we can use a recursive function with memoization.\n    # dp(i, used, last_char)\n    # i: current index\n    # used: 0 or 1 (if we have already used the change)\n    # last_char: the char we changed s[i] to (to potentially count it as one of the k)\n    \n    memo = {}\n    \n    def dp(i, used, last_char):\n        state = (i, used, last_char)\n        if i == n:\n            return 0\n        if state in memo:\n            return memo[state]\n        \n        # Option 1: Don't change any character in the current partition's start\n        # unless we are forced to or choose to later. \n        # Actually, the only character that matters for the \"change\" is the first \n        # character of a partition, because changing it to a character already \n        # present in the partition allows the partition to extend further.\n        \n        # We want to maximize partitions. A partition ends as soon as we encounter \n        # the (k+1)-th distinct character.\n        \n        # To maximize partitions, we want each partition to be as SHORT as possible?\n        # No, the problem says \"Choose the longest prefix... Delete the prefix\".\n        # This is a greedy process for a FIXED string. \n        # But we can change one char to change the boundaries.\n        \n        # Since we want to maximize the number of partitions, and the partitioning \n        # is greedy (always take longest), this is a shortest-path-like problem \n        # or DP.\n        \n        # Let's use the property: we only change one character. \n        # If we change s[i] to 'x', it's only useful if it allows the current partition \n        # to end earlier or later. But wait, the rule is \"longest prefix\". \n        # To get MORE partitions, we want the \"longest prefix\" to be as SHORT as possible.\n        # To mak