acter that is \"already\" in the \n        # partition or \"not\".\n        \n        # Let's try a different state: dp(i, used, prev_char)\n        # where prev_char is the character that the previous partition's \n        # last character was changed to.\n        # No, the most consistent state is dp(i, used, prev_char) where \n        # prev_char is the character that we changed s[i] to.\n        \n        # Let's use this logic: \n        # For a fixed string, the partitioning is deterministic.\n        # We can change one s[i] to 'c'. \n        # This only matters if s[i] is the character that would have \n        # extended the partition, or if changing it makes the partition end sooner.\n        \n        # Let's use a simpler DP:\n        # dp(i, used, char_of_change): \n        # This is too large.\n        \n        # Observation: The only character we'd ever change s[i] to is a character \n        # that appears in the string or a dummy character.\n        # Even simpler: we only change s[i] to a character that is either \n        # already in the current partition or not.\n        \n        # Actually, the most optimal character to change s[i] to is one \n        # that is either:\n        # 1. Already in the current partition (to make it longer).\n        # 2. Not in the current partition (to make it shorter).\n        # 3. The first character of the NEXT partition.\n        \n        # Let's use the state dp(i, used, prev_char) where prev_char is the \n        # character that we changed s[i-1] to. If s[i-1] wasn't changed, \n        # prev_char is s[i-1].\n        \n        return 0\n\n    # Re-implementing with a correct approach:\n    import functools\n\n    @functools.lru_cache(None)\n    def solve(i, used, prev_char):\n        if i == n:\n            return 0\n        \n        # We are at index i. We want to find the longest prefix with <= k distinct chars.\n        # But we can potentially change one character in this prefix.\n        \n        