    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef goodPartitions(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    # Store the last occurrence index of each number\n    last_occurrence = {num: i for i, num in enumerate(nums)}\n    \n    count = 0\n    partitions = 0\n    max_reach = 0\n    \n    # We iterate through the array to find \"cut points\".\n    # A cut point is where the current subarray contains all instances of the numbers it holds.\n    for i in range(n):\n        # Update the furthest index we must reach to complete the current block\n        max_reach = max(max_reach, last_occurrence[nums[i]])\n        \n        # If the current index reaches the furthest required index, we found a boundary\n        if i == max_reach:\n            partitions += 1\n            \n    # For 'k' independent blocks, there are 'k-1' gaps where we can either \n    # place a partition wall or not. This results in 2^(k-1) ways.\n    # However, the question defines the partition as one or more contiguous subarrays.\n    # If we have 'partitions' blocks, we have (partitions - 1) slots to cut.\n    # Each slot can either be a cut or not, giving 2^(partitions - 1) total ways.\n    # Note: For a single block, partitions=1, 2^(0) = 1 way.\n    \n    return pow(2, partitions - 1, MOD)\n```",
