d 5, inclusive.\n- R and C are strings of length N consisting of A, B, and C.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n5\r\nABCBC\r\nACAAB\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\r\nAC..B\r\n.BA.C\r\nC.BA.\r\nBA.C.\r\n..CBA\r\n\nThe grid in the output example satisfies all the following conditions, so it will be treated as correct.\n\n- Each row contains exactly one A, one B, and one C.\n- Each column contains exactly one A, one B, and one C.\n- The leftmost characters written in the rows are A, B, C, B, C from top to bottom.\n- The topmost characters written in the columns are A, C, A, A, B from left to right.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3\r\nAAA\r\nBBB\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\r\n\nFor this input, there is no way to fill the grid to satisfy the conditions.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom itertools import product\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    n = int(input_data[0])\n    r_target = input_data[1]\n    c_target = input_data[2]\n\n    # Possible characters for each cell\n    chars = ['A', 'B', 'C', '.']\n    \n    # The grid is small (N <= 5), but N^2 cells is too many for brute force.\n    # However, we can iterate through all permutations of row assignments.\n    # A valid grid is essentially 3 permutations (one for A, one for B, one for C)\n    # such that no two permutations share the same index (cell).\n    \n    import itertools\n    \n    perms = list(itertools.permutations(range(n)))\n    \n    # We need to pick 3 permutations: pA, pB, pC\n    # such that pA[i] != pB[i], pA[i] != pC[i], pB[i] != pC[i] for all i.\n    # This ensures each row and column has exactly one A, B, and C.\n    \n    # Optimization: To reduce search space, we can fix pA, pB, pC\n    # and check if they satisfy the R and C constraints.\n    \n    # Instead of 120^3, we can filter permutations.\n    # Let's try all triplets of permutations.\n    for pA in perms:\n        for pB in perms:\n            # Check if pA and pB overlap\n            valid_ab = True\n            for i in range(n):\n                if pA[i] == pB[i]:\n                    valid_ab = False\n                    break\n            if not valid_ab:\n                continue\n                \n            for pC in perms:\n                # Check if pC overlaps with pA or pB\n                valid_abc = True\n                for i in range(n):\n                    if pC[i] == pA[i] or pC[i] == pB[i]:\n                        valid_abc = False\n                        break\n                if not valid_abc:\n                    continue\n                \n                # Now check the \"leftmost\" and \"topmost\" constraints\n                # Construct grid\n                grid = [['.' for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]\n                for i in range(n):\n                    grid[i][pA[i]] = 'A'\n                    grid[i][pB[i]] = 'B'\n                    grid[i][pC[i]] = 'C'\n                \n                # Row constraint: leftmost char in row i must be R[i]\n                row_ok = True\n                for i in range(n):\n                    # Find first non-dot char in row i\n                    first = '.'\n                    for j in range(n):\n                        if grid[i][j] != '.':\n                            first = grid[i][j]\n                            break\n                    if first != r_target[i]:\n                        row_ok = False\n                        break\n                if not row_ok:\n                    continue\n                \n                # Column constraint: topmost char in col j must be C[j]\n                col_ok = True\n                for j in range(n):\n                    # Find first non-dot char in col j\n                    first = '.'\n                    for i in range(n):\n                        if grid[i][j] != '.':\n                            first = grid[i][j]\n                            break\n                    if first != c_target[j]:\n                        col_ok = False\n                        break\n                \n                if col_ok:\n                    print(\"Yes\")\n                    for row in grid:\n                        print(\"\".join(row))\n                    return\n\n    print(\"No\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
