    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_221",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nWe have a sequence A=(A_1,A_2,\\dots,A_N) of length N.  Initially, all the terms are 0.\r\nUsing an integer K given in the input, we define a function f(A) as follows:\n\n- Let B be the sequence obtained by sorting A in descending order (so that it becomes monotonically non-increasing).\n- Then, let f(A)=B_1 + B_2 + \\dots + B_K.\n\nWe consider applying Q updates on this sequence.\r\nApply the following operation on the sequence A for i=1,2,\\dots,Q in this order, and print the value f(A) at that point after each update.  \n\n- Change A_{{X_i}} to Y_i.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN K Q\r\nX_1 Y_1\r\nX_2 Y_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nX_Q Y_Q\n\nOutput\n\nPrint Q lines in total.  The i-th line should contain the value f(A) as an integer when the i-th update has ended.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- All input values are integers.\n- 1 \\le K \\le N \\le 5 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\le Q \\le 5 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\le X_i \\le N\n- 0 \\le Y_i \\le 10^9\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4 2 10\r\n1 5\r\n2 1\r\n3 3\r\n4 2\r\n2 10\r\n1 0\r\n4 0\r\n3 1\r\n2 0\r\n3 0\n\nSample Output 1\n\n5\r\n6\r\n8\r\n8\r\n15\r\n13\r\n13\r\n11\r\n1\r\n0\r\n\nIn this input, N=4 and K=2.  Q=10 updates are applied.\n\n- The 1-st update makes A=(5, 0,0,0).  Now, f(A)=5.\n- The 2-nd update makes A=(5, 1,0,0).  Now, f(A)=6.\n- The 3-rd update makes A=(5, 1,3,0).  Now, f(A)=8.\n- The 4-th update makes A=(5, 1,3,2).  Now, f(A)=8.\n- The 5-th update makes A=(5,10,3,2).  Now, f(A)=15.\n- The 6-th update makes A=(0,10,3,2).  Now, f(A)=13.\n- The 7-th update makes A=(0,10,3,0).  Now, f(A)=13.\n- The 8-th update makes A=(0,10,1,0).  Now, f(A)=11.\n- The 9-th update makes A=(0, 0,1,0).  Now, f(A)=1.\n- The 10-th update makes A=(0, 0,0,0).  Now, f(A)=0.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nimport heapq\n\ndef solve():\n    # Increase recursion depth for deep trees if necessary, \n    # though we use an iterative approach here.\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    K = int(input_data[1])\n    Q = int(input_data[2])\n    \n    A = [0] * (N + 1)\n    \n    # We maintain two heaps:\n    # top_k: a min-heap containing the K largest elements currently in A.\n    # rest: a max-heap containing the remaining N-K elements.\n    # Since Python's heapq is a min-heap, we store elements in 'rest' as negative values.\n    \n    top_k = [] # Min-heap\n    rest = []   # Max-heap (stored as negative)\n    \n    # current_sum tracks the sum of elements in top_k\n    current_sum = 0\n    \n    # Initially, all A_i are 0. \n    # We fill top_k with K zeros and the rest with N-K zeros.\n    for _ in range(K):\n        heapq.heappush(top_k, 0)\n    for _ in range(N - K):\n        heapq.heappush(rest, 0)\n        \n    # To handle updates (removing elements from heaps), we use lazy removal.\n    # We track the counts of elements that need to be removed.\n    top_k_remove = {}\n    rest_remove = {}\n    \n    def clean_heap(heap, remove_dict, is_max_heap):\n        while heap:\n            val = -heap[0] if is_max_heap else heap[0]\n            if remove_dict.get(val, 0) > 0:\n                heapq.heappop(heap)\n                remove_dict[val] -= 1\n            else:\n                break\n\n    ptr = 3\n    results = []\n    \n    for _ in range(Q):\n        X = int(input_data[ptr])\n        Y = int(input_data[ptr+1])\n        ptr += 2\n        \n        old_val = A[X]\n        A[X] = Y\n        \n        # 1. Remove old_val from whichever heap it is in\n        # Check if old_val is in top_k or rest. \n        # Since we can't search the heap, we check against the current min of top_k.\n        clean_heap(top_k, top_k_remove, False)\n        \n        if top_k and old_val >= top_k[0]:\n            # Potentially in top_k. But old_val might be exactly top_k[0] and actually be in rest.\n            # However, the logic of maintaining top_k means if it's >= min(top_k), \n            # and we are updating a value, we need to be careful.\n            # A better way: if it's in top_k, it contributed to current_sum.\n            # We use the fact that if it's in top_k, current_sum must be reduced.\n            # But we don't know for sure. Let's use a more robust tracking.\n            pass\n        \n        # Correct logic for lazy removal in this specific problem:\n        # We must track if the value we are removing was part of the top K.\n        # Since multiple indices can have the same value, we can't just check the value.\n        # But we only care about the sum of the top K.\n        \n        # Let's redefine: top_k always contains the K largest.\n        # When A[X] changes from old_val to Y:\n        # if old_val was in top_k: current_sum -= old_val, remove from top_k, add Y to top_k logic\n        # else: remove from rest, add Y to top_k logic\n        \n        # Since we don't know if A[X] was in top_k, we can use the property:\n        # If old_val > top_k[0], it must have been in top_k.\n        # If old_val < top_k[0], it must have been in rest.\n        # If old_val == top_k[0], it could be either, but logically it doesn't matter which one we remove.\n        \n        clean_heap(top_k, top_k_remove, False)\n        if old_val >= top_k[0]:\n            # Remove from top_k\n            current_sum -= old_val\n            top_k_remove[old_val] = top_k_remove.get(old_val, 0) + 1\n            # We just \"emptied\" a slot in top_k, we'll fill it with Y or something from rest.\n            # Temporarily, top_k has K-1 elements.\n        else:\n            # Remove from rest\n            rest_remove[old_val] = rest_remove.get(old_val, 0) + 1\n            \n        # Now insert Y\n        # We want to put Y in the best possible place and then balance.\n        # Simplified approach: push Y into top_k, then move the smallest of top_k to rest.\n        heapq.heappush(top_k, Y)\n        current_sum += Y\n        \n        # Balance: move smallest of top_k to rest to keep top_k size at K\n        clean_heap(top_k, top_k_remove, False)\n        smallest_top = heapq.heappop(top_k)\n        current_sum -= smallest_top\n        heapq.heappush(rest, -smallest_top)\n        \n        # Balance: if rest has something larger than the smallest of top_k, swap them.\n        clean_heap(top_k, top_k_remove, False)\n        clean_heap(rest, rest_remove, True)\n        \n        if rest and -rest[0] > top_k[0]:\n            largest_rest = -heapq.heappop(rest)\n            smallest_top = heapq.heappop(top_k)\n            \n            current_sum += largest_rest\n            current_sum -= smallest_top\n            \n            heapq.heappush(top_k, largest_rest)\n            heapq.heappush(rest, -smallest_top)\n            \n        results.append(str(current_sum))\n        \n    sys.stdout.write('\\n'.join(results) + '\\n')\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
