    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_136",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given three strings s1, s2, and s3. You have to perform the following operation on these three strings as many times as you want.\nIn one operation you can choose one of these three strings such that its length is at least 2 and delete the rightmost character of it.\nReturn the minimum number of operations you need to perform to make the three strings equal if there is a way to make them equal, otherwise, return -1.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s1 = \"abc\", s2 = \"abb\", s3 = \"ab\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: Performing operations on s1 and s2 once will lead to three equal strings.\nIt can be shown that there is no way to make them equal with less than two operations.\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s1 = \"dac\", s2 = \"bac\", s3 = \"cac\"\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: Because the leftmost letters of s1 and s2 are not equal, they could not be equal after any number of operations. So the answer is -1.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s1.length, s2.length, s3.length <= 100\ns1, s2 and s3 consist only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve(s1: str, s2: str, s3: str) -> int:\n    # The only way to make strings equal by deleting from the right\n    # is to find the longest common prefix shared by all three strings.\n    \n    # Find the length of the shortest string to limit the prefix search\n    min_len = min(len(s1), len(s2), len(s3))\n    common_len = 0\n    \n    # Iterate through the characters of the strings\n    for i in range(min_len):\n        if s1[i] == s2[i] == s3[i]:\n            common_len += 1\n        else:\n            break\n            \n    # The goal is to make all three strings equal to this common prefix.\n    # The number of operations is the total characters removed.\n    # Operations = (len(s1) - common_len) + (len(s2) - common_len) + (len(s3) - common_len)\n    \n    # However, the prompt says \"if there is a way to make them equal\".\n    # Since we can delete any number of characters from the right,\n    # as long as they share a common prefix (even if it's an empty string),\n    # they can be made equal.\n    # BUT, wait: the problem says \"delete the rightmost character... such that its length is at least 2\".\n    # Actually, reading carefully: \"choose one of these three strings such that its length is at least 2\".\n    # This means we can reduce a string down to length 1, but not length 0.\n    \n    # Let's re-evaluate: if the first characters are not the same, they can NEVER be equal\n    # because we can't delete the very first character (since length must be >= 2 to delete).\n    # Therefore, if s1[0], s2[0], and s3[0] are not identical, return -1.\n    \n    if s1[0] != s2[0] or s2[0] != s3[0]:\n        return -1\n    \n    total_ops = (len(s1) - common_len) + (len(s2) - common_len) + (len(s3) - common_len)\n    return total_ops\n```",
