    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Many cryptographic protocols base their security on assumptions about the computational difficulty of integer factorization. Integer factorization serves this purpose because we believe that\n\nOptions: \nA. testing primality is computationally intractable\nB. integer multiplication is a function whose inverse, factorization, remains difficult for a large class of inputs\nC. only prime numbers can be factored efficiently\nD. even if P = NP, integer factorization is still likely not to be polynomial-time computable\nE. P = NP\nF. integer factorization is a NP-complete problem\nG. integer multiplication is a function whose inverse, factorization, is easy for a large class of inputs\nH. all integers can be factored in polynomial time\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The security of cryptographic protocols like RSA relies on the existence of \"one-way functions,\" where the forward operation is computationally easy but the inverse operation is computationally difficult. In this case, multiplying two large prime numbers is easy (polynomial time), but finding those primes given only their product (integer factorization) is believed to be computationally intractable for large inputs.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
