  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9090",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Roughly how many bits are required on the average to describe to 3 digit accuracy the decay time (in years) of a radium atom if the half-life of radium is 80 years? Note that half-life is the median of the distribution.\n\nOptions: \nA. 50\nB. 35\nC. 10\nD. 40\nE. 28\nF. 16\nG. 24\nH. 45\nI. 19\nJ. 30\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the number of bits required to describe a value to a certain accuracy, we calculate the entropy of the distribution or the range of values. For an exponential distribution (like radioactive decay), the average information (entropy) is given by $H = \\log_2(e \\cdot \\tau)$, where $\\tau$ is the mean lifetime. \n\n1. The mean lifetime $\\tau$ is related to the half-life $t_{1/2}$ by $\\tau = t_{1/2} / \\ln(2) \\approx 80 / 0.693 \\approx 115.4$ years.\n2. The entropy of the continuous distribution is $\\log_2(e \\cdot 115.4) \\approx \\log_2(313.6) \\approx 8.3$ bits.\n3. To achieve 3-digit accuracy (which implies a precision of roughly $1/1000$ of the value), we add $\\log_2(1000) \\approx 9.97$ bits.\n4. Total bits $\\approx 8.3 + 9.97 = 18.27$ bits. Among the options, 19 is the closest fit.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
