    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9175",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: How many labeled graphs with a score of (6, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2) are there?\n\nOptions: \nA. 35\nB. 20\nC. 25\nD. 18\nE. 30\nF. 24\nG. 40\nH. 12\nI. 15\nJ. 10\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A score sequence $(d_1, d_2, \\dots, d_n)$ represents the degrees of the vertices in a labeled graph. In this case, we have 7 vertices where one vertex has degree 6 and the other 6 vertices have degree 2.\n\n1. A vertex with degree 6 in a graph with 7 vertices must be connected to every other vertex in the graph.\n2. Once the central vertex (degree 6) is connected to all others, each of the remaining 6 vertices already has a degree of 1. To reach a degree of 2, each of these 6 vertices must be involved in exactly one additional edge among themselves.\n3. This reduces the problem to finding the number of ways to form a perfect matching (3 edges) among the remaining 6 vertices, which is calculated as $\\frac{1}{3!} \\binom{6}{2} \\binom{4}{2} \\binom{2}{2} = \\frac{15 \\times 6 \\times 1}{6} = 15$.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
