    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the joint probability $P(A, B, C)$, we use the general chain rule of probability. The chain rule states that for any set of random variables, the joint distribution can be decomposed as:\n$P(A, B, C) = P(A, B | C) \\cdot P(C)$ (or any permutation thereof, such as $P(C | A, B) \\cdot P(A, B)$).\n\nLooking at the options:\n- Option D is $P(A, B | C) \\cdot P(C)$, which is a direct application of the chain rule.\n- Other options (like B or C) assume independence (e.g., $P(A, B) = P(A)P(B)$), which the question explicitly forbids.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
