    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9234",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A particular parallel program computation requires 100 seconds when executed on a single processor. If 40 percent of this computation is \"inherently sequential\" (i.e., will not benefit from additional processors), then the theoretically best possible elapsed times for this program running with 2 and 4 processors, respectively, are\n\nOptions: \nA. 100 and 50 seconds\nB. 40 and 20 seconds\nC. 60 and 30 seconds\nD. 20 and 10 seconds\nE. 80 and 40 seconds\nF. 70 and 55 seconds\nG. 30 and 15 seconds\nH. 50 and 25 seconds\nI. 110 and 55 seconds\nJ. 90 and 45 seconds\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the theoretical best elapsed time, we use **Amdahl's Law**.\n\n1.  **Identify the components:** The total time is 100 seconds. The sequential portion is $40\\%$ (40 seconds) and the parallelizable portion is $60\\%$ (60 seconds).\n2.  **Calculate for 2 processors:** The sequential part remains 40s, and the parallel part is divided by 2: $40 + (60 / 2) = 40 + 30 = 70$ seconds.\n3.  **Calculate for 4 processors:** The sequential part remains 40s, and the parallel part is divided by 4: $40 + (60 / 4) = 40 + 15 = 55$ seconds.\n\nThe resulting times are 70 and 55 seconds.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
