  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9302",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Of the following, which best approximates the ratio of the number of nonterminal nodes to the total number of nodes in a complete K-ary tree of depth N?\n\nOptions: \nA. N/K\nB. (N-1)/K\nC. K-1/K\nD. N-1/m\nE. log_10 (1/N)\nF. 1/N\nG. (K-1)/N\nH. K/N\nI. 1/(K-1)\nJ. 1/K\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the ratio of nonterminal nodes to total nodes in a complete $K$-ary tree of depth $N$, we look at the properties of the tree.\n1. In a complete $K$-ary tree, the number of leaf nodes (terminal nodes) is $K^N$.\n2. The total number of nodes $T$ is given by the geometric series $\\sum_{i=0}^{N} K^i = \\frac{K^{N+1}-1}{K-1}$.\n3. The number of nonterminal nodes is $T - K^N = \\frac{K^{N+1}-1}{K-1} - K^N = \\frac{K^{N+1}-1 - K^{N+1} + K^N}{K-1} = \\frac{K^N-1}{K-1}$.\n4. The ratio of nonterminal nodes to total nodes is $\\frac{(K^N-1)/(K-1)}{(K^{N+1}-1)/(K-1)} = \\frac{K^N-1}{K^{N+1}-1}$. As $N \\to \\infty$, this ratio simplifies to $\\frac{K^N}{K^{N+1}} = \\frac{1}{K}$.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
