    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9450",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a CSMA/CD network with a data rate of 10 Mbps, the minimum frame size is found to be 512 bits for the correct operation of the collision detection process. What should be the minimum frame size (in bits) if we increase the data rate to 1 Gbps?\n\nOptions: \nA. 25600\nB. 10000\nC. 102400\nD. 32000\nE. 51200\nF. 6400\nG. 204800\nH. 256000\nI. 409600\nJ. 12800\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To ensure the correct operation of the collision detection process in CSMA/CD, the transmission time of the minimum frame size must be at least twice the maximum propagation delay ($T_{trans} \\ge 2 \\cdot T_{prop}$). \n\nSince the propagation delay depends on the physical medium and the distance between nodes (which remain constant here), the transmission time must remain the same when the data rate increases. \n\nThe formula for transmission time is $\\text{Frame Size} / \\text{Data Rate}$. Therefore:\n$\\frac{\\text{Old Size}}{\\text{Old Rate}} = \\frac{\\text{New Size}}{\\text{New Rate}} \\implies \\frac{512 \\text{ bits}}{10 \\text{ Mbps}} = \\frac{\\text{New Size}}{1000 \\text{ Mbps}}$\n$\\text{New Size} = 512 \\times \\frac{1000}{10} = 512 \\times 100 = 51,200 \\text{ bits}$.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
