  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9343",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When doing least-squares regression with regularisation (assuming that the optimisation can be done exactly), increasing the value of the regularisation parameter λ the testing error.\n\nOptions: \nA. will not have any effect on the testing error.\nB. will always increase the testing error.\nC. may decrease or increase the testing error.\nD. will never increase the training error.\nE. will always decrease the testing error.\nF. may decrease or increase the training error.\nG. will not have any effect on the training error.\nH. will never decrease the testing error.\nI. will never decrease the training error.\nJ. will never increase\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In least-squares regression with regularization (such as Ridge or Lasso), the regularization parameter $\\lambda$ controls the trade-off between fitting the training data (bias) and keeping the model weights small to prevent overfitting (variance). \n\n1. If $\\lambda$ is too small, the model may overfit the training data, leading to high testing error due to high variance. In this case, increasing $\\lambda$ can **decrease** the testing error.\n2. If $\\lambda$ is too large, the model may underfit the training data, leading to high testing error due to high bias. In this case, further increasing $\\lambda$ will **increase** the testing error.\n\nTherefore, the testing error typically follows a U-shaped curve relative to $\\lambda$, meaning increasing $\\lambda$ may either decrease or increase the testing error depending on the starting point.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
