  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9139",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: You are training a linear regression model for a simple estimation task, and notice that the model is overfitting to the data. You decide to add in $\\ell_2$ regularization to penalize the weights. As you increase the $\\ell_2$ regularization coefficient, what will happen to the bias and variance of the model?\n\nOptions: \nA. Bias increase ; Variance increase\nB. Bias decrease ; Variance increase\nC. Neither bias nor variance is affected by $\\ell_2$ regularization\nD. Variance remains constant ; Bias decreases\nE. Bias increase ; Variance decrease\nF. Bias remains constant ; Variance increases\nG. Both bias and variance remain constant\nH. Variance remains constant ; Bias increases\nI. Bias decrease ; Variance decrease\nJ. Bias remains constant ; Variance decreases\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In machine learning, $\\ell_2$ regularization (Ridge regression) is used to prevent overfitting by penalizing large weights, which simplifies the model. Increasing the regularization coefficient increases the **bias** (as the model becomes less flexible and makes stronger assumptions about the weights being small) and decreases the **variance** (as the model becomes less sensitive to specific fluctuations in the training data). This is a classic example of the bias-variance tradeoff.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 233,
        "output_tokens": 96,
        "total_tokens": 329
