    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9347",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let HH be a Merkle-Damgard hash function is H:X^{{\\leq L}} \\to TH:X^≤L  →T. Construct a MAC from this HH as follows: \\ \\ S(k,m) = H(k \\| m)  S(k,m)=H(k∥m). This mac is insecure because:\n\nOptions: \nA. Given H(k \\| m)H(k∥m) anyone can compute H(w \\| k \\| m \\| \\text{{PB}})H(w∥k∥m∥PB) for any ww\nB. Given H(k \\| m)H(k∥m) anyone can compute H(k \\| m \\| w)H(k∥m∥w) for any ww\nC. Given H(k \\| m)H(k∥m) anyone can compute H(k \\| m \\| \\text{{PB}} \\| w)H(k∥m∥PB∥w) for any ww\nD. Anyone can compute H( k \\| m )H(k∥m) for any mm\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The construction $S(k, m) = H(k \\| m)$ is vulnerable to a **length-extension attack**, which is a characteristic weakness of Merkle-Damgard hash functions. If an attacker knows the hash of a message $H(k \\| m)$ and the length of $(k \\| m)$, they can use the hash output as the internal state to continue hashing additional data $w$. To do this correctly, the attacker must include the original padding (PB) used by the hash function to complete the block.\n\nTherefore, given $H(k \\| m)$, an attacker can compute $H(k \\| m \\| \\text{PB} \\| w)$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
