  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9176",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose that feedback is used on a binary symmetric channel with parameter $p=0.5$. Each time a $Y$ is received, it becomes the next transmission. Thus $X_1$ is Bern(1/2), $X_2=Y_1$, $X_3=Y_2$, \\ldots, X_n=Y_{{n-1}}. Find $\\lim_{{n\\to\\infty}} \\frac{{1}}{{n}} I(X_n;Y_n)$ in bits.\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.25\nB. 1.0\nC. 2.0\nD. 1.5\nE. 0.5\nF. 0.75\nG. 0.9\nH. 0.0\nI. 0.25\nJ. 0.1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve for $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{1}{n} I(X_n; Y_n)$, we first analyze the mutual information $I(X_n; Y_n)$.\n\nFor any $n$, $X_n$ and $Y_n$ are binary random variables. The mutual information is given by:\n$I(X_n; Y_n) = H(Y_n) - H(Y_n | X_n)$.\nSince the channel is a Binary Symmetric Channel (BSC) with parameter $p=0.5$, the conditional entropy $H(Y_n | X_n) = H(p) = H(0.5) = 1$ bit.\nThe entropy $H(Y_n)$ is maximized at 1 bit (since $Y_n$ is binary).\nThus, $I(X_n; Y_n) = H(Y_n) - 1$. Since $H(Y_n) \\le 1$, $I(X_n; Y_n) \\le 0$. Because mutual information is non-negative, $I(X_n; Y_n) = 0$.\n\nMathematically, if $p=0.5$, $Y_n$ is independent of $X_n$ regardless of the input distribution, meaning $I(X_n; Y_n) = 0$ for all $n$. Therefore:\n$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{1}{n} I(X_n; Y_n) = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{0}{n} = 0$.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
      "success": true,
