    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9321",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A cascade of $n$ identical independent binary symmetric channels each with raw error probability $p$, and $0<p<1$. What is the capacity of the cascade when $n$ goes to infinity?\n\nOptions: \nA. $n$\nB. 0.0\nC. 1.0\nD. $p$\nE. $\\frac{{1}}{{2}}$\nF. $n(1-p)$\nG. $2p$\nH. $1-p$\nI. $p^n$\nJ. $\\sqrt{{p}}$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the capacity of a cascade of $n$ identical binary symmetric channels (BSCs), we first determine the overall error probability $p_n$. For a single BSC with error probability $p$, the transition probability after $n$ stages is given by the formula $p_n = \\frac{1}{2} [1 - (1 - 2p)^n]$.\n\nAs $n \\to \\infty$, since $0 < p < 1$, the term $(1 - 2p)^n$ approaches $0$ (unless $p=0$ or $p=1$, which are excluded). Therefore, $p_n \\to \\frac{1}{2}$. The capacity of a BSC is defined as $C = 1 - H(p_n)$, where $H(p_n)$ is the binary entropy function. Since $H(1/2) = 1$, the capacity $C = 1 - 1 = 0$.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
