    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose Unix did not provide a way of passing file descriptors between processes, but still allowed inheriting file descriptors from a parent on fork and exec. What aspects of the OKWS design would break without file descriptor passing?\n1. It would be impossible for services to get a TCP connection to the client web browser.\n2. It would be impossible for okd to run as a non-root user.\n\nOptions: \nA. True, False\nB. Partially false, True\nC. Partially false, False\nD. True, True\nE. Partially true, True\nF. True, Partially true\nG. Partially true, False\nH. False, True\nI. False, Partially true\nJ. False, False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In the OKWS (OK Web Server) architecture, the **okd** process (the dispatcher) receives incoming TCP connections and passes the corresponding file descriptors to the appropriate service processes via Unix domain sockets.\n\n1. **Statement 1 is False:** While the design would need to change, it would not be *impossible* for services to get connections. A service could potentially be launched as a child process (using fork/exec inheritance) for each request, or a different proxying mechanism could be used, though it would be significantly less efficient.\n2. **Statement 2 is False:** The ability to pass file descriptors is independent of the user ID under which **okd** runs. **okd** typically runs as root initially to bind to port 80, but its ability to hand off descriptors is a functional design choice, not a requirement for dropping privileges.\n\nBoth statements are false in the context of absolute impossibility.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
