    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9362",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An integer c is a common divisor of two integers x and y if and only if c is a divisor of x and c is a divisor of y. Which of the following sets of integers could possibly be the set of all common divisors of two integers?\n\nOptions: \nA. {{-10, -5, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 5, 10}}\nB. {{-6, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 6}}\nC. {{-6, -3, -2, -1, 1, 2, 3, 6}}\nD. {{-6, -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 6}}\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the set of all common divisors of two integers $x$ and $y$, we must identify the divisors of their greatest common divisor (GCD). \n\n1.  **Divisibility by Zero**: By definition, 0 is not a divisor of any non-zero integer because division by zero is undefined. Therefore, any set containing 0 (Options A, B, and D) cannot be a set of divisors.\n2.  **Symmetry of Divisors**: If $c$ is a divisor of an integer, $-c$ must also be a divisor. Option C satisfies this property.\n3.  **Completeness of Divisors**: In Option C, the set $\\{-6, -3, -2, -1, 1, 2, 3, 6\\}$ contains all the divisors of 6 (and -6). If $\\text{gcd}(x, y) = 6$, the set of all common divisors is exactly this set.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
