    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9089",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let a undirected graph G with edges E = {{<0,2>, <2,4>, <3,4>, <1,4>}}, which <A,B> represent Node A is connected to Node B. What is the minimum vertex cover of G if 0 is one of vertex cover? Represent the vertex cover in a list of ascending order.\n\nOptions: \nA. [0, 1, 2]\nB. [0, 2, 4]\nC. [0, 1, 4]\nD. [0, 4]\nE. [0, 3, 4]\nF. [0, 1, 3]\nG. [0, 1]\nH. [0, 2, 3]\nI. [0, 2]\nJ. [0, 3]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the minimum vertex cover of the graph $G$, we first list all the edges: $\\{0,2\\}, \\{2,4\\}, \\{3,4\\}, \\{1,4\\}$. A vertex cover is a set of vertices such that every edge is incident to at least one vertex in the set.\n\n1.  The edge $\\{0,2\\}$ must be covered. Since we are told $0$ is in the vertex cover, this edge is already covered.\n2.  The remaining edges are $\\{2,4\\}, \\{3,4\\}, \\{1,4\\}$.\n3.  All three remaining edges share the common vertex $4$. By selecting vertex $4$, we cover all these remaining edges.\n4.  The resulting set $\\{0, 4\\}$ covers all edges in the graph. Any set with only two vertices including $0$ that doesn't include $4$ would fail to cover at least two of the other edges (e.g., if we chose $\\{0, 2\\}$, the edges $\\{3,4\\}$ and $\\{1,4\\}$ would remain uncovered).\n\nThe minimum vertex cover containing $0$ is therefore $[0, 4]$.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
