  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9371",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| The kernel density estimator is equivalent to performing kernel regression with the value Yi = 1/n at each point Xi in the original data set. Statement 2| The depth of a learned decision tree can be larger than the number of training examples used to create the tree.\n\nOptions: \nA. Both Statements are context-dependent\nB. True, True\nC. Statement 1 is True only for non-parametric Kernels, Statement 2 is False\nD. Both Statements are partially True\nE. False, False\nF. Statement 1 is True only for Gaussian Kernels, Statement 2 is False\nG. Statement 1 is False, Statement 2 is True only for Binary Decision Trees\nH. False, True\nI. True, False\nJ. Statement 1 is False, Statement 2 is True only for Multiclass Decision Trees\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To evaluate the statements:\n\n1.  **Statement 1 is True.** The Kernel Density Estimator (KDE) is defined as $\\hat{f}(x) = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} K(x - X_i)$. In kernel regression (Nadaraya-Watson), the estimate is $\\hat{m}(x) = \\frac{\\sum K(x - X_i) Y_i}{\\sum K(x - X_i)}$. If we set $Y_i = 1/n$ and assume a normalized kernel where the denominator $\\sum K(x - X_i)$ equals $n$ (or specifically, if the regression is performed without the denominator normalization, which is the basis for the equivalence of the sum), the KDE is essentially a kernel regression where each point is weighted equally. More formally, KDE is the kernel regression of a constant $1/n$ over the sample.\n2.  **Statement 2 is False.** In a standard decision tree, each split partitions the data. The maximum depth of a tree is reached when each leaf contains at least one unique training example (or when no further splits are possible). Consequently, the maximum depth cannot exceed the number of training examples $n$ (in fact, it is typically at most $n$ for a binary tree, often $n-1$ or $\\log n$ depending on the structure, but it can never be *larger* than the number of examples).\n\nTherefore, Statement 1 is True and Statement 2 is False.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 254,
        "output_tokens": 334,
        "total_tokens": 588
      },
      "provider": "openai",
