    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9396",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Of the following, which gives the best upper bound for the value of f(N) where f is a solution to the recurrence f(2N + 1) = f(2N) = f(N) + log N for N >= 1, with f(1) = 0?\n\nOptions: \nA. O(N log(log N))\nB. O(N^2)\nC. O(N)\nD. O(log N)\nE. O((log N)^2)\nF. O(N log N)\nG. O(2^N)\nH. O(1)\nI. O(log N) + 0(1)\nJ. O(N^2 log N)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve the recurrence $f(2N + 1) = f(2N) = f(N) + \\log N$, we can focus on the growth of the function for powers of 2. Let $N = 2^k$; then $f(2^k) = f(2^{k-1}) + \\log(2^{k-1})$.\nExpanding this summation: $f(2^k) = \\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \\log(2^i) = \\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} i \\log 2 = \\log 2 \\cdot \\frac{(k-1)k}{2}$.\nSince $k = \\log_2 N$, we have $f(N) \\approx \\frac{\\log 2}{2} (\\log_2 N)^2$, which is $O((\\log N)^2)$.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
