      "generated_answer": "The \"Magic memory\" operations allow for atomic state changes and feedback: a `Read` operation returns a unique, incrementing value (the count since the last `Clear`), which can be used to implement a Test-and-Set equivalent (e.g., if `Read` returns 0, the process \"wins\" the lock; otherwise, it fails). Because it can implement synchronization primitives, it can coordinate processes on a shared-memory multiprocessor, but it is also useful for process coordination on single-processor systems. Therefore, statements I and II are true, while III is false.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
