  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9455",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the maximum number of entries that can be stored in a binary tree if the longest path from the root to any node does not exceed N? Find the general equation for the aver-age search time for any entry in a tree with N levels.\n\nOptions: \nA. 2^(2N) - 1, (2N - 1) / (2^(2N) - 1)\nB. N^3, (log(N) + 1) / N\nC. 2^(N-1), (2N) / (2^(N-1))\nD. 2^(N+1) - 1, N / (2^(N+1) - 1)\nE. 2^N, N / (2^N)\nF. 2^N + 1, N / (2^N - 1) - 1\nG. 3^N - 1, log(N) / (3^N - 1)\nH. N!, (N^2) / (N!)\nI. 2^N - 1, N - 1 + (N / (2^N - 1))\nJ. N^2 - 1, N + 1 - (N / (2^N - 1))\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the maximum number of entries in a binary tree where the longest path from the root to any node does not exceed $N$, we identify the height of the tree as $N$. For a full binary tree with $N$ levels (where the root is at level 1), the total number of nodes is given by the geometric series $\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1} 2^i = 2^N - 1$.\n\nThe average search time (average number of comparisons) for a successful search in a full binary tree is calculated by summing the levels of all nodes and dividing by the total number of nodes:\nTotal internal path length $= \\sum_{i=1}^{N} i \\cdot 2^{i-1} = (N-1)2^N + 1$.\nAverage search time $= \\frac{(N-1)2^N + 1}{2^N - 1} = \\frac{N \\cdot 2^N - 2^N + 1}{2^N - 1} = \\frac{N(2^N - 1) + N - 2^N + 1}{2^N - 1} = N - 1 + \\frac{N}{2^N - 1}$.\n\nComparing these results to the options, Option I matches both the maximum number of entries ($2^N - 1$) and the general equation for average search time.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
