    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9230",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Any set of Boolean operators that is sufficient to represent all Boolean expressions is said to be complete. Which of the following is NOT complete?\n\nOptions: \nA. {{NAND}}\nB. {{NOT, NAND}}\nC. {{AND, OR}}\nD. {{NOT, OR}}\nE. {{XOR, NOT}}\nF. {{AND, NOT}}\nG. {{OR, NOR}}\nH. {{NOR}}\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine which set is NOT complete, we look for the set that cannot express all possible Boolean functions (specifically, those that cannot represent one of the basic operators like NOT, AND, or OR).\n\n1.  **NAND** and **NOR** are \"universal\" gates, meaning they can implement any Boolean function on their own (Options A, H).\n2.  Sets containing **{NOT, AND}**, **{NOT, OR}**, or **{NOT, XOR}** (with AND/OR) are functionally complete (Options B, D, E, F).\n3.  **{AND, OR}** is not complete because it cannot represent the **NOT** operation (inversion); any combination of AND and OR applied to inputs $x, y$ will always return 1 if all inputs are 1, and 0 if all inputs are 0, making it impossible to invert a signal.\n4.  **{OR, NOR}** is complete because NOR is universal.\n\nThe set {{AND, OR}} lacks the ability to perform negation.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
