    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9335",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In systems with support for automatic memory management, a garbage collector typically has the responsibility for reclaiming allocated memory objects whose contents cannot affect any future legal computation. Such objects are identified by determining that they cannot be reached from a root set. Which of the following is NOT part of the root set in a typical garbage collector?\n\nOptions: \nA. Newly created objects awaiting assignment\nB. Return addresses on the call stack\nC. Objects referenced by CPU instructions\nD. Temporary variables in the active procedures\nE. Global variables of the program\nF. Local variables on the call stack\nG. Dynamically allocated objects on the heap\nH. Static variables in the program\nI. Actual parameters of the active procedures\nJ. Objects stored in registers\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In garbage collection, the \"root set\" consists of references that are directly accessible to the program without following other object references, such as variables on the stack, registers, and global/static variables. **Dynamically allocated objects on the heap** are the targets of garbage collection and are reached *from* the root set, rather than being part of the root set themselves. Therefore, they are not considered roots unless they are specifically pointed to by one of the other categories.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
