    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9441",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| For any two variables x and y having joint distribution p(x, y), we always have H[x, y] ≥ H[x] + H[y] where H is entropy function. Statement 2| For some directed graphs, moralization decreases the number of edges present in the graph.\n\nOptions: \nA. True, False\nB. False, Depends on the graph\nC. Not enough information, False\nD. False, Not enough information\nE. Not enough information, True\nF. False, False\nG. False, True\nH. True, True\nI. True, Not enough information\nJ. Not enough information, Not enough information\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Statement 1** is false. The joint entropy $H(X, Y)$ is always less than or equal to the sum of the marginal entropies $H(X) + H(Y)$, with equality holding if and only if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\n**Statement 2** is false. Moralization involves adding \"moral\" edges between parents of a common child and then removing the directionality of existing edges; it never removes edges, so the number of edges can only increase or stay the same.\n\nBoth statements are false.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
