    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9265",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let T (n) be defined by T(1) = 7 and T(n + 1) = 3n + T(n) for all integers n ≥ 1. Which of the following represents the order of growth of T(n) as a function of n?\n\nOptions: \nA. Θ(n^3 log n)\nB. Θ(n log n^2)\nC. Θ(sqrt(n))\nD. Θ(n log n)\nE. Θ(log n)\nF. Θ(n^3)\nG. Θ(n)\nH. Θ(n^2 sqrt(n))\nI. Θ(n^2 log n)\nJ. Θ(n^2)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the order of growth of $T(n)$, we can express the recurrence relation as a summation.\nGiven $T(n+1) = 3n + T(n)$, we can write:\n$T(n) = T(1) + \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} 3i$\n$T(n) = 7 + 3 \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} i$\n$T(n) = 7 + 3 \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}$\n$T(n) = \\frac{3}{2}n^2 - \\frac{3}{2}n + 7$\n\nThe leading term of this quadratic polynomial is $\\frac{3}{2}n^2$, which means the order of growth is $\\Theta(n^2)$.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
