      "generated_answer": "To determine which statements must be true for a finite directed acyclic graph (DAG) with $|E| > 0$:\n\n1.  **Statement I**: In any finite DAG, there must be at least one source (a vertex with no incoming edges). If every vertex had an incoming edge, you could trace backwards infinitely, which in a finite graph would eventually create a cycle, contradicting the \"acyclic\" property.\n2.  **Statement II**: Similarly, there must be at least one sink (a vertex with no outgoing edges). If every vertex had an outgoing edge, you could trace forwards infinitely, eventually creating a cycle.\n3.  **Statement III**: A DAG does not need to have an isolated vertex. For example, a graph with two vertices $u, v$ and one edge $(u, v)$ is a DAG, but neither vertex is isolated.\n\nThus, statements I and II must be true, while III is not necessarily true.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
