      "generated_answer": "To evaluate the statements:\n1. **Ambiguity**: A grammar is ambiguous if a single string can have more than one leftmost derivation or parse tree. For this grammar, any string (e.g., `((x))`) has exactly one unique derivation; thus, it is **not ambiguous**.\n2. **Top-down parsing**: The grammar does not have left recursion, and the starting terminals for the productions of $S$ are distinct ( '(' vs 'x'). Therefore, it is LL(1) and **suitable for top-down parsing**.\n3. **Bottom-up parsing**: The grammar is simple, non-ambiguous, and fits the requirements for LR parsing (it is an LR(0) grammar). Therefore, it is **suitable for bottom-up parsing**.\n\nSince statements II and III are true and statement I is false, the correct option is G.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
