  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9167",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| Since the VC dimension for an SVM with a Radial Base Kernel is infinite, such an SVM must be worse than an SVM with polynomial kernel which has a finite VC dimension. Statement 2| A two layer neural network with linear activation functions is essentially a weighted combination of linear separators, trained on a given dataset; the boosting algorithm built on linear separators also finds a combination of linear separators, therefore these two algorithms will give the same result.\n\nOptions: \nA. False, False\nB. False, True\nC. Neither True nor False, False\nD. Neither True nor False, True\nE. True, True\nF. False, Neither True nor False\nG. Neither True nor False, Neither True nor False\nH. True, False\nI. True, Neither True nor False\nJ. True, False and False, True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Statement 1 is False.** While the VC dimension of an RBF kernel SVM is infinite (meaning it can shatter any number of points), this does not imply it is \"worse\" than a polynomial kernel. In practice, regularization (the margin) controls overfitting, and RBF kernels often provide superior generalization performance on complex datasets.\n\n**Statement 2 is False.** A two-layer neural network with linear activations collapses into a single linear transformation ($W_2 W_1 x$), resulting in a single linear decision boundary. Boosting, however, creates a weighted sum of *non-linear* step functions (the sign of the linear separators), which can represent a complex non-linear decision boundary.\n\nThe correct letter choice is **A**.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
