    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "According to Joseph Butler’s moral philosophy, particularly in his *Sermons*, the conscience acts as a supreme authoritative faculty that judges our actions. He argues that while one may act against their own interest or passion, it is psychologically and morally impossible to perform an action that one does not, in some sense, approve of through the lens of their own judgment at the moment of action. Therefore, the most fitting interpretation of his view on the internal authority of conscience is that one cannot act against their own moral approval.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
