    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In his work *On Liberty*, John Stuart Mill argues that to silence an opinion is to assume one’s own infallibility, as the censored speech might actually be true. He contends that even if an opinion is partially true, its suppression deprives humanity of the opportunity to exchange error for truth. Therefore, the most accurate representation of his specific argument regarding the act of censoring possibly true speech is that it assumes the censor is incapable of being wrong.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
