    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the immediate consequence, we look for a rule of inference that can be applied to the given premises:\n1. $(N \\supset \\sim O) \\supset (O \\lor Q)$\n2. $P \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$\n3. $N \\supset P$\n\nBy applying the rule of **Hypothetical Syllogism** to premises 3 and 2:\nFrom $N \\supset P$ and $P \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$, we can immediately derive $N \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$. However, looking at the options, we check for a derivation involving premise 1.\nBy applying **Hypothetical Syllogism** to premise 2 and premise 1:\nFrom $P \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$ and $(N \\supset \\sim O) \\supset (O \\lor Q)$, we derive $P \\supset (O \\lor Q)$.\n\nWait, looking at the options again, let's check if any other immediate step exists. If we combine $N \\supset P$ and $P \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$, we get $N \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$, which simplifies to $N \\supset \\sim O$ (via contraction). If we then use $N \\supset \\sim O$ as a premise with premise 1 via **Modus Ponens**, we get $O \\lor Q$. However, a \"one-step\" consequence typically refers to a single application of a rule.\nApplying Hypothetical Syllogism to (2) and (1) yields $P \\supset (O \\lor Q)$, which is not listed.\nApplying Hypothetical Syllogism to (3) and (2) yields $N \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$.\nIf the question considers the chain $N \\to P \\to (N \\supset \\sim O) \\to (O \\lor Q)$, the most direct derived conclusion appearing in the options is $O \\lor Q$, though it requires multiple steps unless treated as a chain. Let's re-evaluate: $N \\supset P$ and $P \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$ implies $N \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$. Since $(N \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)) \\equiv (N \\supset \\sim O)$, and $(N \\supset \\sim O)$ triggers premise 1, we get $O \\lor Q$. Among the choices, B is the most logical result of the premise set.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
