    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **C**.\n\nShrinkage in multiple correlation occurs when a model that fits the initial sample well performs poorly on new data, which typically happens when the regression weights are \"overfitted\" to the specific noise and random fluctuations of the original sample. This capitalization on chance is most common when the sample size is small relative to the number of predictors. Therefore, the loss of predictive power during cross-validation indicates that the initial weights were not representative of the true population parameters.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
