    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2512",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Consider the distribution of the previous problem. Let us add 2 relatively high values in order to observe the differential effecton the three measures of central tendency. Use the followingeight ordered values: 3, 5, 6, 6, 7, 9, 16, 20.\n\nOptions: \nA. Mean is unaffected, median is somewhat affected, mode is most affected\nB. Median is most affected, mean is somewhat affected, mode is unaffected\nC. All measures are unaffected\nD. Mean and median are equally affected, mode is most affected\nE. All measures are equally affected\nF. Mean is most affected, median is somewhat affected, mode is unaffected\nG. Median is unaffected, mean and mode are equally affected\nH. Mode and median are equally affected, mean is unaffected\nI. Mode is unaffected, mean is somewhat affected, median is most affected\nJ. Mode is most affected, median is somewhat affected, mean is unaffected\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the effect of adding high values (outliers) to a dataset, we examine how the three measures of central tendency respond:\n1. **Mean**: The mean is calculated by summing all values; therefore, it is highly sensitive to extreme values and will increase significantly.\n2. **Median**: The median is the middle value of an ordered set; adding high values shifts the middle position slightly, but the value itself changes much less than the mean.\n3. **Mode**: The mode is the most frequent value; since the new values are high and distinct, the most frequent value (6) remains unchanged.\n\nComparing these, the mean is most affected, the median is somewhat affected, and the mode is unaffected.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
