      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **C**.\n\nWaiving co-payments for insured clients is generally considered insurance fraud because the provider is misrepresenting the actual cost of the service to the insurance company. By not collecting the co-payment, the provider is essentially asking the insurance company to pay more than the agreed-upon contracted rate for the service. Most insurance contracts explicitly prohibit the waiving of cost-sharing requirements.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
