    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_108",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nH ≡ (I · ~J)\nK ≡ ~H\n~(K ⊃ J) / I ≡ J\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when I, H, and K are true and J is false\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when I and H are true and J and K are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when K is true and I, H, and J are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine if the argument is valid using an indirect truth table, we assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false.\n\n1.  **Conclusion False:** $I \\equiv J$ is false, meaning one is true and the other is false ($I=T, J=F$ or $I=F, J=T$).\n2.  **Premise 3 True:** $\\sim(K \\supset J)$ is true, which means $K \\supset J$ is false. This only occurs when **$K = T$** and **$J = F$**.\n3.  **Premise 2 True:** $K \\equiv \\sim H$ is true. Since $K = T$, then $\\sim H = T$, so **$H = F$**.\n4.  **Premise 1 True:** $H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J)$ is true. Substituting our known values: $F \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim F) \\implies F \\equiv (I \\cdot T)$. For this to be true, $(I \\cdot T)$ must be false, which means **$I = F$**.\n\nChecking these assignments ($I=F, J=F, K=T, H=F$):\n- $H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J) \\implies F \\equiv (F \\cdot T) \\implies F \\equiv F$ (True)\n- $K \\equiv \\sim H \\implies T \\equiv \\sim F \\implies T \\equiv T$ (True)\n- $\\sim(K \\supset J) \\implies \\sim(T \\supset F) \\implies \\sim(F) \\implies T$ (True)\n- $I \\equiv J \\implies F \\equiv F$ (True)\n\nWait, if the conclusion is True, this specific assignment is not a counterexample. Let's re-evaluate the conclusion $I \\equiv J$. If $I=F$ and $J=F$, the conclusion is True. For the conclusion to be False, $I$ and $J$ must have different values. If $J=F$ (from premise 3), then $I$ must be $T$.\nTesting $I=T, J=F, K=T, H=F$:\n- Premise 1: $F \\equiv (T \\cdot T) \\implies F \\equiv T$ (False).\nTesting $I=T, J=F, K=F, H=T$:\n- Premise 2: $F \\equiv \\sim T \\implies F \\equiv F$ (True)\n- Premise 3: $\\sim(F \\supset F) \\implies \\sim(T) \\implies F$ (False).\n\nReviewing the options provided:\nOption C: $I=T, H=T, J=F, K=F$.\n- $H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J) \\implies T \\equiv (T \\cdot T) \\implies T \\equiv T$ (True)\n- $K \\equiv \\sim H \\implies F \\equiv \\sim T \\implies F \\equiv F$ (True)\n- $\\sim(K \\supset J) \\implies \\sim(F \\supset F) \\implies \\sim T \\implies F$ (Invalid)\n\nChecking Option B: $I=T, H=T, K=T, J=F$.\n- $K \\equiv \\sim H \\implies T \\equiv \\sim T \\implies T \\equiv F$ (Invalid)\n\nChecking Option D: $K=T, I=F, H=F, J=F$.\n- Conclusion $I \\equiv J \\implies F \\equiv F$ (True). Not a counterexample.\n\nRe-evaluating the premises: If $\\sim(K \\supset J)$ is true, $K=T$ and $J=F$. If $K \\equiv \\sim H$ is true, $H=F$. If $H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J)$ is true, then $F \\equiv (I \\cdot T)$, so $I=F$. In this case, $I \\equiv J$ is $F \\equiv F$, which is True. The argument is valid.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
