    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_3",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Construct a complete truth table for the following argument. Then, using the truth table, determine whether the argument is valid or invalid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\n~G ⊃ H\n~H / G\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when G and H are true\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when G is true and H is false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when H is true and G is false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the validity of the argument, we examine the truth table for the premises ($\\sim G \\supset H$ and $\\sim H$) and the conclusion ($G$).\n\n1. If $G$ is true and $H$ is true: $\\sim G$ is false, so $\\sim G \\supset H$ is true; $\\sim H$ is false. (Not a counterexample).\n2. If $G$ is true and $H$ is false: $\\sim G$ is false, so $\\sim G \\supset H$ is true; $\\sim H$ is true. Here, both premises are true, but the conclusion $G$ is true. (Not a counterexample).\n3. If $G$ is false and $H$ is true: $\\sim G$ is true, so $\\sim G \\supset H$ is true; $\\sim H$ is false. (Not a counterexample).\n4. If $G$ is false and $H$ is false: $\\sim G$ is true, so $\\sim G \\supset H$ is false. (Not a counterexample).\n\nWait, let's re-evaluate the logic:\nPremise 1: $\\sim G \\supset H$ (Equivalent to $G \\lor H$)\nPremise 2: $\\sim H$\nConclusion: $G$\nBy Disjunctive Syllogism, if $G \\lor H$ is true and $H$ is false, $G$ must be true. Therefore, there is no case where the premises are true and the conclusion is false.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
