    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine consistency, we test if there is a valuation where all three propositions are true.\n\n1.  **Analyze Option B:** If $T$ is false, then $T \\supset \\sim(U \\supset X)$ is true (false antecedent). If $T$ is false, then $(T \\supset \\sim X) \\supset W$ becomes $(\\text{True}) \\supset W$; since $W$ is true, this is true. For the first proposition $(T \\supset U) \\supset (W \\supset U)$, if $T$ is false, the antecedent $(T \\supset U)$ is true, and the consequent $(W \\supset U)$ is true (since $U$ is true), making the whole proposition true.\n2.  **Analyze Option C:** If $T$ is false, the second proposition is true. For the third, $(\\text{True}) \\supset W$ is true since $W$ is true. For the first, $(\\text{True}) \\supset (W \\supset U)$ becomes $\\text{True} \\supset (\\text{True} \\supset \\text{False})$, which is $\\text{True} \\supset \\text{False}$, resulting in False.\n3.  **Analyze Option D:** If $T$ and $U$ are true, the first proposition $(T \\supset U) \\supset (W \\supset U)$ becomes $\\text{True} \\supset \\text{True}$, which is true. The second $T \\supset \\sim(U \\supset X)$ becomes $\\text{True} \\supset \\sim(\\text{True} \\supset \\text{False})$, which is $\\text{True} \\supset \\text{True}$, which is true. The third $(T \\supset \\sim X) \\supset W$ becomes $(\\text{True} \\supset \\text{True}) \\supset \\text{False}$, which is $\\text{True} \\supset \\text{False}$, resulting in False.\n\nValuation B makes all three propositions true.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
