    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_42",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: How much will it cost to cover a floor 13'6\" × 18'8\" with asphalt tile if the price is 9 (3/4) \\textcent per 9\" tile?\n\nOptions: \nA. $42.50\nB. $49.99\nC. $46.25\nD. $40.75\nE. $47.50\nF. $48.95\nG. $43.88\nH. $51.20\nI. $45.00\nJ. $44.00\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the cost, first calculate the total area of the floor in square inches.\n1. **Floor Area**: $13'6\" = 162\"$ and $18'8\" = 224\"$. Total area = $162 \\times 224 = 36,288$ square inches.\n2. **Tile Area**: A $9\"$ tile is $9 \\times 9 = 81$ square inches.\n3. **Number of Tiles**: $36,288 \\div 81 = 448$ tiles.\n4. **Total Cost**: $448 \\text{ tiles} \\times 9.75\\text{ cents/tile} = 4,368$ cents, which is $\\$43.68$.\n\nComparing this result to the provided options, Option G ($43.88) is the closest, though a precise calculation yields $\\$43.68$. However, if the tiles are laid without cutting and rounded up to fit the dimensions ($18 \\text{ tiles} \\times 25 \\text{ tiles} = 450 \\text{ tiles}$), the cost is $450 \\times 0.0975 = \\$43.875$, which rounds to $\\$43.88$.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
