    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_141",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Assume the Black-Scholes framework. For $t \\ge 0$, let $S(t)$ be the time-$t$ price of a nondividend-paying stock. You are given:\n(i) $S(0)=0.5\n(ii) The stock price process is $\\frac{{dS(t)}}{{S(t)}} = 0.05dt+0.2dZ(t)$ where $Z(t)$ is a standart Brownian motion.\n(iii) $E[S(1)^\\alpha]=1.4$, where $\\alpha$ is a negative constant.\n(iv) The continuously compounded risk-free interest rate is $3%$.\nConsider a contingent claim that pays $S(1)^\\alpha$ at time 1. What is the time-0 price of the contigent claim?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.450\nB. 1.475\nC. 1.415\nD. 1.250\nE. 1.360\nF. 1.390\nG. 1.325\nH. 1.500\nI. 1.287\nJ. 1.372\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the time-0 price of the contingent claim, we use the risk-neutral pricing formula: $V(0) = e^{-rT} E^{\\mathbb{Q}}[S(T)^\\alpha]$.\n\nUnder the risk-neutral measure $\\mathbb{Q}$, the stock price process is $dS(t) = r S(t) dt + \\sigma S(t) dZ^{\\mathbb{Q}}(t)$, so $S(1) = S(0) \\exp((r - \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2) + \\sigma Z^{\\mathbb{Q}}(1))$.\nSince $S(1)$ is log-normally distributed, $E[S(1)^\\alpha] = S(0)^\\alpha \\exp(\\alpha( \\mu - \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2) + \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha^2\\sigma^2)$ under the physical measure $\\mathbb{P}$ (where $\\mu = 0.05$) and $E^{\\mathbb{Q}}[S(1)^\\alpha] = S(0)^\\alpha \\exp(\\alpha( r - \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2) + \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha^2\\sigma^2)$ under the risk-neutral measure $\\mathbb{Q}$ (where $r = 0.03$).\n\nThe ratio is:\n$E^{\\mathbb{Q}}[S(1)^\\alpha] = E^{\\mathbb{P}}[S(1)^\\alpha] \\cdot e^{\\alpha(r - \\mu)} = 1.4 \\cdot e^{\\alpha(0.03 - 0.05)} = 1.4 \\cdot e^{-0.02\\alpha}$.\nTo find $\\alpha$, we use $E[S(1)^\\alpha] = 0.5^\\alpha \\exp(\\alpha(0.05 - 0.02) + 0.5\\alpha^2(0.04)) = 1.4$.\nSolving $0.5^\\alpha \\exp(0.03\\alpha + 0.02\\alpha^2) = 1.4$ for negative $\\alpha$:\n$\\alpha \\ln(0.5) + 0.03\\alpha + 0.02\\alpha^2 = \\ln(1.4) \\Rightarrow -0.6931\\alpha + 0.03\\alpha + 0.02\\alpha^2 = 0.3365 \\Rightarrow 0.02\\alpha^2 - 0.6631\\alpha - 0.3365 = 0$.\nUsing the quadratic formula: $\\alpha = \\frac{0.6631 \\pm \\sqrt{0.6631^2 - 4(0.02)(-0.3365)}}{0.04} \\approx \\frac{0.6631 \\pm 0.6831}{0.04}$.\nSince $\\alpha < 0$, $\\alpha \\approx \\frac{-0.02}{0.04} = -0.5$.\nThen $E^{\\mathbb{Q}}[S(1)^\\alpha] = 1.4 \\cdot e^{-0.02(-0.5)} = 1.4 \\cdot e^{0.01} \\approx 1.4 \\cdot 1.01005 = 1.41407$.\nThe price is $V(0) = e^{-0.03(1)} \\cdot 1.41407 \\approx 0.9704 \\cdot 1.41407 \\approx 1.372$.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
      "success": true,
