    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_761",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: On January 17, the shirt buyer for Wellington's Department Store decided to determine his open-to-buy. The following figures were available: Present inventory at retail (Jan 17) Inventory commitments (Jan 17) Planned end-of-month inventory (Jan 31) Planned sales Actual sales Planned markdowns Actual markdowns $12,000 3,000 15,000 6,000 3,000 500 200 What is the buyer's open-to-buy?\n\nOptions: \nA. $18,300\nB. $12,200\nC. $5,000\nD. $10,800\nE. $3,300\nF. $2,800\nG. $15,000\nH. $6,000\nI. $7,700\nJ. $9,500\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To calculate the Open-to-Buy (OTB), we use the formula:\n**OTB = (Planned Sales + Planned Markdowns + Planned End-of-Month Inventory) - (Present Inventory + Inventory Commitments + Actual Markdowns)**\n\nWait, the standard retail OTB formula typically adjusts for the difference between planned and actuals. Let's calculate the total needs versus total available:\nTotal Needs = Planned Sales ($6,000) + Planned Markdowns ($500) + Planned End-of-Month Inventory ($15,000) = $21,500.\nTotal Available = Present Inventory ($12,000) + Inventory Commitments ($3,000) + Actual Markdowns (Note: markdowns reduce inventory value, so we subtract them from the inventory side or add to the needs side).\n\nCorrect Formula: **OTB = (Planned Sales + Planned Markdowns + Planned End-of-Month Inventory) - (Present Inventory + Inventory Commitments - Actual Markdowns)**\nActually, the most common simplified version is:\nOTB = (Planned Sales + Planned End-of-Month Inventory + Planned Markdowns) - (On-hand Inventory + Open-to-receive Inventory + Actual Markdowns).\nWait, markdowns reduce the value of the inventory on hand.\nOTB = ($6,000 + $15,000 + $500) - ($12,000 + $3,000 - $200) = $21,500 - $14,800 = $6,700 (Not an option).\n\nLet's try treating Actual Markdowns as a reduction of inventory value:\nOTB = (Planned Sales + Planned Markdowns + Planned End-of-Month Inventory) - (Present Inventory + Inventory Commitments) + Actual Markdowns\nOTB = ($6,000 + $500 + $15,000) - ($12,000 + $3,000) + $200 = $21,500 - $15,000 + $200 = $6,700.\n\nLet's re-read the values: Actual sales are not provided in the list of numbers, but \"Actual sales\" is listed as a label. The numbers are: 12k, 3k, 15k, 6k, 3k, 500, 200.\nPresent: 12,000; Commitments: 3,000; End-of-month: 15,000; Planned Sales: 6,000; Actual Sales: 3,000; Planned MD: 500; Actual MD: 200.\n\nOTB = (Planned Sales + Planned MD + End Inv) - (Present Inv + Commitments - Actual Sales - Actual MD)\nOTB = ($6,000 + $500 + $15,000) - ($12,000 + $3,000 - $3,000 - $200)\nOTB = $21,500 - $11,800 = $9,700 (Close to J).\n\nLet's try: OTB = (Planned Sales + Planned MD + End Inv) - (Current Inv + Commitments) + Actual Sales + Actual MD\nOTB = ($6,000 + $500 + $15,000) - ($12,000 + $3,000) + $3,000 + $200 = $21,500 - $15,000 + $3,200 = $9,700.\n\nWait, let's try: OTB = (Planned Sales - Actual Sales) + (Planned MD - Actual MD) + (End Inv - (Present Inv + Commitments))\nOTB = (6,000 - 3,000) + (500 - 200) + (15,000 - 15,000) = 3,000 + 300 + 0 = 3,300.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
