    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_199",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Consider an arbitrage-free securities market model, in which the risk-free interest rate is constant. There are two nondividend-paying stocks whose price processes are:\n$S_1(t)=S_1(0)e^{{0.1t+0.2Z(t)}}$\n$S_2(t)=S_2(0)e^{{0.125t+0.3Z(t)}}$\nwhere $Z(t)$ is a standard Brownian motion ant $t\\ge0$. What is the continuously compounded risk-free interest rate?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.08\nB. 0.025\nC. 0.02\nD. 0.09\nE. 0.01\nF. 0.03\nG. 0.06\nH. 0.05\nI. 0.07\nJ. 0.04\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the risk-free interest rate $r$, we use the condition that in an arbitrage-free market, the discounted stock prices $e^{-rt}S_i(t)$ must be martingales under the risk-neutral measure $\\mathbb{Q}$.\n\nFor a stock price process of the form $S(t) = S(0)e^{(\\mu - \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2)t + \\sigma Z(t)}$, the drift under the physical measure is $\\mu$. In the risk-neutral measure, the drift must be $r$. The given processes are:\n$S_1(t) = S_1(0)e^{(0.1)t + 0.2Z(t)} \\implies \\mu_1 - \\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^2 = 0.1 \\implies \\mu_1 = 0.1 + 0.02 = 0.12$\n$S_2(t) = S_2(0)e^{(0.125)t + 0.3Z(t)} \\implies \\mu_2 - \\frac{1}{2}(0.3)^2 = 0.125 \\implies \\mu_2 = 0.125 + 0.045 = 0.17$\n\nUsing the Market Price of Risk $\\lambda$, we have $\\mu_i = r + \\sigma_i \\lambda$:\n1) $0.12 = r + 0.2\\lambda$\n2) $0.17 = r + 0.3\\lambda$\n\nSubtracting equation (1) from (2): $0.05 = 0.1\\lambda \\implies \\lambda = 0.5$.\nSubstituting $\\lambda$ back into (1): $0.12 = r + 0.2(0.5) \\implies 0.12 = r + 0.1 \\implies r = 0.02$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 282,
