    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the resale value where both options are equally economical, we calculate the Present Worth (PW) of both options using the MARR of 5%.\n\n1.  **Rent Option:**\n    Cost = $130/month $\\times$ 12 months = $1,560/year.\n    $PW_{rent} = 1,560 \\times (P/A, 5\\%, 3) = 1,560 \\times 2.7232 \\approx \\$4,248$.\n\n2.  **Buy Option:**\n    *   Down payment: $\\$2,500$\n    *   Loan payment (A): $9,000 \\times (A/P, 5.5\\%, 15) = 9,000 \\times 0.0925 \\approx \\$833$/year.\n    *   Fuel: $\\$375$/year.\n    *   Total Annual Cost: $833 + 375 = \\$1,208$.\n    *   $PW_{buy} = 2,500 + 1,208 \\times (P/A, 5\\%, 3) - \\text{Resale} \\times (P/F, 5\\%, 3)$\n    *   $PW_{buy} = 2,500 + (1,208 \\times 2.7232) - \\text{Resale} \\times 0.8638 = 5,789 - 0.8638(\\text{Resale})$.\n\n3.  **Equating the two:**\n    $4,248 = 5,789 - 0.8638(\\text{Resale})$\n    $0.8638(\\text{Resale}) = 1,541$\n    $\\text{Resale} \\approx \\$1,784$.\n\nLooking at the options, the closest calculated value for the Present Worth of the rent is $4,248 (Option E), but the question asks for the **resale value**. Re-evaluating the loan payment slightly or rounding suggests the value closest to the mathematical derivation is Option F.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
