    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6319",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following estimation techniques are available for the estimation of over-identified systems of simultaneous equations?\n\ni) OLS\n\nii) ILS\n\niii) 2SLS\n\niv) IV\n\nOptions: \nA. (iii) only\nB. (i), (iii) and (iv) only\nC. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\nD. (i), (ii) and (iii) only\nE. (i) and (iii) only\nF. (ii) and (iv) only\nG. (i) and (ii) only\nH. (i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)\nI. (i), (ii) and (iv) only\nJ. (iii) and (iv) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To estimate over-identified systems of simultaneous equations, techniques must be able to handle the endogeneity of regressors and the presence of more instrumental variables than there are endogenous variables. \n\n1. **OLS (Ordinary Least Squares)** is generally inconsistent for simultaneous equations due to simultaneity bias.\n2. **ILS (Indirect Least Squares)** is used for exactly identified systems but is not suitable for over-identified systems.\n3. **2SLS (Two-Stage Least Squares)** is the standard method for estimating over-identified systems.\n4. **IV (Instrumental Variables)** is the broader framework that allows for estimation when instruments are available; specifically, when the system is over-identified, a generalized IV method (like 2SLS) is employed.\n\nTherefore, (iii) and (iv) are the appropriate techniques for over-identified systems.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
