    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5871",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For an autoregressive process to be considered stationary\n\nOptions: \nA. The roots of the characteristic equation must all be greater than one in absolute value\nB. The roots of the characteristic equation must all be equal to one\nC. The roots of the characteristic equation must all be imaginary numbers\nD. The roots of the characteristic equation must all be negative\nE. The roots of the characteristic equation must all be less than one in absolute value\nF. The roots of the characteristic equation must all lie outside the unit circle\nG. The roots of the characteristic equation must all lie inside the unit circle\nH. The roots of the characteristic equation must all be positive\nI. The roots of the characteristic equation must all lie on the unit circle\nJ. The roots of the characteristic equation must all be real numbers\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In time series analysis, an autoregressive (AR) process is stationary if the roots of its characteristic equation lie outside the unit circle in the complex plane. This ensures that the coefficients of the lagged terms sum to less than one in absolute value, preventing the process from diverging. While some texts refer to the \"inverse roots\" (which must be inside the unit circle), the standard definition for the roots of the characteristic equation itself is that they must be greater than one in absolute value.\n\nAmong the options provided, both A and F describe the same mathematical condition. However, in formal econometric terminology regarding the characteristic equation $\\phi(z) = 0$, the standard phrasing is that the roots must lie outside the unit circle.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
