  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5659",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following features of financial asset return time-series could be captured using a standard GARCH(1,1) model?\n\ni) Fat tails in the return distribution\n\n\nii) Leverage effects\n\n\niii) Volatility clustering\n\n\niv) Volatility affecting returns\n\nOptions: \nA. (ii) and (iv) only\nB. (iii) and (iv) only\nC. (i) and (iii) only\nD. (i), (ii), and (iv) only\nE. (ii) and (iii) only\nF. (i) and (iv) only\nG. (i), (ii), and (iii) only\nH. (i), (ii), (iii), and (iv)\nI. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\nJ. (i), (iii), and (iv) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct answer, we evaluate each feature:\n\ni) **Fat tails**: GARCH models generate returns with higher kurtosis than a normal distribution (even with Gaussian innovations), capturing the \"fat tails\" characteristic of financial data.\nii) **Leverage effects**: A standard GARCH(1,1) model is symmetric; it cannot capture the asymmetry where negative shocks increase volatility more than positive shocks (this requires models like EGARCH or GJR-GARCH).\niii) **Volatility clustering**: This is the primary purpose of GARCH; it models the tendency for high-volatility periods to be followed by high-volatility periods.\niv) **Volatility affecting returns**: GARCH models volatility as a latent process that affects the variance of returns, but the model does not imply that volatility itself causes the direction of returns (e.g., the GARCH-in-Mean model is required for that).\n\nTherefore, only features (i) and (iii) are captured by a standard GARCH(1,1) model.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
