    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5668",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If OLS is applied separately to each equation that is part of a simultaneous system, the resulting estimates will be\n\nOptions: \nA. Biased, inconsistent, and inefficient.\nB. Biased but consistent\nC. Unbiased and consistent\nD. Biased but inconsistent for one equation only\nE. Unbiased and efficient\nF. Unbiased but inconsistent\nG. It is impossible to apply OLS to equations that are part of a simultaneous system\nH. Consistent but inefficient\nI. It is possible to apply OLS to equations that are part of a simultaneous system, but the results will be unpredictable\nJ. Biased and inconsistent\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In a simultaneous equation system, the dependent variable in one equation often appears as an explanatory variable in another. This creates **simultaneity bias**, where the explanatory variables are correlated with the error term (endogeneity). Consequently, OLS estimates are both biased and inconsistent because the assumption of exogeneity is violated.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
      "success": true,
