    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6117",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Consider the following sample autocorrelation estimates obtained using 250 data points:\n\n1) Lag 1 2 3\n\n2) Coefficient 0.2 -0.15 -0.1\n\n3) Assuming that the coefficients are approximately normally distributed, which of the coefficients are statistically significant at the 5% level?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1, 2 and 3 only\nB. All the coefficients are statistically significant\nC. None of them are statistically significant\nD. 1 and 3 only\nE. 1 only\nF. 3 only\nG. It is not possible to determine the statistical significance since no standard errors have been given\nH. 2 only\nI. 1 and 2 only\nJ. 2 and 3 only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine if the sample autocorrelation coefficients ($\\hat{\\rho}_k$) are statistically significant, we use the standard error formula for a large sample size $n$: $SE = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}$.\n\n1.  Calculate the standard error: $SE = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{250}} \\approx 0.0632$.\n2.  Calculate the critical value for a 5% significance level (two-tailed): $1.96 \\times 0.0632 \\approx 0.1239$.\n3.  Compare the coefficients to the critical value:\n    *   Lag 1: $|0.2| > 0.1239$ (Significant)\n    *   Lag 2: $|-0.15| > 0.1239$ (Significant)\n    *   Lag 3: $|-0.1| < 0.1239$ (Not significant)\n\nTherefore, only the coefficients for Lag 1 and Lag 2 are statistically significant.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
