  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5930",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following are plausible approaches to dealing with residual autocorrelation?\n\ni) Take logarithms of each of the variables\n\nii) Add lagged values of the variables to the regression equation\n\niii) Use dummy variables to remove outlying observations\n\niv) Try a model in first differenced form rather than in levels.\n\nOptions: \nA. (ii) and (iv) only\nB. (iii) and (iv) only\nC. (i), (ii), (iii), and (iv)\nD. (i) and (iii) only\nE. (i) and (iv) only\nF. (i), (ii), and (iii) only\nG. (i) and (ii) only\nH. (i), (ii), and (iv) only\nI. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\nJ. (i), (iii), and (iv) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To address residual autocorrelation (often found in time-series data), the goal is to account for the temporal dependence of the error terms.\n\n- **(ii) Add lagged values of the variables:** This is a standard approach (creating a distributed lag model) to capture the persistence of the effect over time, which often removes the autocorrelation in the residuals.\n- **(iv) First differencing:** Transforming the data into first differences ($\\Delta Y_t = Y_t - Y_{t-1}$) is a primary method for removing autocorrelation, especially when the data is non-stationary (stochastic trend).\n- **(i) Logarithms:** This is used to handle non-linearity or heteroscedasticity, not autocorrelation.\n- **(iii) Dummy variables for outliers:** While this handles specific shocks or structural breaks, it is not a general approach to fixing systematic autocorrelation.\n\nTherefore, only (ii) and (iv) are plausible approaches for dealing with residual autocorrelation.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 264,
        "output_tokens": 210,
        "total_tokens": 474
      },
