    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6058",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following statements are true concerning the Box-Jenkins approach to diagnostic testing for ARMA models?\n\n(i) The tests will show whether the identified model is either too large or too small\n\n(ii) The tests involve checking the model residuals for autocorrelation, heteroscedasticity, and non-normality\n\n(iii) If the model suggested at the identification stage is appropriate, the acf and pacf for the residuals should show no additional structure\n\n(iv) If the model suggested at the identification stage is appropriate, the coefficients on the additional variables under the overfitting approach will be statistically insignificant\n\nOptions: \nA. (iii) and (iv) only\nB. (i) and (iii) only\nC. (i), (iii), and (iv) only\nD. (i), (ii), and (iii) only\nE. (ii) and (iii) only\nF. (i) and (iv) only\nG. (i), (ii), (iii), and (iv)\nH. (ii) and (iv) only\nI. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\nJ. (i) and (ii) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is based on the standard procedures of the Box-Jenkins methodology for time series analysis.\n\n*   **(i) is true:** Diagnostic testing (specifically overfitting) helps determine if a model is underspecified (too small) or overspecified (too large).\n*   **(iii) is true:** A well-specified model should leave residuals that are white noise, meaning their ACF and PACF should show no significant structure.\n*   **(iv) is true:** In the overfitting stage, if the original model is correct, any additional parameters added to the model should be statistically insignificant.\n*   **(ii) is generally considered false in the strict context of the original Box-Jenkins diagnostic stage:** While modern econometrics checks for all three, the classical Box-Jenkins approach focuses primarily on residual autocorrelation (white noise properties) rather than heteroscedasticity or normality.\n\nTherefore, the most accurate combination reflecting the core Box-Jenkins diagnostic principles is (i), (iii), and (iv).\n\n\\boxed{C}",
