  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5739",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following statements are true concerning the standardised residuals (residuals divided by their respective conditional standard deviations) from an estimated GARCH model?\n\ni) They are assumed to be normally distributed\n\n\nii) Their squares will be related to their lagged squared values if the GARCH model is\n\nappropriate\n\n\niii) In practice, they are likely to have fat tails\n\n\niv) If the GARCH model is adequate, the standardised residuals and the raw residuals\n\nwill be identical\n\nOptions: \nA. (i), (ii), and (iii) only\nB. (i), (ii), (iii), and (iv)\nC. (iii) and (iv) only\nD. (i) and (iv) only\nE. (i) and (iii) only\nF. (i), (iii), and (iv) only\nG. (ii) and (iv) only\nH. (i) and (ii) only\nI. (ii) and (iii) only\nJ. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To evaluate the statements concerning standardised residuals in a GARCH model:\n\ni) **True**: In the basic specification of a GARCH model, the innovations (standardised residuals) are typically assumed to be independently and identically distributed (i.i.d.) normal, although other distributions (like Student's t) can be used.\nii) **False**: If the GARCH model is appropriate, the conditional variance has been successfully captured; therefore, the standardised residuals should be i.i.d., and their squares should **not** be related to their lagged values (no remaining ARCH effects).\niii) **True**: In practice, financial time series often exhibit \"excess kurtosis\" that a standard GARCH model with normal innovations cannot fully remove, leaving the standardised residuals with fat tails.\niv) **False**: Standardised residuals are raw residuals divided by the conditional standard deviation ($\\epsilon_t / \\sigma_t$); they are only identical if the conditional variance is constant and equal to 1.\n\nThus, statements (i) and (iii) are true.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
