      "generated_answer": "In a \"lien theory\" state, a mortgage is viewed as a security interest (a lien) rather than a transfer of title. Under the majority view, the creation of a mortgage lien by one joint tenant does not sever the joint tenancy or destroy the right of survivorship. Therefore, when the mortgaging tenant dies, their interest passes automatically to the surviving joint tenant, and the lien is extinguished along with the deceased tenant's interest.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
