    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **C**.\n\nThe revival of natural law in the 20th century was largely a reaction to the atrocities committed during World War II, which demonstrated the dangers of legal positivism (the idea that \"law is law\" regardless of morality). The subsequent international recognition of human rights and the principles established during the Nuremberg Trials necessitated a return to universal moral standards inherent in natural law. This shift aimed to ensure that state-sanctioned laws could be judged against a higher moral framework to prevent future human rights abuses.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
