    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question refers to a common critique of H.L.A. Hart's legal theory regarding the nature of the Rule of Recognition. While secondary rules generally confer power (e.g., the power to make law), the Rule of Recognition is often viewed as imposing a duty on officials (particularly judges) to identify valid laws, rather than simply granting them a power. Therefore, the tension lies in the fact that it appears to function as both a power-conferring rule and a duty-imposing rule.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
