      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is based on legal and historical scholarship regarding Sir Henry Maine’s work *Ancient Law*, which notes that his observation was a historical generalization rather than a teleological prophecy.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\nMaine intended the aphorism to describe a historical trend observed in the development of Roman and Western law up to his time, but it is frequently misunderstood as a universal prediction or an inevitable law of social evolution. Critics often point to modern developments, such as the rise of collective bargaining or standardized contracts, as \"refutations\" of a prediction he never actually made.",
