      "generated_answer": "The central legal issue is whether Congress can enact a federal law that targets only one state. Under the principle of \"equal footing\" and the prohibition against laws that discriminate against a single state (which would violate the spirit of the Constitution and the principles of federalism), Congress generally cannot pass legislation that applies to only one state to restrict its sovereign powers, such as taxing, unless the law is based on a specific constitutional authorization or is non-discriminatory in its application. Since the statute specifically forbids the tax in a way that affects only one state, it is likely unconstitutional.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
