    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Friedrich Carl von Savigny’s concept of the *Volksgeist* (spirit of the people) posits that law evolves organically from the history, culture, and language of a society, rather than being imposed from above. Option A is the least likely to follow because it represents the \"Legal Positivism\" view (associated with thinkers like Austin or Bentham), which defines law as a top-down command of a sovereign, directly opposing Savigny's view of law as a bottom-up cultural growth. While options C and F are also external to his theory, they represent specific sociological/Marxist critiques, whereas Option A is the fundamental theoretical antithesis to the Historical School of Jurisprudence.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
