    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1468",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A shrimp fishery is located in the coastal waters of a state. A large part of the catch is either frozen or canned, and distributed nationwide. The state legislature passed a statute requiring payment of a license fee of $25 for each shrimp boat owned by a resident and $2,500 for each boat owned by a nonresident. A resident of a neighboring state was a commercial shrimp fisherman who was denied a license after refusing to pay the $2,500 fee. The resident brought suit in federal court challenging the constitutionality of the state shrimp boat licensing statute. The federal court should\n\nOptions: \nA. hear the case on its merits.\nB. abstain from jurisdiction because it involves the operations of a commercial business.\nC. dismiss the case because the resident is not a citizen of the state implementing the statute.\nD. dismiss the suit because it involves a question of state law.\nE. hear the case but uphold the statute as it is within the state's rights to regulate its resources.\nF. hear the case because it involves a potential violation of the Equal Protection Clause.\nG. dismiss the suit because the resident lacks standing.\nH. dismiss the suit because the resident has not exhausted all state-level legal remedies.\nI. abstain from jurisdiction because the constitutional issue should be litigated first in a state court.\nJ. dismiss the suit because the federal court lacks jurisdiction over state legislature decisions.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The issue at hand is whether a state may charge significantly higher licensing fees to nonresidents than to residents for a commercial activity. This creates a potential violation of the **Privileges and Immunities Clause** of Article IV (which prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states regarding fundamental rights, including the right to pursue a livelihood) and the **Equal Protection Clause** of the Fourteenth Amendment. Because the plaintiff is a citizen of another state challenging a state statute in federal court on constitutional grounds, the federal court has jurisdiction to hear the case.\n\nWhile Option F mentions the Equal Protection Clause, Option A is the most comprehensive answer in the context of a procedural question regarding whether the court \"should\" hear the case (i.e., does it have jurisdiction and a reason to proceed). The court will not abstain or dismiss based on the reasons provided in the other options, as constitutional challenges to state laws are quintessential federal court matters.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
