    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1449",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which one of the following statements is false?\n\nOptions: \nA. Protocol 14 changed the committee that hears admissibility cases from a three judge to a one judge formation\nB. Protocol 14 added a new criterion for admissibility\nC. Protocol 14 abolished the Commission of Human Rights\nD. Protocol 14 extended the judicial term of office for the European Court of Human Rights\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Protocol 14 to the European Convention on Human Rights was designed to improve the efficiency of the Court. While it abolished the European Commission of Human Rights (which had actually happened earlier under Protocol 11) and changed judge formations, it specifically aimed to *reduce* the workload by simplifying procedures. Option B is false because Protocol 14 actually introduced a more restrictive admissibility criterion (the \"significant disadvantage\" rule) to filter out repetitive cases, but the statement's phrasing often appears in legal examinations to test the specific structural changes; however, strictly speaking, Protocol 14 did not \"add a new criterion\" in a way that expanded access, but rather refined it. More importantly, Option C is technically redundant as the Commission was abolished by Protocol 11 (1998), not Protocol 14 (2004).\n\n\\boxed{C}",
