  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1462",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: On September 15, a card shop sent the following fax to a printing company: \"Please deliver 100 dozen assorted Christmas cards, delivery by November 1. \"On October 10, the printing company shipped 100 dozen assorted Thanksgiving cards to the card shop, which were received on October 12. The following day, the card shop sent the following fax to the printing company: \"Be advised that your shipment is rejected. Order stipulated Christmas cards, not Thanksgiving cards. \"On October 15, the printing company sent the following fax to the card shop: \"Will ship 100 dozen assorted Christmas cards by November 1. Please ship the Thanksgiving cards back to our warehouse and bill us for the shipping charges. \" This fax was received by the card shop, but the card shop did not respond to it or ship the Thanksgiving cards back to the printing company. On October 25, the printing company attempted to deliver 100 dozen assorted Christmas cards to the card shop, but the latter refused to accept. Did the card shop properly reject the October 25 delivery?\n\nOptions: \nA. No, because the card shop did not explicitly agree to the modified offer from the printing company.\nB. No, because the printing company cured the October 10 defective shipment by its tender of conforming goods on October 25.\nC. Yes, because the printing company's shipping of the Thanksgiving cards on October 10 constituted a present breach of contract.\nD. Yes, because the card shop was not obligated to accept the second shipment of Christmas cards.\nE. No, because under the UCC a contract for the sale of goods can be modified without consideration.\nF. Yes, because the printing company did not deliver the correct order initially as requested by the card shop.\nG. Yes, because the card shop had already rejected the initial shipment of Thanksgiving cards.\nH. Yes, because the printing company's shipping of the Thanksgiving cards on October 10 constituted an anticipatory breach.\nI. No, because the card shop did not return the Thanksgiving cards as requested by the printing company.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The scenario is governed by the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under UCC § 2-508, a seller who fails to make a perfect tender has a right to \"cure\" the defective delivery if the time for performance has not yet expired. Since the original delivery date was November 1, the printing company's shipment of conforming Christmas cards on October 25 was a valid cure. Therefore, the card shop was obligated to accept the conforming goods.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
