      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is based on the legal principle that while the First Amendment protects the free exercise of religion, the government may enforce neutral, generally applicable laws or regulations that do not target specific religions, even if they incidentally burden religious practice (as established in *Employment Division v. Smith*). In this scenario, the regulation requiring standard headgear applies to all rangers for the purpose of morale, efficiency, and professionalism, rather than targeting the employee's specific faith. Because the regulation is neutral and generally applicable, it is likely constitutional.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
