      "generated_answer": "For Émile Durkheim, punishment serves primarily to reaffirm the collective conscience and reinforce the shared values of society rather than to simply deter or rehabilitate the individual. By publicly reacting to a crime, society expresses its moral outrage, thereby strengthening social solidarity and educating the public on the boundaries of acceptable behavior. Therefore, punishment functions as a form of moral ritual that maintains the integrity of the social order.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
