    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the three-digit integer $N = \\underline{a}\\,\\underline{b}\\,\\underline{c}$, we express both sides of the given condition in base ten:\n$100a + 10b + c = 81b + 9c + a$.\n\nRearranging the terms to group the variables, we get:\n$99a - 71b - 8c = 0 \\implies 99a = 71b + 8c$.\n\nSince $a, b, c$ are digits in base nine, they must be in the set $\\{0, 1, \\dots, 8\\}$, and $a, b \\neq 0$ because they are leading digits. \nTesting values for $b$:\n- If $b=1$, $99a = 71 + 8c$. For $c=0$, $99a=71$ (no); for $c=7$, $99a=71+56=127$ (no).\n- If $b=2$, $99a = 142 + 8c$. For $c=7$, $99a=142+56=198$, which gives $a=2$.\nChecking the digits: $a=2, b=2, c=7$ are all within the range $[0, 8]$.\nThe integer is $100(2) + 10(2) + 7 = 227$.\n\n\\boxed{227}",
