  {
    "global index": "AIME_16",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the remainder when\\[\\binom{{\\binom{{3}}{{2}}{{2}} + \\binom{{\\binom{{4}}{{2}}{{2}} + \\dots +  \\binom{{\\binom{{40}}{{2}}{{2}}\\]is divided by $1000$.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the remainder of $S = \\sum_{n=3}^{40} \\binom{\\binom{n}{2}}{2}$ modulo $1000$, we first simplify the general term:\n$\\binom{\\binom{n}{2}}{2} = \\frac{\\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left( \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} - 1 \\right)}{2} = \\frac{n(n-1)(n^2-n-2)}{8} = \\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)(n+1)}{8} = 3 \\binom{n+1}{4}$.\n\nUsing the Hockey-stick identity, $\\sum_{i=r}^n \\binom{i}{r} = \\binom{n+1}{r+1}$, we calculate the sum:\n$S = 3 \\sum_{n=3}^{40} \\binom{n+1}{4} = 3 \\left( \\binom{4}{4} + \\binom{5}{4} + \\dots + \\binom{41}{4} \\right) = 3 \\binom{42}{5}$.\n\nEvaluating the binomial coefficient:\n$3 \\binom{42}{5} = 3 \\times \\frac{42 \\times 41 \\times 40 \\times 39 \\times 38}{120} = 3 \\times (42 \\times 41 \\times 13 \\times 19) = 3 \\times 425418 = 1276254$.\nDividing by 1000, the remainder is $254$.\n\n\\boxed{254}",
