gency point $P$ is $r = 14 \\sin \\phi \\cdot \\frac{11}{14} = 11 \\sin \\phi$.\nThe generating circle is centered at $(6, y)$, so $6 = 14 \\sin \\phi \\pm 3 \\sin \\psi$.\nThe tangency point is $P = (11 \\sin \\phi, 11 \\cos \\phi)$. The generating circle center is $C = (14 \\sin \\phi, 14 \\cos \\phi)$.\nThe distance from $C$ to the axis is $6$. So $|14 \\sin \\phi| = 6 \\implies \\sin \\phi = \\pm 6/14 = \\pm 3/7$.\nThen $r = 11 \\cdot (3/7) = 33/7$.\nWait, the torus can be tangent in two ways: the sphere is \"outside\" the torus's tube (which is what I just did) or the sphere is \"inside\" the tube. But the problem says \"externally tangent\", meaning the volumes don't overlap.\nThe two cases are: 1) The sphere is in the \"hole\" of the donut. 2) The sphere is \"outside\" the donut.\nCase 2: $r_o = 11 \\cdot (6/14) = 33/7$.\nCase 1: The axis of rotation is the $z$-axis. $C = (6, y)$. $P$ is the point on the sphere. $C$ is the center of the generating circle. $\\vec{CP}$ is the normal. $\\vec{CP}$ must be parallel to $\\vec{OP}$.\n$P = (r, z)$. $C = (6, y)$. $P-C = (r-6, z-y)$.\n$|P|=11$, $|P-C|=3$. $\\vec{P} = \\frac{11}{14}\\vec{C} \\implies r = \\frac{11}{14}(6) = 33/7$.\nWait, there's another way: $\\vec{P} = \\frac{11}{14}\\vec{C}$ is the only way for external tangency.\nBut the generating circle can be on the other side of the axis!\n$C = (-6, y)$. Then $r = |\\frac{11}{14}(-6)| = 33/7$. Same.\nWhat if the axis of the torus is not the $z$-axis? The problem says \"When $T$ rests on $S$\". This means the axis of the torus must be parallel to the $z$-axis for the tangency to be a circle.\nLet the axis be $x=s$. $C = (s+6, y)$. $|C|=14 \\implies (s+6)^2 + y^2 = 196$.\n$P = \\frac{11}{14}C = (\\frac{11}{14}(s+6), \\frac{11}{14}y)$.\n$r = |P_x - s| = |\\frac{11}{14}s + \\frac{66}{14} - s| = |-\\frac{3}{14}s + \\frac{33}{7}|$.\nFor $T$ to rest on $S$, $T$ must be in a stable position. This usually means $s$ is minimized or maximized.\nThe range of $s$ is from $s+6=14 \\implies s=8$ to $s+6=-14 \\implies s=-20$.\nIf $s=8$, $r = |-\\frac{24}{14} + \\frac{66}{14}| = \\frac{42}{14} = 3$.\nIf $s=-20$, $r = |\\frac{60}{14} + \\frac{66}{14}| = \\frac{126}{14} = 9$.\n$r_i = 3, r_o = 9$. Difference $r_o - r_i = 6$. Not a fraction.\nRe-read: \"tangent... along a circle\". This happens only if the axis is the $z$-axis ($s=0$).\nIf $s=0$, $r = 33/7$. Only one value.\nIs there another way? The generating circle center is at distance 6 from the axis.\nLet the axis be the $z$-axis. $C = (6, y)$. $P$ is the point of tangency.\nExternal tangency means the distance between centers is $R+a=14$.\nThe distance from $P$ to the axis is $r$.\n$P = \\frac{11}{14}C = \\frac{11}{14}(6, y)$. $r = \\frac{66}{14} = \\frac{33}{7}$.\nWhat if the torus axis is not the $z$-axis? For the tangency to be a circle, the torus axis must be the $z$-axis.\nIs it possible that \"externally tangent\" means the sphere is inside the torus's hole?\nThen $C = (6, y)$ and $|C| = R-a = 11-3=8$? No, that's internal.\nWait, the generating circle can be centered at $C=(6, y)$ and the distance from the sphere center $(0,0)$ to the generating circle center could be $R+a=14$ or $|R-a|=8$.\nIf $|C|=8$, $P = \\frac{11}{8}C \\implies r = \\frac{11}{8}(6) = \\frac{33}{4}$.\n$r_i = 33/7, r_o = 33/4$. $r_o - r_i = \\frac{33}{4} - \\frac{33}{7} = \\frac{33(3)}{28} = \\frac{99}{28}$.\n$m=99, n=28$. $m+n = 127$.\n\n\\boxed{127}",
