    "global index": "AIME_89",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There is a collection of $25$ indistinguishable white chips and $25$ indistinguishable black chips. Find the number of ways to place some of these chips in the $25$ unit cells of a $5\\times5$ grid such that: \n\neach cell contains at most one chip\nall chips in the same row and all chips in the same column have the same colour\nany additional chip placed on the grid would violate one or more of the previous two conditions.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemma-4-31b-it",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, let the $5 \\times 5$ grid be represented by cells $(i, j)$ where $1 \\le i, j \\le 5$. Let $R$ be the set of rows containing chips and $C$ be the set of columns containing chips. \n\n1.  **Consistency Condition**: If cell $(i, j)$ contains a chip, the entire row $i$ and column $j$ must be restricted to that color. If there are both white and black chips on the board, no row or column can contain both. Thus, the set of rows/columns containing white chips must be disjoint from the set of rows/columns containing black chips. Let $R_W, C_W$ be the rows and columns containing white chips, and $R_B, C_B$ be those containing black chips. Then $R_W \\cap R_B = \\emptyset$ and $C_W \\cap C_B = \\emptyset$.\n2.  **Maximality Condition**: A chip can be placed in cell $(i, j)$ if it doesn't violate the color rules. If $i \\in R_W$ and $j \\in C_W$, a white chip must already be there. If $i \\in R_W$ and $j \\notin C_W \\cup C_B$, a white chip could be placed, violating maximality unless $j \\in C_W$. Thus, for any color, the chips must occupy the full Cartesian product of their active rows and columns (i.e., the chips form rectangles).\n3.  **Saturation**: For the board to be maximal, every row $i$ and column $j$ must either be \"assigned\" a color or be unable to accept any chip. If row $i$ is empty, it can only accept a chip in column $j$ if $j$ is empty. If $R_W, C_W, R_B, C_B$ are the sets defined, then any cell $(i, j)$ must be \"blocked\". A cell $(i, j)$ is blocked if:\n    -   It already contains a chip.\n    -   Row $i$ is occupied by one color and column $j$ is occupied by the other.\n    -   Row $i$ and column $j$ are both \"empty\" but adding a chip would allow further additions (this is handled by the requirement that $R_W \\cup R_B = \\{1, \\dots, 5\\}$ or $C_W \\cup C_B = \\{1, \\dots, 5\\}$).\n    Actually, for no more chips to be added, every row $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, 5\\}$ must belong to $R_W \\cup R_B$ AND every column $j \\in \\{1, \\dots, 5\\}$ must belong to $C_W \\cup C_B$. If some $i \\notin R_W \\cup R_B$ and $j \\notin C_W \\cup C_B$, we could place a chip at $(i, j)$.\n    Thus, $R_W \\cup R_B = \\{1, \\dots, 5\\}$ and $C_W \\cup C_B = \\{1, \\dots, 5\\}$. Since $R_W \\cap R_B = \\emptyset$ and $C_W \\cap C_B = \\emptyset$, let $|R_W| = r$ and $|C_W| = c$. Then $|R_B| = 5-r$ and $|C_B| = 5-c$.\n    The number of ways to choose these sets is $\\binom{5}{r} \\binom{5}{c}$.\n    The case where only one color is used (e.g., $r=5, c=5$) is included. However, if $r=0$ or $r=5$, we must ensure we aren't overcounting.\n    For $1 \\le r \\le 4$ and $1 \\le c \\le 4$, there are $\\binom{5}{r} \\binom{5}{c}$ ways. \n    Summing $\\sum_{r=1}^4 \\sum_{c=1}^4 \\binom{5}{r} \\binom{5}{c} = (2^5-2)(2^5-2) = 30 \\times 30 = 900$.\n    If $r=0$, then $R_B=\\{1..5\\}$, so $C_B$ must be $\\{1..5\\}$ to be maximal (otherwise any $j \\notin C_B$ would allow a black chip in any row since $R_W=\\emptyset$), giving 1 way (all black). Similarly, $r=5$ gives 1 way (all white).\n    Wait, if $r=0$, then $R_W = \\emptyset$. For maximality, any $(i, j)$ must be blocked. Row $i$ is always $R_B$. Column $j$ is either $C_B$ (contains black chip) or $C_W$ (contains white chip). If $C_W$ is non-empty, say $j \\in C_W$, we can't place a black chip there. But we could place a white chip if $i \\in R_W$, which is false. So if $R_W = \\emptyset$, then any $j \\notin C_B$ must be in $C_W$.\n    This means $C_W \\cup C_B = \\{1..5\\}$. If $R_W = \\emptyset$, then $C_W$ can be any subset of $\\{1..5\\}$, and we just place black chips in $\\{1..5\\} \\times C_B$. This is maximal because any $(i, j)$ with $j \\in C_W$ can't take a black chip (since $j \\in C_W$) and can't take a white chip (since $i \\notin R_W$).\n    Thus, for $r=0$, we have $2^5 = 32$ ways. For $r=5$, $2^5 = 32$ ways.\n    Total = $900 + 32 + 32 = 964$. But the cases where both colors are absent or only one exists are handled. Let's re-evaluate:\n    Ways = $\\sum_{r=0}^5 \\binom{5}{r} \\sum_{c=0}^5 \\binom{5}{c} = 32 \\times 32 = 1024$.\n    Subtract the case where no chips are placed: $r=0, c=0 \\implies$ no chips, but we can add any chip. Not maximal.\n    Actually, the conditions $R_W \\cup R_B = \\{1..5\\}$ and $C_W \\cup C_B = \\{1..5\\}$ with the disjointness are the only way to ensure maximality.\n    The number of ways to partition $\\{1..5\\}$ into $R_W, R_B$ is $2^5$ and $\\{1..5\\}$ into $C_W, C_B$ is $2^5$. \n    Total = $32 \\times 32 = 1024$.\n    We must subtract cases where one set is empty and the other is also empty (no chips), but that's already covered. The only invalid case is when no chips are placed at all (which happens if $R_W=C_W=\\emptyset$ and $R_B=C_B=\\emptyset$, but the partitions must cover the whole set, so $R_B=\\{1..5\\}$ and $C_B=\\{1..5\\}$ would be \"all black\").\n    The only case where no chips are placed is if $R_W \\times C_W = \\emptyset$ and $R_B \\times C_B = \\emptyset$.\n    $R_W \\times C_W = \\emptyset \\iff R_W = \\emptyset$ or $C_W = \\emptyset$.\n    Let $S$ be the set of all pairs of partitions. $|S| = 32 \\times 32 = 1024$.\n    A configuration is empty if $(R_W = \\emptyset \\text{ or } C_W = \\emptyset)$ AND $(R_B = \\emptyset \\text{ or } C_B = \\emptyset)$.\n    Since $R_B = R_W^c$ and $C_B = C_W^c$, this is:\n    $(R_W = \\emptyset \\text{ or } C_W = \\emptyset) \\text{ AND } (R_W = \\{1..5\\} \\text{ or } C_W = \\{1..5\\})$.\n    Case 1: $R_W = \\emptyset$. Then we need $R_W = \\{1..5\\}$ (impossible) or $C_W = \\{1..5\\}$. (1 case)\n    Case 2: $C_W = \\emptyset$. Then we need $R_W = \\{1..5\\}$ or $C_W = \\{1..5\\}$ (impossible). (1 case)\n    Wait, $R_W = \\emptyset$ and $C_W = \\{1..5\\}$ means $R_B=\\{1..5\\}$ and $C_B=\\emptyset$. No chips are placed.\n    $R_W = \\{1..5\\}$ and $C_W = \\emptyset$ means $R_B=\\emptyset$ and $C_B=\\{1..5\\}$. No chips are placed.\n    These are the only 2 cases where the board is empty.\n    $1024 - 2 = 1022$.\n\n\\boxed{1022}",
