    "global index": "AIME_79",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let $\\omega\\neq 1$ be a 13th root of unity. Find the remainder when\n\\[\\prod_{{k=0}}^{{12}}(2-2\\omega^k+\\omega^{{2k}})\\]\nis divided by 1000.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the value of $P = \\prod_{k=0}^{12} (2 - 2\\omega^k + \\omega^{2k})$, we factor the quadratic expression $x^2 - 2x + 2$. The roots of $z^2 - 2z + 2 = 0$ are $1 \\pm i$, so we can write $2 - 2\\omega^k + \\omega^{2k} = (\\omega^k - (1+i))(\\omega^k - (1-i))$.\nUsing the identity $\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1} (x - \\omega^k) = x^n - 1$ for $n=13$, we have:\n\\[ P = \\prod_{k=0}^{12} (1+i - \\omega^k) \\prod_{k=0}^{12} (1-i - \\omega^k) = ((1+i)^{13} - 1)((1-i)^{13} - 1) \\]\nSince $1 \\pm i = \\sqrt{2}e^{\\pm i\\pi/4}$, we have $(1 \\pm i)^{13} = (\\sqrt{2})^{13} e^{\\pm i 13\\pi/4} = 2^6 \\sqrt{2} e^{\\pm i (3\\pi + \\pi/4)} = 64\\sqrt{2}(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\mp i\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) = -64 \\mp 64i$.\nSubstituting these values into the product:\n\\[ P = (-64 - 64i - 1)(-64 + 64i - 1) = (-65 - 64i)(-65 + 64i) = 65^2 + 64^2 = 4225 + 4096 = 8321 \\]\nThe remainder when 8321 is divided by 1000 is 321.\n\n\\boxed{321}",
