s wrong.\n$q_n$ is even iff $q_n \\equiv 0 \\pmod 2$.\n$q_n \\equiv 11 \\cdot 12^{n-1} \\pmod{23}$.\nThe sequence $q_n \\pmod 2$ is determined by $q_n \\pmod{2 \\cdot 23}$? No.\n$q_{n+1} = \\frac{q_n + 23(q_n \\pmod 2)}{2}$.\nThis is $q_n = \\frac{q_1 \\cdot 2^{n-1} + 23 \\sum_{i=0}^{n-2} 2^i (q_{n-1-i} \\pmod 2)}{2^{n-1}}$.\n$2^{n-1} q_n = q_1 + 23 \\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} 2^{j-1} (q_j \\pmod 2)$.\n$q_n \\equiv 0 \\pmod 2 \\iff 2^{n-1} q_n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{2^n} \\iff q_1 + 23 \\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} 2^{j-1} (q_j \\pmod 2) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{2^n}$.\nLet $S_n = \\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} (q_j \\pmod 2) 2^{j-1}$. This is the binary representation of an integer.\n$11 + 23 S_n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{2^n} \\implies 23 S_n \\equiv -11 \\equiv 2^n - 11 \\pmod{2^n}$.\n$S_n \\equiv -11 \\cdot 23^{-1} \\pmod{2^n}$.\n$23^{-1} \\pmod{2^n}$ is $x_n$. So $S_n \\equiv -11 x_n \\pmod{2^n}$.\nSince $0 \\le S_n < 2^{n-1}$, $S_n$ is the unique value in that range.\n$q_n$ is even $\\iff S_{n+1} = S_n + 0 \\cdot 2^{n-1} = S_n$.\nThis occurs if the $n$-th bit of $S_\\infty$ is 0.\n$S_\\infty = -11 \\cdot 23^{-1}$ in 2-adic integers $\\mathbb{Z}_2$.\n$23^{-1}$ in $\\mathbb{Z}_2$: $23 \\cdot 1 = 23$. $23 \\cdot (-1) = -23$.\n$23^{-1} = \\frac{1}{23} = \\frac{1}{24-1} = -\\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{24^{k-1}}{24^k}$ ... no.\n$23^{-1}$ in $\\mathbb{Z}_2$: $23x = 1 \\implies (24-1)x = 1 \\implies x = \\frac{1}{23}$.\n$S_\\infty = \\frac{-11}{23}$.\nThe number of $n \\le 1000$ such that the $n$-th bit of $S_\\infty$ is 0 is the number of 0s in the binary expansion of $\\frac{-11}{23} \\pmod{2^{1001}}$.\n$\\frac{-11}{23} = \\frac{-11 + 23k}{23}$. To be a 2-adic integer, it's just a fraction.\nThe binary expansion of $\\frac{a}{b}$ is periodic.\n$\\frac{-11}{23} = \\frac{12}{23}$.\nBinary of $12/23$: $12/23 \\times 2 = 24/23 = 1 + 1/23$. (Bit 1)\n$1/23 \\times 2 = 2/23 = 0 + 2/23$. (Bit 0)\n$2/23 \\times 2 = 4/23 = 0 + 4/23$. (Bit 0)\n$4/23 \\times 2 = 8/23 = 0 + 8/23$. (Bit 0)\n$8/23 \\times 2 = 16/23 = 0 + 16/23$. (Bit 0)\n$