    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the speed of the boat in still water ($x$) and the speed of the stream ($y$), let the speed upstream be $u = x - y$ and the speed downstream be $v = x + y$. Based on the problem, we can set up the following equations for time:\n1) $\\frac{30}{u} + \\frac{44}{v} = 10$\n2) $\\frac{40}{u} + \\frac{55}{v} = 13$\n\nLet $a = \\frac{1}{u}$ and $b = \\frac{1}{v}$. Solving the system $30a + 44b = 10$ and $40a + 55b = 13$ (by multiplying the first by 4 and the second by 3) gives $11b = 1$, so $b = \\frac{1}{11}$ and $a = \\frac{1}{5}$. Thus, $x - y = 5$ and $x + y = 11$.\n\nAdding the two equations gives $2x = 16$, so $x = 8$ km/h, and subtracting them gives $2y = 6$, so $y = 3$ km/h.\n\nSpeed of boat in still water = 8 km/h, Speed of stream = 3 km/h.\n\n\\boxed{8 \\text{ km/h and } 3 \\text{ km/h}}",
