    "global index": "AsDiv_1433",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: A man walks certain distance with certain speed. If he walks 1/2 km/hr faster, he takes 1 hr less. But if he walks 1 km/hr slower, he takes 3 more hours.\n\nQuestion: Find the distance covered by the man and his original rate of walking.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the distance ($d$) and the original speed ($v$), we use the relationship $\\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Speed}}$. Let $t$ be the original time, so $d = vt$.\n\n1.  From the first condition (walking $1/2$ km/hr faster takes 1 hr less):\n    $\\frac{d}{v + 0.5} = t - 1 \\implies d = (v + 0.5)(t - 1) = vt - v + 0.5t - 0.5$.\n    Since $d = vt$, we have: $-v + 0.5t - 0.5 = 0 \\implies t - 2v = 1$.\n\n2.  From the second condition (walking $1$ km/hr slower takes 3 hrs more):\n    $\\frac{d}{v - 1} = t + 3 \\implies d = (v - 1)(t + 3) = vt + 3v - t - 3$.\n    Since $d = vt$, we have: $3v - t - 3 = 0 \\implies 3v - t = 3$.\n\n3.  Solving the system of equations:\n    Adding $(t - 2v = 1)$ and $(3v - t = 3)$ gives $v = 4$ km/hr.\n    Substituting $v = 4$ into $t - 2(4) = 1$ gives $t = 9$ hours.\n    The distance is $d = vt = 4 \\times 9 = 36$ km.\n\nThe distance is 36 km and the original rate is 4 km/hr.\n\n\\boxed{36\\text{ km, } 4\\text{ km/hr}}",
