    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: A railway half ticket costs half the full fare and the reservation charge is the same on half ticket as on full ticket. One reserved first class from Michigan to Massachusetts costs $216 and one full and one half reserved first class tickets cost $327.\n\nQuestion: What is the basic first class full fare and what is the reservation charge?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the basic first class full fare ($x$) and the reservation charge ($y$), we can set up a system of linear equations based on the given information:\n1. For one full reserved ticket: $x + y = 216$\n2. For one full and one half reserved ticket: $(x + y) + (0.5x + y) = 327$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second gives $0.5x + y = 327 - 216 = 111$. Now, subtracting this new equation ($0.5x + y = 111$) from the first equation ($x + y = 216$) yields $0.5x = 105$, which means $x = 210$. Substituting $x = 210$ back into the first equation gives $210 + y = 216$, resulting in $y = 6$.\n\nThe basic first class full fare is $210 and the reservation charge is $6.\n\n\\boxed{210, 6}",
