    "global index": "GeoBench_683",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The workers who survived the Black Plague, which killed nearly a third of European population, ironically saw their wages rise in later years. This phenomenon is best explained by\n\nOptions: \nA. natural resource depletion\nB. national choice theory\nC. Marx's theory of surplus value\nD. Keynesian economics\nE. the iron law of wages\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **E**.\n\nThe **iron law of wages** (associated with David Ricardo) suggests that wages are determined by the supply and demand of labor; when the population decreased drastically due to the plague, the scarcity of labor forced employers to compete by offering higher wages. While the \"iron law\" often focuses on wages falling to subsistence levels during population growth, the inverse relationship explains the post-plague wage spike. Other options like Keynesianism or Marxism are modern economic frameworks that do not directly address this historical supply-side shift.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
      "success": true,
