    "global index": "GeoBench_1172",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following best explains why Ullman and Harris developed the multiple nuclei model?\n\nOptions: \nA. To demonstrate the effects of decreased car ownership\nB. To support the concentric zone model\nC. To describe the complex development of urban areas\nD. To standardize urban studies terminology\nE. To explain the consolidation of cities' central business districts\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **C**.\n\nUllman and Harris developed the multiple nuclei model in 1945 to address the increasing complexity of urban structures that the concentric zone and sector models failed to explain. It reflects the reality that modern cities often develop around several independent nodes or nuclei rather than a single central business district, largely due to increased mobility and specialized land use.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
